is used by  LOCATE  to display the block.      To disable this feature, change  B/H  to 0 and put parens       around the phrase  ' <CREATE> 'CREATE !                                                                                      Note that the 'mm/dd/yy' calendar is used here.  Change            the default setting of the year every January!                                                                               Additional terminal tasks (if any) should be prompted after        the phrases on line 14.                                      Line 15 is the product release date.  Please don't change it.      These constants provide names for the utilities supplied     with FORTH/level 4.  The system "help screen" lists them for    convenience.  The user is encouraged to add the names of        additional utilities and application programs as they are       developed, both here and in the "help screen" in Block 11.                                                                      LOADS   is provided for one-word invocations of an overlay.        (Usage:  141 LOADS APPLICATION  allows  APPLICATION  to         load Block 141)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              This is the system help screen.  Feel free to change it when    you reconfigure the system.  Note that the utilities each have  their own help screens.  You can find them by typing               utility LIST   and looking for the phrase:  n HELPS          The number will be the block number of the help screen.                                                                            You may find it convenient to configure your application as autility, if so, please include a help screen that comes up upon loading, and add to the utility constants on Block 10.                                                                             If your application utility is important, add an entry to    the system help screen in Block 11.                                                                                                Note that in the utilities  HELP  is redefined to display theutility help screen.                                                                                                            RELOAD  jumps to the startup location of the program, which        causes  the system to be re-loaded.                                                                                          The interrupt routines for the clock & function keys are defined   here. They use the FIRQ and work through the monitor's          second level vector structure. Second level vectoring is        through locations  F0 - FF.  By storing FF in location EF2E     the firmware is signalled that any interrupts that are not      from sources with which it is currently concerned are passed    to user interrupt routines whose addresses appear at F0 - FF.The word  VECTOR  is defined as a code ending for an interrupt     routine, which stores the address left by  BEGIN  in the        appropriate second level vector.                                                                                             The sequence on line 7 initializes counter 2 of the PTM for     use by the clock to count 10 ms intervals.                      WITHIN returns true iff  n-1 < h  unsigned.                     U<  compares numbers in the range  0 - 65535.                   IMMEDIATE marks the last word defined as immediate (executed       during compilation); it is useful for adding to the compiler.DEFINITIONS permits words to be defined in vocabularies other      than FORTH .                                                 [COMPILE] compiles an immediate word.  It is useful only           when writing compilers (see the FORTH compiler directives).  .(  types the text up to a ")". Executes immediately.           'HEAD  returns the address of the 1st byte of the word which       follows in the input stream (with  B/H  subtracted).         LOCATE types the source block of the following word.  It relies    upon the version of  CREATE  loaded in Block 9.              FORGET forgets the next word and all words defined more            recently.  It strips the 8 dictionary chains back to before     the word being forgotten.  It won't forget the nucleus.      ~ causes the next word to be compiled with a full length name.  RECOVER  decrements  H  by 2 bytes.  Used after an unneeded  ; .HELPS displays a block of text presumably helpful to the user.     Each application should have one.  See shadows for 10 and 11.SHADOWS is the offset to the shadow blocks (even mult. of 3).   CAST   takes offset & block no. to toggle corresponding drive.  Q displays a block of text associated with each block of code.     Termed shadow blocks, these keep comments out of the way        while permitting their easy retrieval and maintenance.       .S   displays the current stack items non-destructively.        THRU   Loads blocks l thru h. Displays stack before each block. ?CREATE will generate a warning message when words are redefined   It is useful for debugging, although redefined words are not    bad technique.                                                                                                                                                                                  These versions of  2VARIABLE  and  2CONSTANT  are not correctif you plan to target compile this block.  Their colons need to be preceded by  FORTH ; moreover, if the resulting program is   intended to be capable of compiling new words of these types,   you will need to insert a second definition as illustrated in   the Target Compiler appendix.                                      Finally, the HOST version of  2VARIABLE  must be brought intoconsistency with whatever version (RAM or PROM) of  VARIABLE    you are using.                                                     Some examples:                                                     FORTH : 2VARIABLE   CREATE 4 ALLOT ;  ( PROM)                   FORTH : 2VARIABLE   VARIABLE 0 , ;     ( RAM)             The 1st version allocates target RAM, which must be initialized in the power-up sequence.  See definitions of  VARIABLE  in     this system and its documentation for details.                                                                                     These versions of the 32-bit relational, MAX , and MIN words are of the "half-range local" variety.  That is, they           behave as signed operators (  -2,000 is less than -1,000  ) whenthe arguments are within half of the range of the numbers,      or +/- 2,147,483,648.   However,  3,000,000,000 is seen as less than  2,000,000,000  because the double precision integers are  manipulated as 2's complement numbers.                                                                                             Note that the prefix 'D' in general denotes operators whose  arguments are signed, 31-bit integers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             The prefix 'M' denotes mixed-precision operations, some of   whose arguments are 16-bit and some 32-bit.  The exact usage    of these operators has been determined by experience to be      useful in the largest number of cases.  Please check the        stack arguments in the definitions, glossaries, or programmer's card carefully.                                                    The operators in this block are all signed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     M*/  is an mixed precision version of  */ , which allows     multiplication of a signed 31-bit integer by a ratio of 16-bit  numbers, with a 48-bit intermediate product, giving a precise   signed 31-bit result.  The divisor must be unsigned; this       restriction, which is rarely inconvenient in practice, gives    substantial improvement in speed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  The commands in this block are used by the  DISKING          utility and the Data Base Support System.  Otherwise they may   be omitted from Block 9 if you wish to conserve space.          C+!  adds an 8-bit value to a byte whose address is given.      -TEXT  takes the address of two strings and a length and returns   +1 if the 1st string has a higher ASCII value, -1 if it has     a lower value, and 0 (false) if they are equal.              <TEXT  is like  -TEXT , but returns 'true' if the first string     has a lower value, 'false' otherwise.                        KEY  awaits one keystroke and returns the ASCII code typed.     EMIT   outputs a single character.                              TEXT  takes a delimiter and parses the input stream, moving the    string from  >IN  to the delimiter or the end of the string     to  PAD .                                                    MSG  is used to create output ascii strings for serial devices. e.g. MSG CR  2 C, 0D C, 0A C,                                   These editor extensions embellish the resident editor which was    compiled from the load Block 54 during Target Compilation.   ADVANCE   increments the USER variable  SCR  .                  N and  B  move forward and back one block, respectively.        WIPE   erases the current ( SCR ) block to blanks, with 0 in the   first cell.                                                  M  copies the current line under a specified block and line.    K  swaps the insert and find buffers.                           S  searches the blocks from  SCR  to the block whose number is     the argument to  S  for a match on the pattern.                 Usage:  n S pattern                                                                                                          N and B  are made available from  FORTH .                       (COPY)  does not  FLUSH  and is used by  BLOCKS  in Block 36.   COPY  identifies the given destination block no. as  UPDATEd .                                                                    The program as booted is capable of converting 16-bit numbers for input and output.  This block adds 32-bit output.              Note that  #  and  #S  are the only numeric output words     that need to be recoded to handle 32-bit numbers.                                                                               /DIGIT  divides the 32-bit integer on the stack by  BASE ,         leaving the 32-bit quotient and the remainder, a digit to       be put in an output string by  # .                           #  converts the next low-order digit for output, adding it to      the output string.                                           #S  converts all remaining significant digits for output.  Adds    at least one digit (0 if the number is zero) to the string.  (D.)  converts a 32-bit signed integer for output, leaving the     address and length of the string as arguments for  TYPE .    D.  converts and types a 32-bit signed integer.                                                                                    The rules governing 32-bit numeric input are discussed at    length in Starting FORTH and other documentation.                  Note that upon completion of input number conversion the     number of digits to the right of the rightmost punctuation in   a 32-bit number is given in the USER variable  PTR .  PTR  is   negative if there was no punctuation (e.g., a 16-bit result).   The high-order part of an unpunctuated number is contained in   'NUMBER 2+  in case the user wants to check it (it should be    zero or -1 depending on the sign of the result).                *DIGIT  takes a 32-bit integer and the address and length of a     string, and converts one digit from the string adding it to     the low end of the integer.                                  CONVERT  converts a string to binary, leaving the address of the   first un-convertable digit and length of the remainder.      (NUMBER)  converts a string at address  a  to binary, leaving a    32-bit number if it was punctuated, 16-bits otherwise.       ASSIGN  provides a facility for giving behavior to a vectored      execution variable.  Usage is as follows:                                                                                       VARIABLE loc                                                    : mode   loc ASSIGN  { desired behavior } ;                                                                                  Invoking  mode  sets  loc  to the address of the behavior.      The behavior can be performed by:   loc @EXECUTE                                                                                >< swaps the bytes in the top stack item.                       TALLY  adds 1 to the 8-bit number in the address given.         RECURSE  invokes the definition that contains it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               HIS  takes a task address and user variable and returns the        address of that user variable for the task.                     Ex.:  TYPIST CTR HIS  (returns the addr. of  TYPIST's  CTR ) BACKGROUND  sets up the task definition table for a non-terminal   task, given user area size and stack sizes.                  BUILD  takes the address of a table set up by  BACKGROUND  and     builds the task's user area in RAM.  In a target compiled       program this is done in the target system's power-up code.   The version of  BACKGROUND  here is for resident applications      only.  For an application to be target compiled, this must be   preceded by  FORTH  to make it a HOST definition.  BUILD , on   the other hand, must be in the target dictionary.            ACTIVATE  starts a task whose address is given executing the       remainder of the colon definition in which  ACTIVATE  occurs.   Note that this code must end in  STOP ,  QUIT (for a terminal   task) or in an infinite loop.                                TERMINAL  creates a terminal task definition table,                given its device address and partition size.                 CONSTRUCT  takes the address of a task definition table and        constructs the terminal task's user area in RAM.             PROMPT  "awakens" a terminal task by displaying the system help    screen, leaving it awaiting keyboard input.                  SEND  sends the remainder of the input stream (from the terminal   only) to the task whose address is given.  That task will       interpret the string, executing commands found there.                                                                           The version of  TERMINAL  given in this block is for         resident applications only.  For an application which is        to be target compiled, it must be preceded by  FORTH  in        order to make it a  HOST  definition.  CONSTRUCT , on the       other hand, must be in the target dictionary, and is            normally executed as part of the start-up initialization code.     Refer to block 75 and the Processor Supplement for a map     of the  USER  variables initialized here.                          The argument on the stack when this block is entered is the  size of the task's dictionary.                                                                                                     This block is a good example of the use of interpretive      execution to perform non-time critical and infrequently used    procedures.                                                                                                                     ( This is the target compilable version. )                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      This block is not normally loaded.  It is provided as an example   of how a  BACKGROUND  task might be built and controlled.    If you use  BACKGROUND  tasks, they should be defined in this      block and loaded in Block 9.  The words they execute            (equivalent to  SAMPLER  , etc.)  may be part of your           application source elsewhere.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   If you have a parallel printer compatible with the Centronicsinteface conventions, this block creates a task to output       through the parallel port to such a device.                                                                                        It employs a polled scheme for monitoring device busy and    serves as a reasonable example of a device driver implimentationwhich does not use interrupts for service.                                                                                         Two versions of page are provided to support printers        with no "page" function.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        The interrupt handler for the function keys is built here,        contrast it with the clock interrupt on Block 12.                                                                             Function keys are assigned any FORTH word(s) by association     with an index into the function key table.                                                                                      Examples:   2 <F 2 BASE ! F>                                                3 <F HEX F>                                                     4 <F DECIMAL F>                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       This calendar is appropriate for applications in which        dates entered are distributed over many years (birthdays, etc.).The internal form of dates are compatible with the 'month-name' version in the previous block.  This version requires 32-bit    input number conversions, as the dates are originally entered   in the form  mm/dd/yy (a 32-bit number).                           If you convert to this calendar, remember to remove the      default setting of the year in block 9.                                                                                         To set the date:    12/27/82 NOW                                To type the date:   TODAY @ .DATE  (responds) 12/27/82 ok                                                                       Both calenders (blocks 30 and 31) store the date as a 16-bit    count of the days since JAN 1, 1900, which was a Monday.                                                                        DATED  produces a dated help screen (see  SYSTEM ).                These routines provide convenient control and access to      the system clock, which ticks once per 2.5 milliseconds.                                                                        COUNTER (in the program)  and  TIMER  are used like this:          COUNTER  process-to-be-timed  TIMER ( return) #ms                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            This block supports disk error recovery in a device independent    manner.  It is affected by changes in the buffer manager,       however, such as 32-bit id's,  IDENTIFY ,  (BUFFER)  etc.    #RDS & #WTS  are the read and write retry counts.               IDENTIFIER  returns the absolute block # of the most recent        read attempt.                                                ERROR?  is code because it is in the critical path.  If there      was no error the retry count is reset, the  PREV 2@ is drop'd   leaving the buffer address on top and control passes out        via EXIT.  If an error occurred the retry count is decre-       mented and if zero, the count is reset.  The results of the     decrement are then left on the stack (true=more left).       /BLOCK/ & /BUFFER/ must act the same as (BLOCK) & (BUFFER).        Note that they abort for unrecovered errors and must be         bypassed when doing a SWEEP. When they abort, /BLOCK/ leaves    buffer not in, while /BUFFER/ preserves buffer unupdated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    These editor extensions embellish the resident editor which was    compiled from the load Block 54 during Target Compilation.   ADVANCE   increments the USER variable  SCR  .                  N and  B  move forward and back one block, respectively.        WIPE   erases the current ( SCR ) block to blanks, with 0 in the   first cell.                                                  M  copies the current line under a specified block and line.    K  swaps the insert and find buffers.                           S  searches the blocks from  SCR  to the block whose number is     the argument to  S  for a match on the pattern.                 Usage:  n S pattern                                                                                                          N and B  are made available from  FORTH .                       (COPY)  does not  FLUSH  and is used by  BLOCKS  in Block 36.   COPY  identifies the given destination block no. as  UPDATEd .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This simple utility tests target-compiled programs.             NEW  is the block in which target code starts.  It is presumed     that 8 blocks of code exist.  This constant must agree with     its version in  COMPILER .                                                                                                   GO  copies a string of code from RAM to location 0.  MOVE cannot   be used since it resides in that region.  It then jumps         to the location of the startup code.                         TRY  loads 8 blocks into PAD and calls GO.  You must have 8192     bytes available at PAD.                                      INSTALL copies the 8 blocks from NEW to 0, from whence the disk    boot in Block 2 can load them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  pF6809/30 polyFORTH Level 4                                  This is the target testing help screen.  Feel free to change it if you move the target program area or make other changes.                                                                      You may re-display this by typing  HELP .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       This block provides compatibility between Target Compiler usage    for testing an application which is destined to be target       compiled in a resident environment.  The major differences      are:                                                               1. RAM is segregated into a separate region, whose alloca-      tion is controlled by  'R ,  THERE , and special versions       of  ALLOT ,  VARIABLE , and  CVARIABLE .                        2. Simulated versions are provided for words which exist        only in the target compiler, such as  LABEL ,  EQU , and        ORG .  Their behavior is functionally compatible.               3. Dummy versions are provided for words that are relevant      only in target compiling such as  HOST ,  FORTH , and  | .When you are ready to test your application with this block,       construct two load blocks: one which loads this block before    your application, and one for use when actually target          compiling.  See Targeting FORTH for more details.            This is the principal load block for the Target Compiler.       NEW  is the block where the target code will begin.             M6809  is the name of the polyFORTH source code.                LOG  is a dummy for a logging word that prints names and           addresses.  The real version is in Block 110.  See the          Shadow Block for Block 110 for instructions on use.          HEAD  allots space for the target dictionary heads.             HDS  stores the current and default size of a dictionary header.?TWO  indicates whether HOST definitions of target constants are   desired.                                                     'H  is the target H.     VOC  is the target CONTEXT.            ORG  sets 'H,  HERE  gets it and  GAP  increments it.           'R  is the next available location in target RAM.               THERE  gets  'R  and  ALLOT  increments it.                     W0  is the target address of the beginning of target code.      WINDOW  sets  W0  and initializes the other variables.          NAME  returns the name of the last word defined                 PREVIOUS  returns its target address                            -'  searches for a word in the target dictionary                                                                                FORTH  is an immediate vocabulary used with HOST                HOST  is an immediate vocabulary in which host definitions and     host versions of target definitions are placed               ASSEMBLER  is the target assembler vocabulary                                                                                   HASH  is a redefinition of host's dictionary hash algorithm        used to re-hash for the target vocabulary                    TARGET  moves the last definition from host's vocabulary to the    (unnamed) target vocabulary                                                                                                  TWIN  constructs a host version of a target definition                                                                          This is the  HELP  screen for the Target Compiler.                                                                              The words at the bottom of the screen provide headings for         addresses generated at compile time, to inform the programmer   of the progress of the compile and the sizes of various         components of the system.  The two numbers under "Nucleus"      give the last byte of  ROM  and  RAM  used, respectively.       "Edit" and  "Asm" report the last addresses in the editor       and assembler.  The last two numbers report the final           addresses in  PROM  and  RAM .  The latter should agree with    the starting address of the block buffers, obtainable at this   time by typing  FIRST .  or you may compute it:                    limit  2000 NB * -                                           The region just below the block buffers contains the system     variables.  See also the shadow block for Block 61.                                                                          This version of memory access words compiles target code into      RAM.   Block 49 compiles onto disk.                                                                                          Most words are analogous to those in Block 49.                                                                                  RAM is a n-byte array for storing the target dictionary         FLUSH is redefined to copy RAM onto disk starting at block NEW     Note that a reference to this FLUSH appears at the end of       block 61.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This version of memory access words compiles target code onto      disk.  Block 48 compiles to RAM                                                                                              DICTIONARY  clears the target dictionary and selects the           default header size                                          >T  converts a target address to a host RAM address                                                                             TC@ TC! T@ T! T+! are target versions of C@ C! @ ! and +!                                                                       C,  and  ,  are redefined to compile into the target dictionary CMOVE  is redefined to move from host to target                 DUMP  is redefined to dump the target dictionary                                                                                The old versions of C, , CMOVE and DUMP are available in the       FORTH vocabulary                                                                                                             EMPLACE  constructs a target definition.  When executed it         will compile a reference to itself.                          (CREATE)  constructs a target entry.  It includes a header if WD   indicates and links into the vocabulary indicated by VOC                                                                     CODE  creates a target CODE entry                               EQU  (for equate) defines a host constant ( CONSTANT  will be      redefined)                                                   LABEL  names the current position in the target dictionary      STRING  constructs a string in the target dictionary                                                                            BEGIN  and U) as well as all other assembler words are redefined   to compile into the target dictionary                        USE  is also so redefined                                                                                                                                                                       The words in this block are redefined to act on the target         dictionary as their host counterparts act.                                                                                   Note that all dictionary searches are restricted to the target     (unnamed) vocabulary                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         These words redefine the standard structure words in Block         102 to use the new target run-time code.                                                                                     The word  (  is redefined to work inside target definitions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block gives the Target Compiler defining words which          compile target definitions referencing the run-time code        that has now been assembled into the target system's nucleus.                                                                Two versions of CVARIABLE and VARIABLE are provided for either     PROM or RAM.  PROM variables point to their value from          their parameter field.  IBM-PC system variables are stored      in high RAM for security and memory protection, thus look       like PROM variables.  PROM variables are selected by            FORGETting the RAM version of VARIABLE (in Block 60).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This screen editor uses simple, visible, 1-letter commands.  It   is compatible with previous polyFORTH editors and with the      instructions in Starting FORTH.  It contains cursor commands    present on most CRTs with some cursor control capability.     Note that words in blocks 55, 58,  and 59 are in the FORTH        vocabulary. Additional editor words are built as extensions;    see Block 20.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 These words, though intended for editing support, are in the       FORTH vocabulary.                                                                                                            >TYPE types a line after moving it to  PAD .  This is necessary    because in a multi-user environment you can't type from a       block buffer.                                                                                                                LIST lists a block in the form in which you see it now.         L  lists the current block, and selects  EDITOR .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This editor uses two string buffers, a "find" buffer (#F)    and an "insert buffer" (#I).  Their use is described in StartingFORTH.                                                                                                                          P  puts text following or the insert buffer in the current line.X  deletes the current line, moving lower lines up, with blank     fill from the bottom.                                        U  inserts the text following or the insert buffer under the       current line, moving lower lines down and dropping line 15      off the bottom.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              TILL  deletes all the text from the character pointer up to        the find buffer.  TILL  sets the find buffer.                   The usage is:  TILL pattern                                  E  deletes backwards from the character pointer, the number of     characters in the find buffer.  It's good for deleting          a piece of text which has been found by  F .                 I  inserts text, and sets the insert buffer.  Usage:  I text    D  searches for and deletes the first occurrence of the find       buffer.  D  sets the find buffer.  Usage:  D pattern         F  finds the text in the find buffer.  It also sets the find       buffer.  The usage is:  F pattern                            R  replaces text found by  F  with the text in the insert buffer   and sets the insert buffer.  The usage is:  R text                                                                                                                                                                                                           -MATCH  takes the text address (d) and the text                    length (the first n) and tries to find a find buffer in it.     The find buffer begins at  s  and has a length of  (the 2nd)    n .   If the search is successful, the values returned are a    false, and the address just after the end of the string.  If    the pattern is not found, the values returned are a true (non   zero) and garbage.                                           <CMOVE  is a backwards character move.  It's good for copying      from a field into an overlapping field in higher memory.        <CMOVE  is backwards because it starts at the end of the        string and moves the rear-most characters first.             CMOVE  is an ordinary character move, starting at the beginning    of a string, and working forward. It's good for most copying,   and when fields overlap, it copies to a field in lower          memory.                                                                                                                         This block contains the hardware-specific features needed    to implement the 'screen editor'.  They are implemented in such a way that you may easily modify the key words for any CRT with a simple cursor-positioning function.                                                                                           (TAB) - Given line (0-23) and column (0-80), positions the         cursor to that position.                                     (MARK) - The purpose of this routine is to highlight the           position of the editing cursor, by whatever means the hard-     ware permits: reverse video, underlining, etc.  If none of      these features are available, insert a caret (HEX 5E).          Given an address and count of a string to be highlighted,       sets the highlighting, outputs the string, and resets it.                                                                                                                                                                                                       The definitions loaded in this block define polyFORTH for theMotorola 6809 computer, without consideration for any           environment-specific features such as disk & terminal drivers.                                                                  The "nucleus" definitions include run-time code for the compilerwords ( : , etc.).  Target words which compile these addresses  are subsequently defined.  Thus there can be only  CODE         definitions until blocks 51-53 are loaded.                                                                                      The compiler in blocks 99-103 and the assembler in blocks       111-115 are the ones for the target system's programmer.  These may not be present in target compiled applications except under license from FORTH, Inc.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           This block controls the entire target compilation of the     system, including both standard FORTH features (block 60) and   configuration characteristics:  disk & terminal drivers, amount of memory, number of disk buffers, presence/absence of heads,   etc.  Consult the Target Compiler documentation for a discussionof specific details.                                                                                                               Note especially the  ALLOT  on line 5.  This sets the  RAM   pointer to the start of the system variables area.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              The numbers in parentheses following the definitions in the next   several blocks represent calculated numbers of "clocks" for     the execution of these primitives.  These numbers may be used   for comparative timings.                                                                                                     cell  The run-time code for 16-bit literals in definitions.     byte  The run-time code for 8-bit literals in definitions.                                                                      ':'  A gap left at compile time, into which the target system's    compiler (block 101) will compile the high-level portion of :   Target applications without a compiler may recover this space   by defining   : GAP   DROP ;                                                                                                 colon  The run-time code for all  :  definitions.               EXIT  In addition to its glossary definition,  EXIT  is the        run-time code for  ; .                                          Following the model discussed in the previous block, this    block defines the run-time code for more compiler words, with   GAPs left for the compile-time code to be added later.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             These definitions are the run-time code for the compiler     directives that form structures, whose names are the upper-case equivalents.                                                                                                                       2>R  is the run-time code for  DO .   It is so named and     allowed to keep its head because it is occasionally useful      as a double-length version of  >R .  Its converse,  2R> is      found in Block 15.                                                                                                                 The  HERE  on line 5 marks the beginning of code shared by   loop  and  /loop .  It is the destination for the  BRA  on      line 8.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            These simple primitives are excellent examples of FORTH's    assembler usage, especially the use if the  IF ... ELSE ... THENstructure.                                                                                                                         Note, as in the previous block, the use of  HERE  on line    5 to mark an address of code to be shared by both  0=  and  = . The same technique is repeated in  0<  for  <  and  > .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            These memory reference operators provide additional examples of good assembler practice.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        The stack operators in this block deal with 16-bit cells.    They offer excellent examples of stack manipulation in code.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       The words 1+, 2+, etc. provide more than convenience: they   are both shorter and faster than 1 + , etc., and thus should    always be preferred.                                               Note the shared code here:  the  HERE  on line 5 is the      address for  BRA  on line 6, for example.  Thus the code        field for  1+  points to the second instruction of  2+ .  This  saves 6 bytes of space at no cost in time.                         Also note that  2*  and  2/  use arithmetic shifts, and are  thus signed operations.  2/  differs from  2 /  in terms of     rounding behavior.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 By convention, all arithmetic operators with  MOD  in their  names are unsigned operations;  all others in this block are    signed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         MOVE   moves a string, given source and destination addresses      and count.  All arguments are in bytes;  however, odd           addresses and counts may not be used, as the move is cell-      by-cell for speed.                                                                                                           CMOVE  copies byte for byte from source to destination n bytes.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Some less common arithmetic operators:                          NEGATE  returns a two's complement                              ABS  returns an absolute value                                  NOT  reverses a truth value.  It is identical to  0=                                                                            MAX  returns the maximum of 2 signed numbers                    MIN     "     "  minimum "  "   "       "                                                                                       0 and 1 are defined as constants.  This provides a shorter         reference during compilation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 A variety of miscellaneous operators:                           FILL  fills a given area of memory with the character on the top   of the stack.                                                ERASE  fills a given area of memory with zeroes.                                                                                'S  returns the address of the top of the stack.  Its use is       discouraged.                                                 ?DUP  duplicates a number if it is non-zero.                       Using  ?DUP IF  makes an  ELSE DROP  phrase unnecessary.                                                                     I'  returns the limit of a LOOP (the 2nd item on the return        stack)                                                       J  returns the index of an outer LOOP (the 3rd item)                                                                            LEAVE  sets the  LOOP  limit to its index.  This will force an    exit at the end of the current iteration.                        The prefix '2' indicates that these operators take           arguments two stack items long.  These may be 32-bit numbers or pairs of 16-bit numbers.  The latter usage justifies the        inclusion of these primitives in the program;  other double-    length operations are loaded by Block 9.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           The  USER  variables for this system are documented in the   Processor Supplement.  Additional  USER  variables are defined  in Block 26.                                                       Most  BACKGROUND  tasks have shorter user areas than TERMINALtasks, usually not extending beyond the first 10 bytes.                                                                                                                                                                                                         GOLDEN holds the dictionary heads used by EMPTY                 The space after  'BUFFER  holds the block# last written.        The space after  'NUMBER  holds the high-order part of the         last number converted.                                       The space after  DISK  is non-zero in case of a disk error.     The space after  PREV  contains the disk buffer status cells.                                                                                                                                   This is the inner loop of the multiprogrammer.                                                                                  WAKE   This code is the instruction stored in the  STATUS  cell    of a user who is ready to become active.  STATUS  normally      contains a  JMP  to the next task in the round robin.        PAUSE   This entry to the multiprogrammer starts by storing        WAKE  in the  STATUS  cell so the task will wake up next time   around the loop.                                             WAIT   This entry point is the normal entry for a task who will    stay 'asleep' until some event (such as an interrupt) awakens   him.                                                         STOP   This provides high-level access to  WAIT .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               GRAB  asserts control of a facility by storing the current         user's  STATUS  address in its facility variable.  If the       facility isn't available (i.e, value non-zero and not this      user)  waits in  PAUSE  until it is available.               GET  calls  GRAB  after one lap around the multiprogrammer.                                                                     RELEASE  releases a specified facility variable by setting it      to zero assuming it belongs to the current user -- does         nothing if it doesn't.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       EXECUTE  takes an address of a FORTH definition (any kind) and     executes it.  On completion, control will pass to the FORTH     word following  EXECUTE .                                    @EXECUTE  performs like  EXECUTE , but takes an address contain-   ing the address of the word to be executed.  If the address     contains 0, branches to the  NEXT  at the end of  TYPE  (i.e.   does nothing)                                                TYPE  stores its arguments in  PTR  and  CTR , respectively, and   executes the routine whose address is in the user's  'TYPE . EXPECT  stores its address argument in  PTR  and its count         (negated) in  CTR , then executes the routine whose address     is in the user's  'EXPECT .                                                                                                  CR ,  PAGE ,  TAB ,  and  MARK  execute CRT-specific routines.  EMIT  outputs a single character.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This block contains inner functions for umbilical protocols.                                                                    ><  swaps bytes.                                                                                                                XMIT  is a straight  TYPE  operation over the umbilical.                                                                        RECV  is a straight  EXPECT .                                                                                                   ACK  is basically a straight  KEY  for the umbilical.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           SCREEN  controls use of the umbilical link for a conversation      about a single purpose.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      These are basic output words plus some orphans:                 HERE  returns the next available byte in the dictionary         PAD  returns the address of a string scratch pad                                                                                COUNT  takes the address of a string (with count in first byte)    and returns the address plus one and count (for TYPE, etc.). BLANK  blanks a string (as  ERASE  zeroes one).                                                                                 ?ANY  is used by  SPACES  to count spaces in bunches of 8       SPACES  types spaces.  A 0 or negative count does nothing.      SPACE  types a space.                                                                                                           HOLD  stores a character in a string indicated by PTR.             It is used during number conversion.  The string actually       runs backwards from PAD.                                                                                                     Number formatting words:                                        DIGIT  converts a digit to ASCII, including the hex offset.                                                                     <#  initialized the string in  PTR .                            #>  discards the number and returns the string address and countSIGN  tests the sign (from the 3rd stack position) and buffers     a minus.                                                     #  buffers a single digit.                                      #S  buffers digits until the number reaches zero.                                                                               (.)  formats a signed integer.                                  .  types a signed integer, with trailing space.                 U.  types an unsigned integer, with trailing space.             U.R  types an unsigned integer, right justified in a field.                                                                     DUMP displays a region of memory, given address and count.      String output words include:                                    ?R@  tests a truth value, if set it returns with an in-line        string address; otherwise it exits from the calling word        (usually ABORT")                                                                                                             ABORT  clears both stacks and goes to  QUIT  for input.                                                                         abort"  is the run-time code for  ABORT" .  Upon error, it types   the word at  HERE , the message, and saves >IN in R# for the    editor.                                                      dot"  is the run-time code for ."  It types the message.                                                                        ABORT"  and  ."  are host definitions that reference the just-     defined run-time code.                                                                                                                                                                       The heart of the interpreter is here:                           SOURCE  locates the input string: BLK zero indicates the message   buffer ( S0 @ ); non-zero the block buffer containing  BLK .                                                                 (WORD)  does the work of extracting a word from the input string   and storing it at HERE 2+ (allowing room for the  LINK  which   will go at  HERE  if the word is compiled) until  CNT expires   If the string is empty, returns a 0 count.  If the string is    of even length, fills out the last byte in the last cell with   a space.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Dictionary search:                                              HASH  selects one of 8 strings based on the first character,       current vocabulary (nibble from stack).  It exits from the      the calling definition when vocabularies are exhausted.                                                                      -FIND  searches the dictionary.  If it finds a match, it returns   the parameter field address, link field address, and 'false'.   Otherwise, it repeats  HASH .                                                                                                Head structure:                                                 +0  Link (points to link)                                       +2  i--n nnnn  Immediate                                        +n  tccc cccc  As needed; t iff truncated.                                     Looks like  t  on +3 also used as SMUDGE.                                                                                                                                        ?DIGIT  increments the address on the stack and then determines   if the character at that location is a valid digit in the cur-  rent  BASE . If the character is valid, a true flag is left on  the stack with the value of that digit underneath.  Otherwise,  only a false flag is left.  For both cases, the address is not  removed.                                                                                                                      (NUMBER)  attempts to convert the string whose address is on the  stack to binary and, if successful, will leave the result on    the stack.  If conversion fails due to illegal characters, an   abort will occur, with an error message echoing the string      followed by "?".                                              NUMBER  executes  (NUMBER)  via the system variable  'NUMBER .                                                                                                                                                                                                  DEPTH  returns the number of cells on the parameter stack          (before it was called).                                      -'  searches the dictionary for the word following in the          input stream.  If found, returns 0 ('false'), with the link     field address and parameter field address beneath.              If not found, returns 'true' and  HERE 2+.                   '  searches the dictionary for the word following in the input     stream, and returns its parameter field address if found;       aborts if not found.                                         INTERPRET  interprets the input stream, executing words found      in the dictionary and converting numbers to binary, until       either the input stream is exhausted or a word is found that    cannot be found or converted.                                RESET  clears the current user's return stack.                                                                                  ...continued on next block.                                     ...continued from previous block.                                                                                               WORD  calls SOURCE which skips if in RAM.  A reference to  BLOCK  relplaces the second reference to SOURCE.                     QUIT  stops whatever is executing and resumes the (terminal's)     normal behavior of awaiting input for the terminal.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          ?ABSENT  checks to see if the block whose number is on the stack   is present in memory.  If the block is present,  ?ABSENT        removes the block number from the stack, and leaves an          address pointing to the beginning of the block buffer, and      then leaves the routine which called  ?ABSENT , by means of     the  ' EXIT BR  on line 4.  If the block is not listed in       PREV  then  ?ABSENT  leaves the block number on the stack,      and exits normally through  NEXT .                           ?UPDATED  finds an old buffer.  If the old buffer has its          update bit set,  ?UPDATED  returns the buffer address with      the block number of the buffer on top.  The block number is     later used by  SECTOR  to write out the data.  If the           buffer's update bit is not set,  ?UPDATED  returns the buffer   address and jumps out of the word which called  ?UPDATED .   PREV points to the most recently accessed block buffer.         PREV 2+  points in turn to each, but skips over  PREV's  buffer.UPDATE  sets the most significant bit of the cell in  PREV         which contains the block number of the most recently accessed   block.                                                       ESTABLISH  makes the oldest buffer ( PREV 2+ ) into the newest     buffer (by putting the pointer into  PREV ) and marks the new   buffer with the block number on the next-to-top of the stack.IDENTIFY  marks the newest buffer (in  PREV ) with the block       number from the stack, with  OFFSET  added.                  BUFFER  takes a block number and frees a buffer, and marks that    buffer with the gien block number, then leaves the address of   the buffer on the stack.                                     BLOCK  takes the block number, then ensures that the block is      present in a buffer, and leaves the address of the buffer.   FLUSH  writes out all  NB  (number of buffers) buffers.         EMPTY-BUFFERS  erases all the block buffers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    (BUFFER)  finds a disk buffer and makes it available to the        task, writing out the old data if the data was marked as        updated with  UPDATE .                                       (BLOCK)  performs the actual work of  BLOCK .                                                                                   DRIVE  sets the user variable  OFFSET  which is added to the       block number to yield a physical block number.  This lets a     user address different disk drives without computing offsets.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                IMMEDIATE causes the preceding definition to be executed           rather than compiled at compile time.                        [COMPILE]  forces compilation of what would otherwise be an        IMMEDIATE  compiler directive following in the input stream. The above words help the host computer compile the target          system's compiler.                                           COMPILE  compiles the execution address of the following word.  ?ALLOT  increments  H  and returns 'true' if there are fewer       than 250 bytes remaining in the user's dictionary.           ALLOT  increments  H  and aborts if insufficient space remains. , and C, compile the given value into the dictionary.              and increment  H  appropriately.                             ?CELL  returns 'true' if a literal requires a full cell.        ] turns on the compiler which continues compiling until            [ or ; is encountered.                                                                                                       [  leaves compile mode.                                         LITERAL  compiles an 8 or 16-bit literal preceded by the address   of the appropriate run-time code ( cell  or  byte ).         [']  compiles the parameter field address of the following word    as a literal.                                                CFA  converts a link field address to a code field address.     USE  changes the code address of the most recent definition to     the address given.                                           ;code  leaves compile mode, and sets the code address of the       most recent definition to the address following.  This is the   run-time behavior of  ;CODE .                                ;CODE  enables the Target Compiler to create defining words in     the target system whose run-time behavior is code.           DOES>  enables the Target Compiler to create defining words in     the target system whose run-time behavior is high-level.     SMUDGE  toggles the smudge bit in the latest definition.        The words in this block allow the target system to compile         "data items".  The use of these words is documented in the      Reference Manual, Starting FORTH, and other sources.         CREATE  executes the word whose address is in the system           variable  'CREATE .                                          (CREATE)  creates a dictionary entry whose link is at  HERE ,      head and code address following.  The code field is set to      the code at  create  (block 64); other defining words may use   (CREATE)  to construct the head and then substitute other       code addresses.                                              The compile time portions of  CREATE (above),  CONSTANT ,  USER    and  :  are compiled into the gaps left before their code in    blocks 63, 64.                                               VARIABLE  shares the code belonging to  CREATE ;  CVARIABLE is     available only on processors that support odd byte addresses.                                                                This block defines the compiler directives that provide            structures in the target system's compiler.  The use of         FORTH's structure words is described in the Reference Manual    and other sources.                                                                                                           The compiler directives use the compile-time stack to pass the     addresses needed to compile branches.  Depending upon the       processor, relative offsets or absolute addresses may be        compiled; this computer uses the latter.                     Most of the words that perform the run-time action for these       words have the same name, but are in lower-case and headless.   They are defined in Block 65.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                VOCABULARY  defines a word which, when executed, will set          CONTEXT  to the sequence of vocabulary indices given.        The two versions of  VOCABULARY  are for the target system and     the Target Compiler, respectively.  The latter is used to       define the target system's vocabularies  FORTH ,  ASSEMBLER ,   and  EDITOR  (in block 54).                                                                                                  The following definitions are for the target system's compiler. CODE  starts an assembler definition.  The code address is set     to the new definition's parameter field.                     ;CODE  terminates the compile-time portion of a defining word      and commences its run-time portion, which is in code         DOES>  is functionally equivalent to  ;CODE  except that its       run-time behavior is described in high level.                                                                                                                                                EMPTY  resets  H  to the beginning of the task's dictionary        given by  H 2+ .  The system definition chains in  GOLDEN       are used to restore the eight link heads,  CONTEXT  and         CURRENT  in the task's user area .                           STRING  compiles a string terminated by a given ASCII character.ABORT"  compiles an error message which will be displayed          along with the word at  HERE  and the task restarted if the     number on the stack at run-time is 'true'.                   ."  compiles a string which will be displayed at run-time.      HEX  and  DECIMAL  change  BASE  appropriately.                 LOAD  saves the current position in the input stream               and calls  INTERPRET  then restores the input stream pointer.THRU loads a range of block numbers, inclusive.  It compacts       load blocks and encourages sequential arrangement of code.   (  stops interpreting until the delimiter ")" is encountered.   HI  loads the  system extensions from block nine.               /INITIALIZE  initializes the umbilical port and transmits          a 12H command until a character is received back from           the host.                                                                                                                    Builds at the end of  ROM  the interrupt vector table.             For development purposes the interrupt vectors have been        initialized to point to vectors at the end of RAM.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           These tables are used by  START  (next block) to initialize        RAM.  The important tables follow a dummy clock interrupt.   RAM  is the table containing initial values for the system         variables, following the map in block 75.  Note that initial    values are needed only for the first 38 bytes.               OPERATOR  contains the information necessary to initialize the     one task that is active following a boot.  The format of this   table is identical to those constructed by  TERMINAL (blk 24,   25).  It starts with STATUS , S0 , and the output and input     interrupt code.  The next 46 bytes map to the 1st 46 bytes      of the user variable area, as described in block 75.  Note      that the latter part of this table, the following table  ram    and the startup code will be overlaid by the dictionary after   it has been used at boot time.                                                                                                                                                               The order of operations in the startup sequence is:                1. The bootstrap loads the 8K program into memory starting at   0 and jumps to 0.  This address contains a jump to the start-   up code,  POWER-UP .                                                                                                            2.  POWER-UP  initializes registers  U & R to the address of    OPERATOR , clears the processor status, initializes  S , and    sets  I  to the address of  START .  NEXT  will therefore       branch to  START .                                                                                                              3.  START  initializes  GOLDEN , erases  OPERATOR's  user       area and copies initial values into its 1st 46 bytes.  If       the original copy of  GOLDEN  didn't contain 1 (FORTH vocab.)   it is presumed that we are starting from power-up (as opposed   to a boot) and the buffers are cleared.  Finally  WIDTH & SCR   are initialized and  OPERATOR  is greeted and activated.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     This block contains the  LOG  code for the target compiler.                                                                     It is optionally loaded on line 4 of Block 45 and produces        a map of the words in the target dictionary with their          addresses.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block loads the assembler that will be resident in the        target system.  It should be omitted from any non-extensible    target application.                                                                                                          WAIT  &  WAKE  are perpetuated into the  TARGET  dictionary        since their  EQU  constructions are solely found in  HOST                                                                    The last RAM address of the assembler is displayed at the end.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This block defines the various addressing modes of the 6809.                                                                    Use of an addressing prefix leaves a constant in the  MODE        assembler variable for intgration into the following op-code.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 This block contains the logic for determination of                the opcode post-byte for indexed addressing modes.                                                                            MR>  resets the  MODE  variable when the assembly process is      completed.                                                                                                                    ?CELL  determines if an offset is a byte or word and compiles      the appropriate number of bytes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              This block contains the defining words which are used to          create op-code definitions.                                                                                                   ALU  creates op-codes whose operands are implicit in the          instruction.                                                  UNI  creates op-codes which have a single operand.              BIN  creates op-codes whose operands specify a source and         destionation.                                                 COND  is used to create opcodes which modify the condition code   register                                                      SWP  defines register transfer and exchange instructions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       This block contains the definitions of most of the opcodes        used by the 6809.                                                                                                             The three tables of  BIN  op-codes are organized by the           commonality of the registers upon which each may act.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         The push and pull instructions are defined in this block          along with the notation necessary to specify pushes and         pulls of multiple registers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  The constants at the beginning of this block specify              the numbers assigned to each of the hardware registers in the   6809.                                                                                                                         The macro  NEXT  is defines here to assemble in-line.                                                                           The constants on lines 9 and 10 define the conditions based       upon which branches are assembled by the subsequent             relationals.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      