                                                                                            : DUMP ( a n)   OVER + SWAP DO  63 L# @ < IF  PAGE THEN            I 'B PAD 16 MOVE  PAD 16  I 'A -TEXT IF                            CR I 8 U.R  2 SPACES  I POOP  THEN 16 /LOOP ;                                                                                                                                                                                                             ( Get dumps from dead gasses)                                   1600 CONSTANT BEFORE   1660 CONSTANT AFTER      HEX                                                                             : 1BLOCK ( b a - b')   OVER BLOCK  OVER 400 + ROT DO                  I T@ OVER !  2+ 2 +LOOP  DROP UPDATE  1+ ;                : SUCKS ( n)   B800 0 DO  I 1BLOCK  400 /LOOP 1+                   E000 C000 DO  I 1BLOCK  400 /LOOP  DROP  FLUSH ;                                                                             : GOOD   BEFORE SUCKS ;      : BAD   AFTER SUCKS ;                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              String output words include:                                    ?R@  tests a truth value, if set it returns with an in-line        string address; otherwise it exits from the calling word        (usually ABORT")                                                                                                             dot"  is the run-time code for ."  It types the message.                                                                        ."  is host definition that references the newly defined           run-time code.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ( POWER-UP)   HEX                                            LABEL POWER-UP   VISUAL T@ # R LD   VISUAL 2+ T@ # S LD            VISUAL D LD   U D ST   START # I LD   NEXT                                                                                   HERE   /PROM 2- ORG   POWER-UP ,                                       /PROM 10 - ORG   A55A ,  ORG                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             This is the So. Cal. Gas application load block.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                ( Bell Prover: target RAM load, for TRY)   HEX                  HOST DEFINITIONS   FORGET ALLOT   : ALLOT ( n)   'R +! ;        0 DICTIONARY   2000 WINDOW   6000 ALLOT   6000 EQU /PROM        DECIMAL  ASSEMBLER DEFINITIONS  VARIABLE 'variable              ~ FORTH : INTERRUPT ( a n)   /PROM + T! ;   HOST DEFINITIONS    THERE EQU U   : GAP ( n)   DROP ;   ( Nucleus)  63 65 THRU      ( Target compiler)  51 53 THRU   FORGET VARIABLE                ( Primitives)  66 74 THRU   ( Users)  137 LOAD                  ( Multitasking)  76 77 THRU  ( Output)  84 LOAD ( 86 THRU)      FORTH : ASSY    ASSEMBLER ; IMMEDIATE                           ( Miscellaneous Extensions)  135 LOAD                           148 LOAD  FORTH : MSG   (CREATE) DOES>  COUNT Type ;            ( Vectored Input)  130 LOAD  179 LOAD                           ( Sonic)  146 LOAD  16 19 THRU  131 132 THRU  258 260 THRU      HEX CR  /PROM HERE - .  B800 THERE - . DECIMAL FLUSH                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            ARRAY  is a defining word used to create ROM word arrays.                                                                       DOES  is used to initialize an array location with the             PFA of the following word.                                      Ex.   n arrayname word DOES                                                                                                  IS  initializes an array location ( 2nd)  with the address         specified on the top of the stack.                              Ex.   n arrayname address IS                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 This block defines the user variables.                                                                                          RADIX  is used in the same manner as  BASE.                     SLAB  is used as a scratch pad area for number conversion          and for MARK.                                                                                                                vCNT , vCTR , and vPTR  are used by the VISUAL task                in the same manner as CNT, CTR and PTR are normally used.       It may be necessary in the future to have a user variable       PTR  if the HOST task needs to do numeric conversion.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        ACTIVATE  starts a task whose address is given executing the       remainder of the colon definition in which  ACTIVATE  occurs.   Note that this code must end in  STOP ,  QUIT (for a terminal   task) or in an infinite loop.                                                                                                NOD  puts the task to sleep and makes sure it stays that way.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    TASK  creates a task definition table.  Note:  Tasks are              allotted only 12 bytes of user area.                                                                                     BIRTH  takes the address of a task definition table and            and constructs the terminal task's user area in RAM.                                                                         TESTER  is the task responsible for running all of the tests.   INTERFACER   is the task responsible for interfacing to the        prover operator (user).                                      COMMUNICATOR   is the task responsible for sending test results    to the host (data collection) computer.                      OPERATOR   will be the task that talks through an umbilical to     the development system.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      U<  returns true only if the first unsigned input is less than     the unsigned second.  Nothing is 0 U<.  All but -1 are -1 U<.                                                                WITHIN  returns true only if  n  can be found in a seach that      that begins with  l  and increments until reaching  h-1 .       If  l  and  h  are equal false is always returned.                                                                           2**  given  " n "  returns  2 to the " n " th                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   AVG  gives the unsigned average of two unsigned inputs.                                                                         UM/  divides a double by a single giving an unsigned quotient.  U*/  multplies an unsigned single by the ratio of two singles.     The multiplier is unsigned but the divisor must be positive.                                                                 UT*  gives the triple product of an unsigned double and single. UT/  divides a positive triple by a positive single giving an      unsigned double.                                             UM*/  multplies an unsigned double by the ratio of two singles.    The multiplier is unsigned but the divisor must be positive.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 SQRT  returns the 14 bit root of a 28 bit number.  15 bit roots    are increasingly incorrect as their magnitude grows, although   the identity relationship holds.  They tend more and more to    truncate toward even numbers.                                HEX 7FFD DUP U*  1 0 D- 2CONSTANT TRIAL                         : SCAN   0  -1 0 DO  I 0 TRIAL D+ SQRT  2DUP - IF                     CR I 0 TRIAL D+ D.  DUP .  SWAP THEN DROP  LOOP DROP ;    SCAN ( Gives the following results)                             3FFD0008 7FFC                                                   3FFD0009 7FFD      ( 7FFD DUP U* D. 3FFD0009)                   3FFD0010 7FFC                                                   3FFE0004 7FFE      ( 7FFE DUP U* D. 3FFE0004)                   ( Advancing  SCAN  through the next 65K gives:)                 3FFF0001 7FFF      ( 7FFF DUP U* D. 3FFF0001)                   3FFF0004 7FFE                                                   40000000 8000      ( 8000 DUP U* D. 40000000)                   <#  initialized the string in  vPTR .                           #>  discards the number and returns the string address and countHOLD  stores a character in a string indicated by vPTR.         ?DIGIT  increments the address on the stack and then determines   if the character at that location is a valid digit in the cur-  rent  RADIX. If the character is valid, a true flag is left on  the stack with the value of that digit underneath.  Otherwise,  only a false flag is left.  For both cases, the address is not  removed.                                                      SIGN  appends either a space or a `-' to a string, depending       on its sign.                                                 /DIGIT  divides the 32-bit integer on the stack by  BASE ,         leaving the 32-bit quotient and the remainder, a digit to       be put in an output string by  # .                                                                                                                                                           #  converts the next low-order digit for output, adding it to      the output string.                                           #S  converts all remaining significant digits for output.  Adds    at least one digit (0 if the number is zero) to the string.  *DIGIT  takes a 32-bit integer and the address and length of a     string, and converts one digit from the string adding it to     the low end of the integer.                                  +/-   converts a single precision number or double precision      with sign extension.                                          #s  converts the specified number of digits for output          (u.)  given the address of the variable and the number of          desired digits  converts the variable to a string,              returning the string address and count.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This calendar is appropriate for applications in which        dates entered are distributed over many years (birthdays, etc.).The internal form of dates are compatible with the 'month-name' version in the previous block.  This version requires 32-bit    input number conversions, as the dates are originally entered   in the form  mm/dd/yy (a 32-bit number).                                                                                        This calendar stores the date as a 16-bit count of days         since JAN 1, 1900, which was a Monday.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          An additional vectoring of type has been implemented               in order to handle the XON XOFF support for the VISUAL 50.      Interrupts must be disabled whenever vCTR is manipulated        in task code for the  VISUAL 50.                                                                                             vCTR  is initialized with the number of characters to transmit,    vPTR  with the address of the text to be transmitted.  If       vCNT  is non-negative ( not waiting for an XON ) , then the     transmitter is enabled, otherwise the transmitter will be       enabled in interrupt code when an  XON  character is received                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                This block supports typed variables.                            'VARIABLE  points to the behavior address for input variables      and to the variable pfa for result variables.                @VARIABLE  returns the address of the VARIABLE and forces          'VARIABLE to point to the behavior vector                    (B)  is used to define the behavior fields of an input               variable                                                   'V.  contains address of current display formatting word           A display formatting word expects a variable address on the     stack and returns the address and count of the string           representing the number.                                                                                                     continued in next block                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         'V!  contains the address of the current store word.               A store word expects a variable address (2nd) and a             double precision number (top).  A store word returns true       if the given number is valid and has been stored;               otherwise  the variable address and false are returned.      .FORM  is the display formatting word for range variables       !FORM  is the store word for range variables.                   RANGE  defines inputs variables which fall within                  a 16 bit signed range of numbers. The form specified            indicates this range of numbers and the number of digits        before and after the decimal point.                          .D  is the display formatting word for double input variables   !D  is the store word for double input variables                dVARIABLE  defines double precision input variables                ( # of digits after the decimal point required).                                                                             FORM  is used to define the format of a single precision           variable with or without punctuation, which is range limited.                                                                 before and after indicate the number of digits before and         after the decimal point respectively.  low and high  specify    the range for the single precision value.  low and high both    equal to zero indicate that there is no range checking on       these particular values.                                                                                                     Note :  All input variables have a byte preceding them to          indicate whether or not they have been modified.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             !MEMBER  is used to store values into MEMBER type variables.    MEMBER  defines unsigned variables which fall within the           specified limits.  The limits must fall between 0 and 255.                                                                   !SET is used to store values into SET type variables.                                                                           Given a display word  S{  and  }S  are used to define              a set.                                                                                                                       S{  saves the display and store vectors, for a set variable      and returns the starting address of the set list                                                                               }S  declares the set variable, stores the set address and         the variables prompt string                                                                                                                                                                   MISSING  is set to indicate that at least one of the required      values has not been entered.                                                                                                 VOID  marks a variable as not having been entered               got  returns true if the input variable has been updated                                                                        GOT  returns true if none of the tested variables were             missing.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     sVARIABLE  is a defining word for single precision input           variables                                                                                                                    .CdA  and  !CdA  are used to display and store the CdA#         .OPID  and  !OPID  are used to display and store the operator      ID number.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   .date  and  now  are used to display and store  DATE variables                                                                  DATE  is a defining word for date variables expressed in the       form  mm/dd/yy.                                                                                                              CHARS  is a defining word for variable strings.  The string        length is expected on the top of stack.                                                                                      .CHARS  and  !CHARS  are used to display and store  CHAR           variables.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   (A)  Used to define offsets into the RAM portion of a              result variable                                              (R)  Used to define offsets into the ROM portion of a              result variable.                                             IN  contains the result ( result variables)                         contains the raw unfiltered value  ( analog variables)      SUM  contains a 24-bit sum for analog variables                 OUT  contains the filtered DAC values for pressures             FIXED  contains the corrected DAC values                        aCORR  and  bCORR  contain correction factors                   PRIOR                                                                                                                           .SHOW  the analog variables display word                        PLACES  the number of digits to the right of the decimal point  >CONV  conversion routine from DAC units to display units       HLIM and LLIM are the high and low limits for the variable      This block is used to define the result display words.                                                                          R.n   given the number of decimal places and the double            precision number, displays the number right justified in an     8 byte field.                                                                                                                ?WITHIN  determines if the given value falls within the current    limits and displays an out of bounds condition with an          asterisk.  .LOW  and  .HIGH  are used to display the asterisk?DWITHIN  same as ?WITHIN but used for double precision numbers                                                                 SCALE   converts the given value                                .RESULT, .tANALOG, .pANALOG  and  .flow  are display words         for result variables.                                                                                                                                                                        This block contains all the result variable defining words         and analog conversion words.                                 CFH, RESULT, tANALOG and pANALOG  are used to define the           different types of result variables.  See blocks 177 - 178.                                                                  >DDD  converts the differential pressure value to w.c.           The differential pressure values can span from -.693            2.770 for a total span of 3.493 w.c..                                                                                          >TTT  converts the fixed temperature data to deg. Fahrenheit     Full scale range of the temperature is 37.5 to 100.0.                                                                          >FLOW  converts the calculated flow per second ( with 6 decimal      places )  to flow per hour ( with 3 decimal places ) .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Prover unit screen input variables are defined in this block                                                                    An "o" OR "c"  preceding a variable indicate open or check         rate respectively.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Meter screen input variables are defined in this block.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Intest specific variables are defined in this block.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Outtest specific variables are defined in this block.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Maintenance limit input variables are defined in this block.                                                                    Preceding  "o" s  indicate open rate                                       "c" s  indicate check rate                                      "h"    indicates a high limit                                   "l"    indicates a low limit                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Option menu variables are defined in this block                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 This block and the next are used to define analog input            variables                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block supports the visual 50 type interrupt.                                                                               At all baud rates above 4800, the Visual 50 requires XON           XOFF support.                                                                                                                <V50>  handles both input and output interrupts.                 OUTPUT - if vCTR is greater than zero, the next character        is output,  vPTR  incremented,  vCTR  decremented.  If  vCTR    is now zero, the task is awoken.                                                                                               INPUT - if an XOFF character is received, vCNT is made           negative, and the transmitter is disabled.  If an XON           character is received,  vCNT  is cleared,  and if  vCTR is      positive, then the transmitter is reenabled.                                                                                                                                                  Status messages are defined in this block.                                                                                      .STATE  displays the given status message at the specified        TAB  location.                                                IMSG  is a defining word for input status messages.                                                                             'FL'  is a table of flow rate string addresses.                 'MD'  is a table of mode string addresses.                                                                                      TSTAT  contains the addresses of 2 strings which specify           the current test status.                                     SUBTST  contains the address of the string which specifies         the current stage of the current test ( MLEAK MET OPEN CHECK)`START'   is used to indicate that a request has been made to      start a test.                                                RUN  is set true whenever a test is begun.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      The  REPAIR#  table contains proposed repairs number for           all legl make, size, and case meters.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This is a 3-dimensional matrix specifying the repair class       to which a meter belongs.  A meter's repair class is determined by the meter's  size, make, and class.                                                                                         The repair class specifies the criteria by which the repair      code is chosen.                                                Repair Class                                                         0 - proposed repair undefined. Repair code 0.                   1 - TIN                                                         2 - WELDED                                                      3 - CAST                                                        4 - M2                                                          5 - M2C34                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  The logic for determining the repair code, as specified in       section 5.3 of the spec, is implemented in this block.                                                                         @CORR  fetches the percent correction for the last intest.         Note:  this value is 100 times the  %correction              ?dP  returns true if the dP was within the maximum dP             specified for the meter.                                      TIN  assigns repair code according to spec 5.3.1                WELDED  assigns repair code according to spec 5.3.2             CAST  assigns repair code according to spec 5.3.3               M2  assigns repair code 6 to MAKE 2 meters whose previous repair was between the years 1962 thru 1965.                          M2C34  if repair code 6 was not assigned to a meter MAKE 2,      CASE 3,4 then the repair code is determined by  CAST 5.3.3     ?REPAIR  determines the repair code for a meter according to       size, make, and case.                                        This block is used to define strings indicating the test state.                                                                  Only the  TESTER  task should execute these words.  Upon        execution the address of the string is placed in the            appropriate status variable.                                                                                                   STATE  is used to define the different subtest strings.                                                                         END  is used to define strings indicating reasons for            terminating a test.  At execution time the word  END  sets      the string address into  TSTAT  , disables edge interrupts,     halts the  TESTER  task  and sets the analog sampling to the    open test state.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               ABORTS  is used to define strings which specify why              a test was aborted.                                                                                                            .STATUS  displays the current status.                                                                                           ?key  displays the current status and indicates whether a          character has been struck.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   NEEDLE  sets direction, resets and enables the interrupt for the   CA1 input of VIA #1 (the needle LED input).  The behavior of    the interrupt routine will depend upon other variables such     as  #REVS  and  'WATCHER .  If both cells are zero then         -SHOT  used to detect the edge (the interrupt will have         disabled itself and stored  WAKE  at location zero).                                                                         -SHOT  returns true if the edge has not been detected (interrupt   is still enabled).                                           DISARM  disables the photocell interrupt.                                                                                       EDGE  defines words that will wait until a specific needle edge.   It sets  'WATCHER  but assumes  #REVS  is already zeros.     BLACK  waits until the next black edge on the LED input.        WHITE  waits until the next white edge on the LED input.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        DEFER  is the only clever word here.  It prevents interrupts       while swapping status values between the user area and loc-     ation 2.  The content of  'WATCHER  is pointed to the alter-    nate of these locations.  It is used to defer a "wake-up"       from the VIA interrupt until after printer output.                                                                           d.n  displays double numbers with  n  decimals.                 .p  .d  and  .t  display values formated as pressure, differen-    tial pressure and temperature respectively.                  .f  displays a flow rate or volume non-destuctively.            LOG  replaces the  D+  in  COMPUTE  in order to generate a hard-   copy of the inputs and computed results for each second of      the test.                                                    sTIME  keeps test start time in clock ticks.                    !TIME  sets  sTIME  and prepares location 2 for  DEFER .        ?TIME  logs cumulative data at the end of a test.               This block is used to abort tests when any of the set              limits are exceeded.                                                                                                         ?limits   aborts the current test if the given value is            outside the limits of the current analog or result variable.                                                                 ?LIMITS   fetches the filtered value of the current variable,      converts it to display units, and does limits check on it.                                                                   SCAN  scans the analog and result variables  to determine        if any of the values are out of limits in  which case the       test is terminated.                                                                                                            WATCH  activates the  SCANNER  task to scan the analog             variables every 100 MS.                                                                                                      COMPUTE  scales the flowrate to the interval size and adds it      into the volume accumulated on the stack.  The flag returned    is true if the last interval has been included.              SAMPLE  takes a  PRECHARGE  offset and a  NEEDLE  direction.  It   performs test setup work including waiting for the edge, set-   ting the initial  PRIOR  temperatures and the first clock       TARG  point.  (Done by  NEEDLE  CORRECT  INTERVAL ).         !%CORR  cleans up at the end of a test and computes the percent    correction based on an assumed volume of 2 cubic feet.       fTEST  is the inner control loop for a flow rate test.             The stack arguments are passed to  SAMPLE .  Some logging       words are included that may be stripped out in production.   oTEST  and  cTEST  condition  fTEST  for open or check rate        testing.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     This block defines the meter and system leak tests.             2.1" and 1.5" are constants representing raw input values         corresponding to 2.1 and 1.5 inches water column respectively..1"D  is a constant specifying the raw input change representing  a change of .1 inches water column.                           TMETER  is the meter leak test.  During the meter leak test       the differential pressure is observed for 10 seconds with the   valves set as follows:  A,D open   K,J,F,E,C,B closed           H to open flow, G to atmosphere                               TLEAK  is the system leak test.  During the system leak test      the differential pressure is observed for 20 seconds with the   valves set as follows:  A,D,E open  K,J,F,C,B closed            H to open flow, G to atmosphere.  Note:  Valve C is a slower    valve then valve F.  Therefore valve C is closed prior to valv  F to prevent the dP from going out of range.                  @adP  defined so as not to change 'VARIABLE.                    The intest and outtest are defined in this block.               CHECK  initiates the check test.                                OPEN  initiates the open test.                                  iMET  exercises the meter prior to an intest.  The meter is       exercised with a combined flow rate going thru the meter.       We are able to do this since we have the needle's width in      which to switch to the check flow rate prior to running         the CHECK test.                                               oMET  exercises the meter prior to an outtest.  Only               while we are looking for the needle's edge, a combined flow     rate is used.  Once the exercise time is initiated, we switch   to an open flow rate.  As soon as MET has ended a stable open   flow rate is required for the  OPEN  test.                   ?INFO  verifies that allo required inputs for running the test     have been entered.                                           tIN  runs the in test.                                          dPSELF  Monitor the differential pressure to atmosphere           during the self tests.                                                                                                        tSELF  runs the self tests.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     #RESULTS  - indicates the number of output results stored          for this meter.                                                                                                              OBUF - the output buffer for storing the last 8 output            results for the same meter.                                                                                                   !RESULTS  stores the most recent output results.                tOUT  runs the out test.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This block defines the screen data structure.                   Screens are defined in a single block. There are 3 tables          associated with each screen: the screen text table,             the input field table and the output field table.            The screen text table consists of a list of text strings           and their location on the screen.                            screen    ( 0 ) pointer to title    ( 2 ) pointer to behavior             ( 4 ) pointer to associated input field table                   ( 6 ) pointer to associated output field table                  ( 8 ) # of text strings on this screen                          ( 9 )  list of coded strings                          coded string    ( 0 ) row  ( 1 ) column ( 2 ) count ( 3) text   The field tables consist of field locations and  the associated    variables.                                                   fields     ( 0 ) number of fields ( 1 ) field spec.             field spec.    ( 0 ) row  ( 1 ) column ( 2 ) address of value   >FIELDS  points to the field table for the current menu         STEP#  contains the # of input fields on the current menu and    the current input index.                                       CHANGE  a flag used to indicate whether or not the current user  should be allowed to change the values displayed on the current menu.                                                          .TEXT  displays the menu title, status, and text prompts        .INS  displays the input field values.                          STEP  advances to the next input field on the menu, waits for      input, then displays the value inputted.                     @INS  queries for all screen inputs.                            .MENU  displays the specified menu.                                                                                             V.  displays the current variables value if the variable is       valid, otherwise displays a template for the variable value                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   This block is used to display report screens.                   .report  displays the result variables for the given screen                                                                     .REPORT  displays the given report screen, and continuously        displays the result variables, until a key is struck.                                                                        RECALL  displays the text portion of the given screen              and the last  8 output results for the current meter.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This block is responsible for password protecting menus.                                                                        pass  contains the password string                                                                                              ?PASSWORD  prompts the operator for the password and receives      characters into the  ?PASS  buffer until a non-numeric key is   struck. ( Only the last six characters entered are saved. )                                                                  -VALID  returns true if the password entered was not valid                                                                      UNLOCK   assigns to  'ENTER  the following behavior:               'ENTER  will enable the menu for input if a valid password is    entered otherwise, the password prompt is redisplayed                                                                       .PMENU  is used to display a password protected menu                                                                            v's   is an array of valves switching words                     VSTATE  switches to the valve state specified by the               variable  vstate                                             VSET  switches the current state of the specified valve.        .VSTATES   assigns to  'VALVES  the behavior of  VSTATE            and the displays the given password protected screen         .VSET  assigns to  'VALVES  the behavior of  VSET                  and displays the given password protected screen                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 