                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Here starts the notorious target compiler:                   NEW is the block where the target code will begin.              CP/M is the name of the TotalFORTH source code.                 AHEAD sizes the next word, leaving it in the input stream.      LOG is a dummy for a logging word that can be loaded from 111.  HEAD holds the target dictionary links.                         WDS stores the current and default size of a dictionary header. ?TWO flags if host definitions of target constants are made.    WO is the target address of the beginning of target code.       'H is the target  H .     VOC is the target  CONTEXT .          ORG sets  'H ,  HERE gets it and  GAP increments it.            'R is the next available location in target RAM.                THERE gets  'R  and  ALLOT increments it.                       | makes the next definition headless.                           WINDOW sets  WO  and initializes the other variables.                                                                           NAME returns the name address of the last word defined.         PREVIOUS returns its target parameter field address.            VOCAB returns its unmasked hash index in target space.          -' searches for a word in the target dictionary                                                                                 FORTH is an immediate vocabulary used with HOST                 HOST is an immediate vocabulary in which host definitions and      host versions of target definitions are placed               ASSEMBLER is the target assembler vocabulary                                                                                    TARGET moves the last definition from host's vocabulary to the     (unnamed) target vocabulary determined by  VOCAB .                                                                           TWIN constructs a host version of a target definition.          SIZE computes the header size of the next word, leaving it in      the input stream.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This version of memory access words compiles target code intoRAM.   Block 49 compiles onto disk.                                                                                             Most words are analogous to those in block 47                                                                                   RAM is a 8192-byte array for storing the target dictionary      FLUSH is redefined to copy RAM onto disk starting at block NEW     Note that a reference to this FLUSH appears at the end of       block 61.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       This version of memory access words compiles target code ontodisk.  Block 48 compiles to RAM.                                                                                                DICTIONARY clears the target dictionary and selects the            default header size                                          >T converts a target address to a host RAM address                                                                              TC@ TC! T@ T! T+! are target versions of C@ C! @ ! and +!                                                                       C, and , are redefined to compile into the target dictionary    CMOVE is redefined to move from host to target                  DUMP is redefined to dump the target dictionary                                                                                 The old versions of C, , CMOVE and DUMP are available in the       FORTH vocabulary                                                                                                                This block is loaded for the Z80 target assembler.                                                                           EMPLACE constructs a target definition.  When executed it          will compile a reference to itself.                          (CREATE) constructs a target entry.  It includes a header if WDS   indicates and links into the vocabulary indicated by VOC                                                                     STRING constructs a string in the target dictionary             CODE creates a target CODE entry                                EQU (for equate) defines a host constant (CONSTANT will be         redefined)                                                   LABEL names the current position in the target dictionary                                                                       BEGIN and U) as well as all other assembler words are redefined    to compile into the target dictionary                        USE is also so redefined                                           The words in this block are redefined to act on the target   dictionary as their host counterparts act.                                                                                         Note that all dictionary searches are restricted to the      target (unnamed) vocabulary.                                                                                                    EXTRA IN THIS SYSTEM:  ['] definition in line 2 changes meaning    of ['] when used in a FORTH : definition.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       These words redefine the standard structure words in block   102 to use the new target run-time code.                                                                                        ( is redefined to work inside target definitions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  These words provide the compile-time code that must be       attached to the run-time code previously assembled.                Exactly the right-sized gaps were left in the dictionary and are now being filled.  Those gaps (blocks 63 and 64) must be    adjusted if changes are made here.                                                                                                 Two versions of CVARIABLE and VARIABLE are provided for      either PROM or RAM.  PROM variables point to their value from   their parameter field.  PROM variables are selected by          FORGETing the RAM version of VARIABLE (in block 60).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               The definitions loaded in this block define FORTH for        any 8080 type CPU, without consideration for any environment    specific features such as disk & terminal drivers.                                                                              The "nucleus" definitions include run-time code for the compilerwords ( : , etc.).  Target words which compile these addresses  are subsequently defined.  Thus there can be only  CODE         definitions until blocks 51-53 are loaded.                                                                                      The compiler in blocks 99-104 and the assembler in blocks       113-116 are the ones for the target system's programmer.  These may not be present in target compiled applications except under license from FORTH, Inc.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           This block controls the entire target compilation of the     system, including both standard FORTH features (block 60) and   configuration characteristics:  disk & terminal drivers, amount of memory, number of disk buffers, presence/absence of heads,   etc.  Consult the Target Compiler documentation for a discussionof specific details.                                                                                                               The values for  0PROM  and  0RAM  set the program start and  ram bottom.  Line 4 sets the numbers of buffers and from that   computes the FIRST buffers address and the user area address.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      This block controls the entire target compilation of the     system, including both standard FORTH features (block 60) and   configuration characteristics:  disk & terminal drivers, amount of memory, number of disk buffers, presence/absence of heads,   etc.  Consult the Target Compiler documentation for a discussionof specific details.                                                                                                               The values for  0PROM  and  0RAM  set the program start and  ram bottom.  Line 4 sets the numbers of buffers and from that   computes the FIRST buffers address and the user area address.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Lengths of name fields are normally identical.  Host machine's     name field is one too BIG if (1) count is even.  Truncation     is no problem whatsoever.                                    Lengths of name fields are identical if the '11 hasn't got a sp    as its last character.  Otherwise, the '11 is one too big.                                                                   EXIT    ( NAME C@ 1 AND  NAME 2- CFA 2- @  0< OR  0= +  T+!)                                                                    FORTH : IMMEDIATE   80 PREVIOUS  NAME 2- CFA 2+ NAME - -              NAME 2- CFA 1- C@  32 = +  T+!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           