 3033 PL/1          3.6ms   ---     780us   Disbelieve                                                                           This is a very convenient benchmark, since we have an instruct-    ion in our "virtual machine" whose behavior is exactly that     described by EDN.                                            On a 150 ns 9300, we get:                                          Compile test case    6.9 ms elapsed                                                 72.6 ms including -MATCH assembly           Execute test case    128 us                                                          120 us with tare removed                   11/23               2808 us with tare removed                   11/73               1326 us with tare removed                                                                                EDN claims  11/23    996 us  (How?  Well, ours is slow!)                    8085    1058 us   5 MHz  my measurement tare removed            8086     193 us  10 MHz                                         68000    244 us  10 MHz                                         Z8000    237 us   6 MHz                             This utility uses the Curtis PD-5000E PROM burner at 9600 baud.    It is set up for byte organized memory!                                                                                      NEW  is the starting block of target compiler output, assumed to   be at zero in memory.                                        STS  is a buffer for reading burner status.  Its first cell is     also used to communicate 1-byte messages to the burner task.                                                                 SIZE  contains the size of the PROM in bytes.                   BIAS  contains the byte origin of the PROM in target output.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block implements the behavior and services that the burner    expects of its host once a burner operation has been started.   All such operations eventually end with an "E" command.                                                                      RAM  contains SIZE bytes of data representing the PROM image.   THERE  contains the current "location counter" address in  RAM .GOOD  is marked with the "good" status indications received from   the burner.                                                  BAD  is marked with any failing status received from the burner.TBL  is an execute table for command characters from the burner.:B  defines behavior for the following character in  TBL .      SERVE  listens to the burner and does whatever it asks.                                                                         The remaining definitions describe the behavior that the burner    expects of us in response to each of its defined command        characters.                                                  This block contains simple burner protocols.                    TASK  clears all status information and returns the address of     the burner task.                                             WAIT  spins until the burner task has completed an operation.   STATE  displays the status information received from the burner.SYNC  waits for the burner to complete an operation ane then       displays its status information.                                                                                             1byte  activates the burner to perform a simple 1-byte operation1BYTE  defines a simple 1-byte command protocol.  Such commands    are:  RESET (resets burner), PGM (starts burning from RAM),     VER (starts verify from RAM), and  MANUAL (manual mode).                                                                     ?sts  and  rb  activate the burner to return 3-byte status or      SIZE-byte PROM contents.                                     ?STS  and  READBACK  perform the complete protocols for these.  This block supports PROM personalities and organization.                                                                        P@  takes a PROM-relative byte address, returning the address of   the corresponding byte of target compiler output.            P!  is the corresponding store operator.                                                                                        HUNK  sets up for operations on the given PROM number, where 0     and 1 are the low and high bytes of the first chunk of the      address space, 2 and 3 of the second, and so on.  Returns       loop parameters for  P@ 'ing the entire device.                                                                              PROM  defines a PROM selection word, given device type codes       from and to the burner, and device size in bytes.  The rest     of the words in this block select the PROM types they name.                                                                                                                                  This block contains the major user-accessible function of the      utility.                                                                                                                     FETCH  fills  RAM  with what should go into the  nTH  PROM.        The values for  n  are 0 and 1 for low and high bytes of the    0th pair, 2 and 3 for the 1st pair, and so on.               STORE  does the opposite.                                                                                                       BURN  burns the  nTH  PROM.                                     ANOTHER  burns another copy of the last PROM, saving some time.                                                                 CHECK  matches the "slave" PROM against desired contents for the   nTH  PROM.                                                   SUCK  reads back the nTH PROM and stores it out into the image.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 The line discipline is biphasic.  In the resting state, either     serial port may provide a character to the '11; the character   will simply be passed to the other port.  HOWEVER, if you       type ESC, the resting state will terminate and you will come    back to the '11.  If the Prolog sends hex 80, we will ask it    to cough up its disk or other service request.  Protocols:                                                                         Disk reads                   Disk writes                        80                           80                                         04                            04                        00                           01                                 hi                           hi                                 lo                           lo                                         1k                            04                                                     1k                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Things done recently:                                           * Make PROMs that work                                          * Get enough RAM to target, verify reproduction                 * Figure out why we have trouble resynching on restart          * Determine whether restart thru RAM is safe                    * Test and identify interrupts, check edge/level sensing                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       