É}É|␀␀␀␀␀ï¸Œ¸H˝L˝W˝l˝a˝é‡∏oÄ␀˛ÿ˛¸û¸˚ˇ6˚O˛ ˝3␀˝Á˝À␀␀Î␀␀‡a␀p␀␀␀˝ÜWHOÓ∆DACU.1  8/24/88 ˇÂÚ‡(„x˛ﬁ‚≤‚3‰€‚3‡Ä‰A‡„x‡‚ïÂ9‡„x‡@‡Â—‰•‡	Â„·—ÌI˛¢¸|Á=ˇ<Á#ÈŒhi Èïˇﬂ‰Œﬂ‰6O∑áˇ¸ﬂÊÉ•Z&˝ﬂÊnüﬂ˛ŒoÄŒo␀éˇOÃˇ†˝ﬂ˛Æ°nîˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇˇ•Z˛‘˛–˛Ã˛»˛ƒ˛¿˛º˛∏˛¥˛∞˛¨ˇ†                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                This block is loaded when, after booting, the operator             types  HI .  The routines loaded here are in the common         dictionary which is available to all users.                                                                                  <CREATE>  This compiles, before each new entry, the block no.      in which the source resides.  The cost is 2 bytes/entry.        This information is used by  LOCATE  to display the block.      To disable this feature, change  B/H  to 0 and put parens       around the phrase  ' <CREATE> 'CREATE !                                                                                      Note that the 'mm/dd/yy' calendar is used here.  Change            the default setting of the year every January!                                                                               Additional terminal tasks (if any) should be prompted after        the phrases on line 14.                                      Line 15 is the product release date.  Please don't change it.      These constants provide names for the utilities supplied     with FORTH/level 4.  The system "help screen" lists them for    convenience.  The user is encouraged to add the names of        additional utilities and application programs as they are       developed, both here and in the "help screen" in Block 11.                                                                      LOADS   is provided for one-word invocations of an overlay.        (Usage:  141 LOADS APPLICATION  allows  APPLICATION  to         load Block 141)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              This is the system help screen.  Feel free to change it when    you reconfigure the system.  Note that the utilities each have  their own help screens.  You can find them by typing               utility LIST   and looking for the phrase:  n HELPS          The number will be the block number of the help screen.                                                                            You may find it convenient to configure your application as autility, if so, please include a help screen that comes up upon loading, and add to the utility constants on Block 10.                                                                             If your application utility is important, add an entry to    the system help screen in Block 11.                                                                                                Note that in the utilities  HELP  is redefined to display theutility help screen.                                                                                                            RELOAD  jumps to the startup location of the program, which        causes  the system to be re-loaded.                                                                                          The interrupt routines for the clock & function keys are defined   here. They use the FIRQ and work through the monitor's          second level vector structure. Second level vectoring is        through locations  F0 - FF.  By storing FF in location EF2E     the firmware is signalled that any interrupts that are not      from sources with which it is currently concerned are passed    to user interrupt routines whose addresses appear at F0 - FF.The word  VECTOR  is defined as a code ending for an interrupt     routine, which stores the address left by  BEGIN  in the        appropriate second level vector.                                                                                             The sequence on line 7 initializes counter 2 of the PTM for     use by the clock to count 10 ms intervals.                      WITHIN returns true iff  n-1 < h  unsigned.                     U<  compares numbers in the range  0 - 65535.                   IMMEDIATE marks the last word defined as immediate (executed       during compilation); it is useful for adding to the compiler.DEFINITIONS permits words to be defined in vocabularies other      than FORTH .                                                 [COMPILE] compiles an immediate word.  It is useful only           when writing compilers (see the FORTH compiler directives).  .(  types the text up to a ")". Executes immediately.           'HEAD  returns the address of the 1st byte of the word which       follows in the input stream (with  B/H  subtracted).         LOCATE types the source block of the following word.  It relies    upon the version of  CREATE  loaded in Block 9.              FORGET forgets the next word and all words defined more            recently.  It strips the 8 dictionary chains back to before     the word being forgotten.  It won't forget the nucleus.      ~ causes the next word to be compiled with a full length name.  RECOVER  decrements  H  by 2 bytes.  Used after an unneeded  ; .HELPS displays a block of text presumably helpful to the user.     Each application should have one.  See shadows for 10 and 11.SHADOWS is the offset to the shadow blocks (even mult. of 3).   CAST   takes offset & block no. to toggle corresponding drive.  Q displays a block of text associated with each block of code.     Termed shadow blocks, these keep comments out of the way        while permitting their easy retrieval and maintenance.       .S   displays the current stack items non-destructively.        THRU   Loads blocks l thru h. Displays stack before each block. ?CREATE will generate a warning message when words are redefined   It is useful for debugging, although redefined words are not    bad technique.                                                                                                                                                                                  These versions of  2VARIABLE  and  2CONSTANT  are not correctif you plan to target compile this block.  Their colons need to be preceded by  FORTH ; moreover, if the resulting program is   intended to be capable of compiling new words of these types,   you will need to insert a second definition as illustrated in   the Target Compiler appendix.                                      Finally, the HOST version of  2VARIABLE  must be brought intoconsistency with whatever version (RAM or PROM) of  VARIABLE    you are using.                                                     Some examples:                                                     FORTH : 2VARIABLE   CREATE 4 ALLOT ;  ( PROM)                   FORTH : 2VARIABLE   VARIABLE 0 , ;     ( RAM)             The 1st version allocates target RAM, which must be initialized in the power-up sequence.  See definitions of  VARIABLE  in     this system and its documentation for details.                                                                                     These versions of the 32-bit relational, MAX , and MIN words are of the "half-range local" variety.  That is, they           behave as signed operators (  -2,000 is less than -1,000  ) whenthe arguments are within half of the range of the numbers,      or +/- 2,147,483,648.   However,  3,000,000,000 is seen as less than  2,000,000,000  because the double precision integers are  manipulated as 2's complement numbers.                                                                                             Note that the prefix 'D' in general denotes operators whose  arguments are signed, 31-bit integers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             The prefix 'M' denotes mixed-precision operations, some of   whose arguments are 16-bit and some 32-bit.  The exact usage    of these operators has been determined by experience to be      useful in the largest number of cases.  Please check the        stack arguments in the definitions, glossaries, or programmer's card carefully.                                                    The operators in this block are all signed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     M*/  is an mixed precision version of  */ , which allows     multiplication of a signed 31-bit integer by a ratio of 16-bit  numbers, with a 48-bit intermediate product, giving a precise   signed 31-bit result.  The divisor must be unsigned; this       restriction, which is rarely inconvenient in practice, gives    substantial improvement in speed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  The commands in this block are used by the  DISKING          utility and the Data Base Support System.  Otherwise they may   be omitted from Block 9 if you wish to conserve space.          C+!  adds an 8-bit value to a byte whose address is given.      -TEXT  takes the address of two strings and a length and returns   +1 if the 1st string has a higher ASCII value, -1 if it has     a lower value, and 0 (false) if they are equal.              <TEXT  is like  -TEXT , but returns 'true' if the first string     has a lower value, 'false' otherwise.                        KEY  awaits one keystroke and returns the ASCII code typed.     EMIT   outputs a single character.                              TEXT  takes a delimiter and parses the input stream, moving the    string from  >IN  to the delimiter or the end of the string     to  PAD .                                                    MSG  is used to create output ascii strings for serial devices. e.g. MSG CR  2 C, 0D C, 0A C,                                   These editor extensions embellish the resident editor which was    compiled from the load Block 54 during Target Compilation.   ADVANCE   increments the USER variable  SCR  .                  N and  B  move forward and back one block, respectively.        WIPE   erases the current ( SCR ) block to blanks, with 0 in the   first cell.                                                  M  copies the current line under a specified block and line.    K  swaps the insert and find buffers.                           S  searches the blocks from  SCR  to the block whose number is     the argument to  S  for a match on the pattern.                 Usage:  n S pattern                                                                                                          N and B  are made available from  FORTH .                       (COPY)  does not  FLUSH  and is used by  BLOCKS  in Block 36.   COPY  identifies the given destination block no. as  UPDATEd .                                                                    The program as booted is capable of converting 16-bit numbers for input and output.  This block adds 32-bit output.              Note that  #  and  #S  are the only numeric output words     that need to be recoded to handle 32-bit numbers.                                                                               /DIGIT  divides the 32-bit integer on the stack by  BASE ,         leaving the 32-bit quotient and the remainder, a digit to       be put in an output string by  # .                           #  converts the next low-order digit for output, adding it to      the output string.                                           #S  converts all remaining significant digits for output.  Adds    at least one digit (0 if the number is zero) to the string.  (D.)  converts a 32-bit signed integer for output, leaving the     address and length of the string as arguments for  TYPE .    D.  converts and types a 32-bit signed integer.                                                                                    The rules governing 32-bit numeric input are discussed at    length in Starting FORTH and other documentation.                  Note that upon completion of input number conversion the     number of digits to the right of the rightmost punctuation in   a 32-bit number is given in the USER variable  PTR .  PTR  is   negative if there was no punctuation (e.g., a 16-bit result).   The high-order part of an unpunctuated number is contained in   'NUMBER 2+  in case the user wants to check it (it should be    zero or -1 depending on the sign of the result).                *DIGIT  takes a 32-bit integer and the address and length of a     string, and converts one digit from the string adding it to     the low end of the integer.                                  CONVERT  converts a string to binary, leaving the address of the   first un-convertable digit and length of the remainder.      (NUMBER)  converts a string at address  a  to binary, leaving a    32-bit number if it was punctuated, 16-bits otherwise.       ASSIGN  provides a facility for giving behavior to a vectored      execution variable.  Usage is as follows:                                                                                       VARIABLE loc                                                    : mode   loc ASSIGN  { desired behavior } ;                                                                                  Invoking  mode  sets  loc  to the address of the behavior.      The behavior can be performed by:   loc @EXECUTE                                                                                >< swaps the bytes in the top stack item.                       TALLY  adds 1 to the 8-bit number in the address given.         RECURSE  invokes the definition that contains it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               HIS  takes a task address and user variable and returns the        address of that user variable for the task.                     Ex.:  TYPIST CTR HIS  (returns the addr. of  TYPIST's  CTR ) BACKGROUND  sets up the task definition table for a non-terminal   task, given user area size and stack sizes.  Table is:             +0  User area address   +2  Size of same, bytes                 +4  S0                  +6  HERE                          BUILD  takes the address of a table set up by  BACKGROUND  and     builds the task's user area in RAM.                          ACTIVATE  starts a task whose address is given executing the       remainder of the colon definition in which  ACTIVATE  occurs.   Note that this code must end in  STOP ,  QUIT (for a terminal   task) or in an infinite loop.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                TERMINAL  creates a terminal task definition table,                given its device address and partition size.                 CONSTRUCT  takes the address of a task definition table and        constructs the terminal task's user area in RAM.             PROMPT  "awakens" a terminal task by displaying the system help    screen, leaving it awaiting keyboard input.                  SEND  sends the remainder of the input stream (from the terminal   only) to the task whose address is given.  That task will       interpret the string, executing commands found there.                                                                           The version of  TERMINAL  given in this block is for         resident applications only.  For an application which is        to be target compiled, it must be preceded by  FORTH  in        order to make it a  HOST  definition.  CONSTRUCT , on the       other hand, must be in the target dictionary, and is            normally executed as part of the start-up initialization code.     Refer to block 75 and the Processor Supplement for a map     of the  USER  variables initialized here.                          The argument on the stack when this block is entered is the  size of the task's dictionary.                                                                                                     This block is a good example of the use of interpretive      execution to perform non-time critical and infrequently used    procedures.                                                                                                                     ( This is the target compilable version. )                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This calendar is appropriate for applications in which        dates entered are distributed over many years (birthdays, etc.).The internal form of dates are compatible with the 'month-name' version in the previous block.  This version requires 32-bit    input number conversions, as the dates are originally entered   in the form  mm/dd/yy (a 32-bit number).                           If you convert to this calendar, remember to remove the      default setting of the year in block 9.                                                                                         To set the date:    12/27/82 NOW                                To type the date:   TODAY @ .DATE  (responds) 12/27/82 ok                                                                       Both calenders (blocks 30 and 31) store the date as a 16-bit    count of the days since JAN 1, 1900, which was a Monday.                                                                        DATED  produces a dated help screen (see  SYSTEM ).                These routines provide convenient control and access to      the system clock, which ticks once per 2.5 milliseconds.                                                                        COUNTER (in the program)  and  TIMER  are used like this:          COUNTER  process-to-be-timed  TIMER ( return) #ms                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               These definitions are the run-time code for the compiler     directives that form structures, whose names are the upper-case equivalents.                                                                                                                       2>R  is the run-time code for  DO .   It is so named and     allowed to keep its head because it is occasionally useful      as a double-length version of  >R .  Its converse,  2R> is      found in Block 15.                                                                                                                 To reduce PROM requirements, this system uses 8-bit signed   displacements.  The displacement is relative to the byte it is  stored in!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      This block defines the compiler directives that provide            structures in the target system's compiler.  The use of         FORTH's structure words is described in the Reference Manual    and other sources.                                                                                                           The compiler directives use the compile-time stack to pass the     addresses needed to compile branches.  Depending upon the       processor, relative offsets or absolute addresses may be        compiled; this computer uses the latter.                     Most of the words that perform the run-time action for these       words have the same name, but are in lower-case and headless.   They are defined in Block 65.                                                                                                ( : /LOOP ( a}   COMPILE /loop  , ;   IMMEDIATE)                                                                                                                                                   These words redefine the standard structure words in block   102 to use the new target run-time code.                                                                                        ( is redefined to work inside target definitions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This nucleus is used on the CT and RT.  It lacks an Editor and     uses the space thus conserved for very stable definitions       that the two applications share in common.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   This highly stripped down version of the Editor supports only      a minimal set of words required by the CT/RT applications.                                                                   LIST  and  L  are provided only because debugging from source is   nearly impossible without them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              These editor extensions embellish the resident editor which was    compiled from the load Block 54 during Target Compilation.   ADVANCE   increments the USER variable  SCR  .                  N and  B  move forward and back one block, respectively.        WIPE   erases the current ( SCR ) block to blanks, with 0 in the   first cell.                                                  M  copies the current line under a specified block and line.    K  swaps the insert and find buffers.                           S  searches the blocks from  SCR  to the block whose number is     the argument to  S  for a match on the pattern.                 Usage:  n S pattern                                                                                                          N and B  are made available from  FORTH .                       (COPY)  does not  FLUSH  and is used by  BLOCKS  in Block 36.   COPY  identifies the given destination block no. as  UPDATEd .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This simple utility tests target-compiled programs.             NEW  is the block in which target code starts.  It is presumed     that 8 blocks of code exist.  This constant must agree with     its version in  COMPILER .                                                                                                   GO  copies a string of code from RAM to location 0.  MOVE cannot   be used since it resides in that region.  It then jumps         to the location of the startup code.                         TRY  loads 8 blocks into PAD and calls GO.  You must have 8192     bytes available at PAD.                                      INSTALL copies the 8 blocks from NEW to 0, from whence the disk    boot in Block 2 can load them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This simple utility tests target-compiled programs.             NEW  is the block in which target code starts.  It is presumed     that 8 blocks of code exist.  This constant must agree with     its version in  COMPILER .                                                                                                   GO  copies a string of code from RAM to location 0.  MOVE cannot   be used since it resides in that region.  It then jumps         to the location of the startup code.                         TRY  loads 8 blocks into PAD and calls GO.  You must have 8192     bytes available at PAD.                                      INSTALL copies the 8 blocks from NEW to 0, from whence the disk    boot in Block 2 can load them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This block provides compatibility between Target Compiler usage    for testing an application which is destined to be target       compiled in a resident environment.  The major differences      are:                                                               1. RAM is segregated into a separate region, whose alloca-      tion is controlled by  'R ,  THERE , and special versions       of  ALLOT ,  VARIABLE , and  CVARIABLE .                        2. Simulated versions are provided for words which exist        only in the target compiler, such as  LABEL ,  EQU , and        ORG .  Their behavior is functionally compatible.               3. Dummy versions are provided for words that are relevant      only in target compiling such as  HOST ,  FORTH , and  | .When you are ready to test your application with this block,       construct two load blocks: one which loads this block before    your application, and one for use when actually target          compiling.  See Targeting FORTH for more details.            This is the principal load block for the Target Compiler.       NEW  is the block where the target code will begin.             M6809  is the name of the polyFORTH source code.                LOG  is a dummy for a logging word that prints names and           addresses.  The real version is in Block 110.  See the          Shadow Block for Block 110 for instructions on use.          HEAD  allots space for the target dictionary heads.             HDS  stores the current and default size of a dictionary header.?TWO  indicates whether HOST definitions of target constants are   desired.                                                     'H  is the target H.     VOC  is the target CONTEXT.            ORG  sets 'H,  HERE  gets it and  GAP  increments it.           'R  is the next available location in target RAM.               THERE  gets  'R  and  ALLOT  increments it.                     W0  is the target address of the beginning of target code.      WINDOW  sets  W0  and initializes the other variables.          NAME  returns the name of the last word defined                 PREVIOUS  returns its target address                            -'  searches for a word in the target dictionary                                                                                FORTH  is an immediate vocabulary used with HOST                HOST  is an immediate vocabulary in which host definitions and     host versions of target definitions are placed               ASSEMBLER  is the target assembler vocabulary                                                                                   HASH  is a redefinition of host's dictionary hash algorithm        used to re-hash for the target vocabulary                    TARGET  moves the last definition from host's vocabulary to the    (unnamed) target vocabulary                                                                                                  TWIN  constructs a host version of a target definition                                                                          This is the  HELP  screen for the Target Compiler.                                                                              The words at the bottom of the screen provide headings for         addresses generated at compile time, to inform the programmer   of the progress of the compile and the sizes of various         components of the system.  The two numbers under "Nucleus"      give the last byte of  ROM  and  RAM  used, respectively.       "Edit" and  "Asm" report the last addresses in the editor       and assembler.  The last two numbers report the final           addresses in  PROM  and  RAM .  The latter should agree with    the starting address of the block buffers, obtainable at this   time by typing  FIRST .  or you may compute it:                    limit  2000 NB * -                                           The region just below the block buffers contains the system     variables.  See also the shadow block for Block 61.                                                                          This version of memory access words compiles target code into      RAM.   Block 49 compiles onto disk.                                                                                          Most words are analogous to those in Block 49.                                                                                  RAM is a n-byte array for storing the target dictionary         FLUSH is redefined to copy RAM onto disk starting at block NEW     Note that a reference to this FLUSH appears at the end of       block 61.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This version of memory access words compiles target code onto      disk.  Block 48 compiles to RAM                                                                                              DICTIONARY  clears the target dictionary and selects the           default header size                                          >T  converts a target address to a host RAM address                                                                             TC@ TC! T@ T! T+! are target versions of C@ C! @ ! and +!                                                                       C,  and  ,  are redefined to compile into the target dictionary CMOVE  is redefined to move from host to target                 DUMP  is redefined to dump the target dictionary                                                                                The old versions of C, , CMOVE and DUMP are available in the       FORTH vocabulary                                                                                                             EMPLACE  constructs a target definition.  When executed it         will compile a reference to itself.                          (CREATE)  constructs a target entry.  It includes a header if WD   indicates and links into the vocabulary indicated by VOC                                                                     CODE  creates a target CODE entry                               EQU  (for equate) defines a host constant ( CONSTANT  will be      redefined)                                                   LABEL  names the current position in the target dictionary      STRING  constructs a string in the target dictionary                                                                            BEGIN  and U) as well as all other assembler words are redefined   to compile into the target dictionary                        USE  is also so redefined                                                                                                                                                                       The words in this block are redefined to act on the target         dictionary as their host counterparts act.                                                                                   Note that all dictionary searches are restricted to the target     (unnamed) vocabulary                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         These words redefine the standard structure words in Block         102 to use the new target run-time code.                                                                                     The word  (  is redefined to work inside target definitions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block gives the Target Compiler defining words which          compile target definitions referencing the run-time code        that has now been assembled into the target system's nucleus.                                                                Two versions of CVARIABLE and VARIABLE are provided for either     PROM or RAM.  PROM variables point to their value from          their parameter field.  IBM-PC system variables are stored      in high RAM for security and memory protection, thus look       like PROM variables.  PROM variables are selected by            FORGETting the RAM version of VARIABLE (in Block 60).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        This screen editor uses simple, visible, 1-letter commands.  It   is compatible with previous polyFORTH editors and with the      instructions in Starting FORTH.  It contains cursor commands    present on most CRTs with some cursor control capability.     Note that words in blocks 55, 58,  and 59 are in the FORTH        vocabulary. Additional editor words are built as extensions;    see Block 20.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 These words, though intended for editing support, are in the       FORTH vocabulary.                                                                                                            >TYPE types a line after moving it to  PAD .  This is necessary    because in a multi-user environment you can't type from a       block buffer.                                                                                                                LIST lists a block in the form in which you see it now.         L  lists the current block, and selects  EDITOR .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This editor uses two string buffers, a "find" buffer (#F)    and an "insert buffer" (#I).  Their use is described in StartingFORTH.                                                                                                                          P  puts text following or the insert buffer in the current line.X  deletes the current line, moving lower lines up, with blank     fill from the bottom.                                        U  inserts the text following or the insert buffer under the       current line, moving lower lines down and dropping line 15      off the bottom.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              TILL  deletes all the text from the character pointer up to        the find buffer.  TILL  sets the find buffer.                   The usage is:  TILL pattern                                  E  deletes backwards from the character pointer, the number of     characters in the find buffer.  It's good for deleting          a piece of text which has been found by  F .                 I  inserts text, and sets the insert buffer.  Usage:  I text    D  searches for and deletes the first occurrence of the find       buffer.  D  sets the find buffer.  Usage:  D pattern         F  finds the text in the find buffer.  It also sets the find       buffer.  The usage is:  F pattern                            R  replaces text found by  F  with the text in the insert buffer   and sets the insert buffer.  The usage is:  R text                                                                                                                                                                                                           -MATCH  takes the text address (d) and the text                    length (the first n) and tries to find a find buffer in it.     The find buffer begins at  s  and has a length of  (the 2nd)    n .   If the search is successful, the values returned are a    false, and the address just after the end of the string.  If    the pattern is not found, the values returned are a true (non   zero) and garbage.                                           <CMOVE  is a backwards character move.  It's good for copying      from a field into an overlapping field in higher memory.        <CMOVE  is backwards because it starts at the end of the        string and moves the rear-most characters first.             CMOVE  is an ordinary character move, starting at the beginning    of a string, and working forward. It's good for most copying,   and when fields overlap, it copies to a field in lower          memory.                                                                                                                         This block contains the hardware-specific features needed    to implement the 'screen editor'.  They are implemented in such a way that you may easily modify the key words for any CRT with a simple cursor-positioning function.                                                                                           (TAB) - Given line (0-23) and column (0-80), positions the         cursor to that position.                                     (MARK) - The purpose of this routine is to highlight the           position of the editing cursor, by whatever means the hard-     ware permits: reverse video, underlining, etc.  If none of      these features are available, insert a caret (HEX 5E).          Given an address and count of a string to be highlighted,       sets the highlighting, outputs the string, and resets it.                                                                                                                                                                                                       The definitions loaded in this block define polyFORTH for theMotorola 6809 computer, without consideration for any           environment-specific features such as disk & terminal drivers.                                                                  The "nucleus" definitions include run-time code for the compilerwords ( : , etc.).  Target words which compile these addresses  are subsequently defined.  Thus there can be only  CODE         definitions until blocks 51-53 are loaded.                                                                                      The compiler in blocks 99-103 and the assembler in blocks       111-115 are the ones for the target system's programmer.  These may not be present in target compiled applications except under license from FORTH, Inc.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           This block controls the entire target compilation of the     system, including both standard FORTH features (block 60) and   configuration characteristics:  disk & terminal drivers, amount of memory, number of disk buffers, presence/absence of heads,   etc.  Consult the Target Compiler documentation for a discussionof specific details.                                                                                                               Note especially the  ALLOT  on line 5.  This sets the  RAM   pointer to the start of the system variables area.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              The numbers in parentheses following the definitions in the next   several blocks represent calculated numbers of "clocks" for     the execution of these primitives.  These numbers may be used   for comparative timings.                                                                                                     cell  The run-time code for 16-bit literals in definitions.     byte  The run-time code for 8-bit literals in definitions.                                                                      ':'  A gap left at compile time, into which the target system's    compiler (block 101) will compile the high-level portion of :   Target applications without a compiler may recover this space   by defining   : GAP   DROP ;                                                                                                 colon  The run-time code for all  :  definitions.               EXIT  In addition to its glossary definition,  EXIT  is the        run-time code for  ; .                                          Following the model discussed in the previous block, this    block defines the run-time code for more compiler words, with   GAPs left for the compile-time code to be added later.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             These definitions are the run-time code for the compiler     directives that form structures, whose names are the upper-case equivalents.                                                                                                                       2>R  is the run-time code for  DO .   It is so named and     allowed to keep its head because it is occasionally useful      as a double-length version of  >R .  Its converse,  2R> is      found in Block 15.                                                                                                                 The  HERE  on line 5 marks the beginning of code shared by   loop  and  /loop .  It is the destination for the  BRA  on      line 8.                                                         ( | CODE /loop ( n}  D S PUL  0 R} W LD  W D} W LEA  SWAP BRA)                                                                                                                                                                                                     These simple primitives are excellent examples of FORTH's    assembler usage, especially the use if the  IF ... ELSE ... THENstructure.                                                                                                                         Note, as in the previous block, the use of  HERE  on line    5 to mark an address of code to be shared by both  0=  and  = . The same technique is repeated in  0<  for  <  and  > .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            These memory reference operators provide additional examples of good assembler practice.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        The stack operators in this block deal with 16-bit cells.    They offer excellent examples of stack manipulation in code.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       The words 1+, 2+, etc. provide more than convenience: they   are both shorter and faster than 1 + , etc., and thus should    always be preferred.                                               Note the shared code here:  the  HERE  on line 5 is the      address for  BRA  on line 6, for example.  Thus the code        field for  1+  points to the second instruction of  2+ .  This  saves 6 bytes of space at no cost in time.                         Also note that  2*  and  2/  use arithmetic shifts, and are  thus signed operations.  2/  differs from  2 /  in terms of     rounding behavior.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 By convention, all arithmetic operators with  MOD  in their  names are unsigned operations;  all others in this block are    signed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         MOVE   moves a string, given source and destination addresses      and count.  All arguments are in bytes;  however, odd           addresses and counts may not be used, as the move is cell-      by-cell for speed.                                                                                                           CMOVE  copies byte for byte from source to destination n bytes.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 Some less common arithmetic operators:                          NEGATE  returns a two's complement                              ABS  returns an absolute value                                  NOT  reverses a truth value.  It is identical to  0=                                                                            MAX  returns the maximum of 2 signed numbers                    MIN     "     "  minimum "  "   "       "                                                                                       0 and 1 are defined as constants.  This provides a shorter         reference during compilation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 A variety of miscellaneous operators:                           FILL  fills a given area of memory with the character on the top   of the stack.                                                ERASE  fills a given area of memory with zeroes.                                                                                'S  returns the address of the top of the stack.  Its use is       discouraged.                                                 ?DUP  duplicates a number if it is non-zero.                       Using  ?DUP IF  makes an  ELSE DROP  phrase unnecessary.                                                                     I'  returns the limit of a LOOP (the 2nd item on the return        stack)                                                       J  returns the index of an outer LOOP (the 3rd item)                                                                            LEAVE  sets the  LOOP  limit to its index.  This will force an    exit at the end of the current iteration.                        The prefix '2' indicates that these operators take           arguments two stack items long.  These may be 32-bit numbers or pairs of 16-bit numbers.  The latter usage justifies the        inclusion of these primitives in the program;  other double-    length operations are loaded by Block 9.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           The  USER  variables for this system are documented in the   Processor Supplement.  Additional  USER  variables are defined  in Block 26.                                                       Most  BACKGROUND  tasks have shorter user areas than TERMINALtasks, usually not extending beyond the first 10 bytes.                                                                                                                                                                                                         GOLDEN holds the dictionary heads used by EMPTY                 The space after  'BUFFER  holds the block# last written.        The space after  'NUMBER  holds the high-order part of the         last number converted.                                       The space after  DISK  is non-zero in case of a disk error.     The space after  PREV  contains the disk buffer status cells.                                                                                                                                   This is the inner loop of the multiprogrammer.                                                                                  WAKE   This code is the instruction stored in the  STATUS  cell    of a user who is ready to become active.  STATUS  normally      contains a  JMP  to the next task in the round robin.        PAUSE   This entry to the multiprogrammer starts by storing        WAKE  in the  STATUS  cell so the task will wake up next time   around the loop.                                             WAIT   This entry point is the normal entry for a task who will    stay 'asleep' until some event (such as an interrupt) awakens   him.                                                         STOP   This provides high-level access to  WAIT .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               GRAB  asserts control of a facility by storing the current         user's  STATUS  address in its facility variable.  If the       facility isn't available (i.e, value non-zero and not this      user)  waits in  PAUSE  until it is available.               GET  calls  GRAB  after one lap around the multiprogrammer.                                                                     RELEASE  releases a specified facility variable by setting it      to zero assuming it belongs to the current user -- does         nothing if it doesn't.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       EXECUTE  takes an address of a FORTH definition (any kind) and     executes it.  On completion, control will pass to the FORTH     word following  EXECUTE .                                    @EXECUTE  performs like  EXECUTE , but takes an address contain-   ing the address of the word to be executed.  If the address     contains 0, branches to the  NEXT  at the end of  TYPE  (i.e.   does nothing)                                                TYPE  stores its arguments in  PTR  and  CTR , respectively, and   executes the routine whose address is in the user's  'TYPE . EXPECT  stores its address argument in  PTR  and its count         (negated) in  CTR , then executes the routine whose address     is in the user's  'EXPECT .                                                                                                  CR ,  PAGE ,  TAB ,  and  MARK  execute CRT-specific routines.  EMIT  outputs a single character.                                                                                               (TYPE)  initiates a TYPE operation to a serial port.  If we are    currently Xoff'd, actual output is delayed indefinitely.                                                                     CANCEL  terminates all activity on a serial port and returns us    to quiescent state.  A good thing to do in  PROMPT .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         <-TYP>  dispenses with a DCD loss by reading the input buffer.                                                                  <TYPE>  handles a normal transmitter ready interrupt.  When the    last output char has been placed in the buffer, awakens the     task, indicates not typing, disables Tx interrupt, and re-      vectors Tx int to loss of DCD handler.                                                                                       <XOFF>  receives unsolicited inputs.  Actual XOFF characters set   xoff'd status and turn off Tx int.  ANY OTHER CHARACTER turns   xoff'd status off and enables Tx int iff transmitting.                                                                       FLG:  t000 000x  t=typing; x=xoff'd.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            These are basic output words plus some orphans:                 HERE  returns the next available byte in the dictionary         PAD  returns the address of a string scratch pad                                                                                COUNT  takes the address of a string (with count in first byte)    and returns the address plus one and count (for TYPE, etc.). BLANK  blanks a string (as  ERASE  zeroes one).                                                                                 ?ANY  is used by  SPACES  to count spaces in bunches of 8       SPACES  types spaces.  A 0 or negative count does nothing.      SPACE  types a space.                                                                                                           HOLD  stores a character in a string indicated by PTR.             It is used during number conversion.  The string actually       runs backwards from PAD.                                                                                                     Number formatting words:                                        DIGIT  converts a digit to ASCII, including the hex offset.                                                                     <#  initialized the string in  PTR .                            #>  discards the number and returns the string address and countSIGN  tests the sign (from the 3rd stack position) and buffers     a minus.                                                     #  buffers a single digit.                                      #S  buffers digits until the number reaches zero.                                                                               (.)  formats a signed integer.                                  .  types a signed integer, with trailing space.                 U.  types an unsigned integer, with trailing space.             U.R  types an unsigned integer, right justified in a field.                                                                     DUMP displays a region of memory, given address and count.      String output words include:                                    ?R@  tests a truth value, if set it returns with an in-line        string address; otherwise it exits from the calling word        (usually ABORT")                                                                                                             ABORT  clears both stacks and goes to  QUIT  for input.                                                                         abort"  is the run-time code for  ABORT" .  Upon error, it types   the word at  HERE , the message, and saves >IN in R# for the    editor.                                                      dot"  is the run-time code for ."  It types the message.                                                                        ABORT"  and  ."  are host definitions that reference the just-     defined run-time code.                                                                                                                                                                       The heart of the interpreter is here:                           SOURCE  locates the input string: BLK zero indicates the message   buffer ( S0 @ ); non-zero the block buffer containing  BLK .                                                                 (WORD)  does the work of extracting a word from the input string   and storing it at HERE 2+ (allowing room for the  LINK  which   will go at  HERE  if the word is compiled) until  CNT expires   If the string is empty, returns a 0 count.  If the string is    of even length, fills out the last byte in the last cell with   a space.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Dictionary search:                                              HASH  selects one of 8 strings based on the first character,       current vocabulary (nibble from stack).  It exits from the      the calling definition when vocabularies are exhausted.                                                                      -FIND  searches the dictionary.  If it finds a match, it returns   the parameter field address, link field address, and 'false'.   Otherwise, it repeats  HASH .                                                                                                Head structure:                                                 +0  Link (points to link)                                       +2  i--n nnnn  Immediate                                        +n  tccc cccc  As needed; t iff truncated.                                     Looks like  t  on +3 also used as SMUDGE.                                                                                                                                        ?DIGIT  increments the address on the stack and then determines   if the character at that location is a valid digit in the cur-  rent  BASE . If the character is valid, a true flag is left on  the stack with the value of that digit underneath.  Otherwise,  only a false flag is left.  For both cases, the address is not  removed.                                                                                                                      (NUMBER)  attempts to convert the string whose address is on the  stack to binary and, if successful, will leave the result on    the stack.  If conversion fails due to illegal characters, an   abort will occur, with an error message echoing the string      followed by "?".                                              NUMBER  executes  (NUMBER)  via the system variable  'NUMBER .                                                                                                                                                                                                  DEPTH  returns the number of cells on the parameter stack          (before it was called).                                      -'  searches the dictionary for the word following in the          input stream.  If found, returns 0 ('false'), with the link     field address and parameter field address beneath.              If not found, returns 'true' and  HERE 2+.                   '  searches the dictionary for the word following in the input     stream, and returns its parameter field address if found;       aborts if not found.                                         INTERPRET  interprets the input stream, executing words found      in the dictionary and converting numbers to binary, until       either the input stream is exhausted or a word is found that    cannot be found or converted.                                RESET  clears the current user's return stack.                                                                                  ...continued on next block.                                     This kludge provides minimal BLOCK support for the RT which has    insufficient RAM left for more than one buffer.                 PREV is unconventional:                                            +0  Nonzero if updated                                          +2  Block number; neg => none.                                                                                            Loaded in lieu of 93, 94 if  nb  is one.                                                                                        4/25/88  Stripped completely down to save space.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                ?ABSENT  checks to see if the block whose number is on the stack   is present in memory.  If the block is present,  ?ABSENT        removes the block number from the stack, and leaves an          address pointing to the beginning of the block buffer, and      then leaves the routine which called  ?ABSENT , by means of     the  ' EXIT BR  on line 4.  If the block is not listed in       PREV  then  ?ABSENT  leaves the block number on the stack,      and exits normally through  NEXT .                           ?UPDATED  finds an old buffer.  If the old buffer has its          update bit set,  ?UPDATED  returns the buffer address with      the block number of the buffer on top.  The block number is     later used by  SECTOR  to write out the data.  If the           buffer's update bit is not set,  ?UPDATED  returns the buffer   address and jumps out of the word which called  ?UPDATED .   PREV points to the most recently accessed block buffer.         PREV 2+  points in turn to each, but skips over  PREV's  buffer.UPDATE  sets the most significant bit of the cell in  PREV         which contains the block number of the most recently accessed   block.                                                       ESTABLISH  makes the oldest buffer ( PREV 2+ ) into the newest     buffer (by putting the pointer into  PREV ) and marks the new   buffer with the block number on the next-to-top of the stack.IDENTIFY  marks the newest buffer (in  PREV ) with the block       number from the stack, with  OFFSET  added.                  BUFFER  takes a block number and frees a buffer, and marks that    buffer with the gien block number, then leaves the address of   the buffer on the stack.                                     BLOCK  takes the block number, then ensures that the block is      present in a buffer, and leaves the address of the buffer.   FLUSH  writes out all  NB  (number of buffers) buffers.         EMPTY-BUFFERS  erases all the block buffers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block contains inner functions for umbilical protocols.                                                                    ><  swaps bytes.                                                                                                                XMIT  is a straight  TYPE  operation over the umbilical.                                                                        RECV  is a straight  EXPECT .                                                                                                   ACK  is basically a straight  KEY  for the umbilical.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           SCREEN  controls use of the umbilical link for a conversation      about a single purpose.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      IMMEDIATE causes the preceding definition to be executed           rather than compiled at compile time.                        [COMPILE]  forces compilation of what would otherwise be an        IMMEDIATE  compiler directive following in the input stream. The above words help the host computer compile the target          system's compiler.                                           COMPILE  compiles the execution address of the following word.  ?ALLOT  increments  H  and returns 'true' if there are fewer       than 250 bytes remaining in the user's dictionary.           ALLOT  increments  H  and aborts if insufficient space remains. , and C, compile the given value into the dictionary.              and increment  H  appropriately.                             ?CELL  returns 'true' if a literal requires a full cell.        ] turns on the compiler which continues compiling until            [ or ; is encountered.                                                                                                       [  leaves compile mode.                                         LITERAL  compiles an 8 or 16-bit literal preceded by the address   of the appropriate run-time code ( cell  or  byte ).         [']  compiles the parameter field address of the following word    as a literal.                                                CFA  converts a link field address to a code field address.     USE  changes the code address of the most recent definition to     the address given.                                           ;code  leaves compile mode, and sets the code address of the       most recent definition to the address following.  This is the   run-time behavior of  ;CODE .                                ;CODE  enables the Target Compiler to create defining words in     the target system whose run-time behavior is code.           DOES>  enables the Target Compiler to create defining words in     the target system whose run-time behavior is high-level.     SMUDGE  toggles the smudge bit in the latest definition.        The words in this block allow the target system to compile         "data items".  The use of these words is documented in the      Reference Manual, Starting FORTH, and other sources.         CREATE  executes the word whose address is in the system           variable  'CREATE .                                          (CREATE)  creates a dictionary entry whose link is at  HERE ,      head and code address following.  The code field is set to      the code at  create  (block 64); other defining words may use   (CREATE)  to construct the head and then substitute other       code addresses.                                              The compile time portions of  CREATE (above),  CONSTANT ,  USER    and  :  are compiled into the gaps left before their code in    blocks 63, 64.                                               VARIABLE  shares the code belonging to  CREATE ;  CVARIABLE is     available only on processors that support odd byte addresses.                                                                This block defines the compiler directives that provide            structures in the target system's compiler.  The use of         FORTH's structure words is described in the Reference Manual    and other sources.                                                                                                           The compiler directives use the compile-time stack to pass the     addresses needed to compile branches.  Depending upon the       processor, relative offsets or absolute addresses may be        compiled; this computer uses the latter.                     Most of the words that perform the run-time action for these       words have the same name, but are in lower-case and headless.   They are defined in Block 65.                                                                                                ( : /LOOP ( a}   COMPILE /loop  , ;   IMMEDIATE)                                                                                                                                                VOCABULARY  defines a word which, when executed, will set          CONTEXT  to the sequence of vocabulary indices given.        The two versions of  VOCABULARY  are for the target system and     the Target Compiler, respectively.  The latter is used to       define the target system's vocabularies  FORTH ,  ASSEMBLER ,   and  EDITOR  (in block 54).                                                                                                  The following definitions are for the target system's compiler. CODE  starts an assembler definition.  The code address is set     to the new definition's parameter field.                     ;CODE  terminates the compile-time portion of a defining word      and commences its run-time portion, which is in code         DOES>  is functionally equivalent to  ;CODE  except that its       run-time behavior is described in high level.                                                                                                                                                EMPTY  resets  H  to the beginning of the task's dictionary        given by  H 2+ .  The system definition chains in  GOLDEN       are used to restore the eight link heads,  CONTEXT  and         CURRENT  in the task's user area .                           STRING  compiles a string terminated by a given ASCII character.ABORT"  compiles an error message which will be displayed          along with the word at  HERE  and the task restarted if the     number on the stack at run-time is 'true'.                   ."  compiles a string which will be displayed at run-time.      HEX  and  DECIMAL  change  BASE  appropriately.                 LOAD  saves the current position in the input stream               and calls  INTERPRET  then restores the input stream pointer.THRU loads a range of block numbers, inclusive.  It compacts       load blocks and encourages sequential arrangement of code.   (  stops interpreting until the delimiter ")" is encountered.   HI  loads the  system extensions from block nine.               /INITIALIZE  initializes the umbilical port and transmits          a 12H command until a character is received back from           the host.                                                                                                                    Builds at the end of  ROM  the interrupt vector table.             For development purposes the interrupt vectors have been        initialized to point to vectors at the end of RAM.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           These tables are used by  START  (next block) to initialize        RAM.  The important tables follow a dummy clock interrupt.   RAM  is the table containing initial values for the system         variables, following the map in block 75.  Note that initial    values are needed only for the first 38 bytes.               OPERATOR  contains the information necessary to initialize the     one task that is active following a boot.  The format of this   table is identical to those constructed by  TERMINAL (blk 24,   25).  It starts with STATUS , S0 , and the output and input     interrupt code.  The next 46 bytes map to the 1st 46 bytes      of the user variable area, as described in block 75.  Note      that the latter part of this table, the following table  ram    and the startup code will be overlaid by the dictionary after   it has been used at boot time.                                                                                                                                                               The order of operations in the startup sequence is:                1. The bootstrap loads the 8K program into memory starting at   0 and jumps to 0.  This address contains a jump to the start-   up code,  POWER-UP .                                                                                                            2.  POWER-UP  initializes registers  U & R to the address of    OPERATOR , clears the processor status, initializes  S , and    sets  I  to the address of  START .  NEXT  will therefore       branch to  START .                                                                                                              3.  START  initializes  GOLDEN , erases  OPERATOR's  user       area and copies initial values into its 1st 46 bytes.  If       the original copy of  GOLDEN  didn't contain 1 (FORTH vocab.)   it is presumed that we are starting from power-up (as opposed   to a boot) and the buffers are cleared.  Finally  WIDTH & SCR   are initialized and  OPERATOR  is greeted and activated.     di  and  ei  provide high level access to interrupt control.                                                                    RELOAD  asserts the RESET line.  All hardware is placed in the     reset state and we execute normal system powerup behaviour.                                                                  >< swaps the bytes in the top stack item.                                                                                       +PIA  takes the address of a 5-byte table:  2-byte port (a/b)      adr, data, control, and direction.  Control MUST have data      access bit set.  Sets desired control mode, then data (which    we now think works), then selects and sets control, and at      the same time reselects data.  Finally reads input port to      turn off IRQ bits and lines, returning advanced table address                                                                uart  is the interrupt routine for operator debugging terminal     I/O.  This is optional and is not used in the working system.PEON  does the actual work of servicing requests from the main     RT box as given us over the 488 link.                        +PEON  starts this process.  In the working system, once this      has been initiated the watchdog is active.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   This block contains the  LOG  code for the target compiler.                                                                     It is optionally loaded on line 4 of Block 45 and produces        a map of the words in the target dictionary with their          addresses.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block loads the assembler that will be resident in the        target system.  It should be omitted from any non-extensible    target application.                                                                                                          WAIT  &  WAKE  are perpetuated into the  TARGET  dictionary        since their  EQU  constructions are solely found in  HOST                                                                    The last RAM address of the assembler is displayed at the end.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This block defines the various addressing modes of the 6809.                                                                    Use of an addressing prefix leaves a constant in the  MODE        assembler variable for intgration into the following op-code.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 This block contains the logic for determination of                the opcode post-byte for indexed addressing modes.                                                                            MR>  resets the  MODE  variable when the assembly process is      completed.                                                                                                                    ?CELL  determines if an offset is a byte or word and compiles      the appropriate number of bytes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              This block contains the defining words which are used to          create op-code definitions.                                                                                                   ALU  creates op-codes whose operands are implicit in the          instruction.                                                  UNI  creates op-codes which have a single operand.              BIN  creates op-codes whose operands specify a source and         destionation.                                                 COND  is used to create opcodes which modify the condition code   register                                                      SWP  defines register transfer and exchange instructions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       This block contains the definitions of most of the opcodes        used by the 6809.                                                                                                             The three tables of  BIN  op-codes are organized by the           commonality of the registers upon which each may act.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         The push and pull instructions are defined in this block          along with the notation necessary to specify pushes and         pulls of multiple registers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  The constants at the beginning of this block specify              the numbers assigned to each of the hardware registers in the   6809.                                                                                                                         The macro  NEXT  is defines here to assemble in-line.                                                                           The constants on lines 9 and 10 define the conditions based       upon which branches are assembled by the subsequent             relationals.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Lengths of name fields are normally identical.  Host machine's     name field is one too BIG if (1) count is even.  Truncation     is no problem whatsoever.                                    Lengths of name fields are identical if the '11 hasn't got a sp    as its last character.  Otherwise, the '11 is one too big.                                                                   EXIT    ( NAME C@ 1 AND  NAME 2- CFA 2- @  0< OR  0= +  T+!)                                                                    FORTH : IMMEDIATE   80 PREVIOUS  NAME 2- CFA 2+ NAME - -              NAME 2- CFA 1- C@  32 = +  T+!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            -PIA  does a fair job of a soft PIA reset.  Both sides' control    regs placed in reset state, direction access; directions set    to zero (input) and control set to data select; zero to data    registers (we now think this works.)  Leaves data selected.                                                                  ($  DENOTES A BLOCK LOADED ELSEWHERE!  WATCH OUT!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This load block constructs an RT system with CRT option.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        PROM  performs a RELOAD clear back to the installed PROM's.                                                                     D~  compares the first number nondestructively with the second,    returning a dirty true if the numbers differ.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Stimulus cells are used to post events for tasks that might be     off doing something else.  MSB is zero if task is busy, in      which case LSB records events.  Otherwise the filing of an      event awakens the task whose STATUS addr is in the Stimulus     and zeros the Stimulus.  Interrupt code may post Stimuli.                                                                    AWAIT  returns when the Stimulus has been or is next posted.    AWAKEN  posts an event in a Stimulus.                                                                                           VECTOR  defines a zero relative  @EXECUTE  vector.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              'PG  holds the address of the current PGS table entry.          #CHAN  is the 1-RELATIVE channel number being looked at with the   front panel.  Zero means "system" channel.  #CHAN 2+  shows     what SORT of thing we are looking at:  0 System, 1 Telem,       2 Status, 3 Command.  #CHAN 4+  holds the encoded alarm no.     we last found via  SEARCH  or  ACK .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         ac  and  ov  are accumulator and overflow flag for floating ari.SL#  current 1-rel slave #; 'SL addresses its SLAVES table.     LLNG  total length of active LATEST table.                      ireg  tracks value in 8010, instrumentation reg.                slo  mask for polls to implement slow polling.                  ros  last masked POLL result;  +ros  is unmasked version.       known  and  -known  poll masks for such boxes last config cycle.cok, tmp  local temps for 488 stuff                             err  holds error status following a receive operation.          OVAL  is nonzero if we've accepted good output data from master.tik  countdown for 10 Hz stim;  2tk for 5;  TIK  awakened at 10 ntik  is line freq Hz /10.                                      ?MNT  ?SET  ?LIM  ?RAI  ?LOW  1st byte FF if corresp led on,       2nd byte mask of bits needed to turn led on.                 ^b  and  ^-b  after call to  )bit  contain addrs in  BITS  for     masks used when turning on and off bits in a bitarray.       DTBL , ATBL , and PTBL  hold flags and retained data for digital   analog and parallel inputs.  ORDER DEPENDENT!                                                                                CMDS  receives data broadcasts from master.                     ONE and TWO  alternate as data response buffers.  Absolute max     size 594; practical limit, 12 boards is 386.  Left at abs max   size so we can force a single box to generate max traffic.                                                                   CVEC  transmits configuration to master, and                    AVEC  receives commands & config confirmations.  See below.        SEE 850 FOR SHAPE OF  CVEC .                                                                                                 #ACK  contains count of on bits in  NACK .                      NACK  bit array of alarms needing acknowledgement (0-32 system;    32-288 status in; 288-592 telem.)                                                                                            SLAVES  holds current status for each box (0=RT).  Fields are:    +0  1  Current operational status.                              +1  2  Expected response length +1.                             +3  2  Address in LATEST where response begins.                 +5  1  Flag, says box has  n  redundant boards.                 +6 10  (UNDEFINED) management counts                                                                                          LATEST  holds the most recent good data in messages from each      box, in box number order.  SLAVES provides starting addrs.   HAS  parallels CVEC but has box numbers, -1 for absent boards.  TELEM  database for real, parallel, derived inputs (30 or 34)      Tpara  address of start of parallel entries                     Tcalc  address of start of derived entries                   pTLM  is data followed by slave status pointers for analogs and    parallels.  pSTS  has the same for status in boards.  pCMD      has only status pointers for command out boards.             These bit arrays mechanize the Status management state machine: s.raw  is raw input from boards.                                ?INV  inverts the raw input.                                    s.prv  is inverse of inverted input from last cycle.            ?LCH  enables latched mode.  s.lch  holds latched value.        new  is direct or latched new value.                            ?FLSH  enables flashed display mode.  s.flsh indicates flashing.S.SAY  is what we will display next modified by flash.          dif  enables  ris  and  fal  indicating edge detection.         ?FEEN  and  ?REEN  enable falling & rising edge events.         S.EV  indicates an event has just occurred.                     ?FAEN  and  ?RAEN  enable falling & rising edge alarms.         S.+A  indicates an alarm has just occurred; S.ALM  says in alarmS.IS  is new processed chan state.  S.REV forces alarm re-eval. S.-MU  is true if alarms NOT muted for this channel.            S.OK  true if data current; S.?OK true if any data ever rcvd.   E.ofs  holds 128 16-bit offset values used for the first 128       telemetry channels only.  The rest use zero.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 HIS  takes a task address and user variable and returns the        address of that user variable for the task.                     Ex.:  TYPIST CTR HIS  (returns the addr. of  TYPIST's  CTR ) BACKGROUND  sets up the task definition table for a non-terminal   task, given user area size and stack sizes.  Table is:             +0  User area address   +2  Size of same, bytes                 +4  S0                  +6  HERE                          BUILD  takes the address of a table set up by  BACKGROUND  and     builds the task's user area in RAM.                          ACTIVATE  starts a task whose address is given executing the       remainder of the colon definition in which  ACTIVATE  occurs.   Note that this code must end in  STOP ,  QUIT (for a terminal   task) or in an infinite loop.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                FPAN  is AWAKENed by receipt of keys and other things that are     of potential interest to the front panel manager.            key  if nonzero is the most recent key value seen.              rpt  if nonzero is a key value being repeated.  New characters     are dragged in at 60 Hz until we see something that differs     from  rpt  at which time it gets zeroed and we awaken FPAN      with a new and probably bogus stimulus.                      NI  at the moment counts keyboard interrupts.                                                                                   kbd  handles keyboard interrupts from the front panel.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          This clock interrupt maintains TICKS at the line frequency.  It    also divides the clock down to 10 Hz at which frequency TIK     is awakened and the alarm LED flashes, and to 5 Hz at which     the cursors flash.                                                                                                           Simulates keyboard interrupts at the line frequency whenever we    are in repeat key (trailing edge sense) mode.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                For network channels:  We check each channel's timeout slot.       If zero, do nothing.  Otherwise count down and if zero we       need to simulate a termination.  This entails loading up W      and simulating an FIRQ type interrupt to the common timeout     handler.  Implies that network interrupts MUST be loaded be-    fore this one!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               BBUF  "big buffers" for async operations.  One per network line.MASTBL  one table per master for funneling and state control.   MBUF  one complete menu table per master.                       DBUF  one main data buffer for each master.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     Various places in this application we need to run a loop through   a bit array, stopping on each 1-bit.  These words provide an    efficient way to manage such loops.                                                                                          <bt  is given the starting address and byte length of a bitarray   and returns stack args for the loop:  starting address, end     test address, a flag that's true if the last time around we     found a bit, the bit number found last time around, and the     address of next byte to test (same if found a bit last time).                                                                ?bit  starts scanning wherever we left off.  If it finds another   bit set, updates stack params and returns index of set bit.     Otherwise discards all of this nonsense and exits calling       definition.  Usage to list bits in an array:                       ...  a n <bt  BEGIN  ?bit .  AGAIN ;  RECOVER                                                                             +IP  and  -IP  set and clear given bits in the instrumentation     register.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block supports CRC calculations using an arbitrary poly-      nomial "Poly" that is bitreversed to account for transmission   order.  It is driven by the  crc  table which contains the      cumulative 16-bit xor'ative effects due to a given byte value   regardless of whether it was the low order byte of the exist-   ing remainder or the new byte being shifted in.              !CRC  initializes this table.                                                                                                   CRC  is an example of use.  Given prior remainder and a byte       to be merged, returns updated remainder.                                                                                     This method precisely tracks that used in Bisync.  If the crc of   the text of a message is transmitted low byte first, then the   same algorithm when applied to the entire message including     crc bytes will return zero (or whatever remainder initialized   to be.)                                                      FP@  and  FP!  are interim fetches and stores to the panel bus.   08 FP@  gives s2s1;  10 FP@  gives  xxs3.  Not as drawn!                                                                      9/10 Changed initialization of U2 so that we are default set for   output.  This to speed up what we do most of the time.                                                                       +TST  and  -TST  set and clear test mode.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Each of the primary state variables is logically connected to a    front panel LED.  These state variables are subject to change   from within CODE definitions.  Hence it is justifiable to go    to some lengths to make it convenient to switch them.           Each state variable has a value FF for "on" and 00 for "off".   This is followed by a mask of the bits that need to be set to   zero in order to make the proper LED(s) turn ON.                                                                             +sta  and  -sta  force the value of the given state variable.                                                                   STA  forces the given value into a state variable.                                                                              +ALM  and  -ALM  turn the alarm flasher and horn on and off,       respectively.  Direct control of these is not intended; the     usual method is instead to go thru  +ACK  and  -ACK .                                                                        Front Panel has two rows of 24 characters, numbered EF thru D8     and D7 thru C0.                                              CP  keeps track of where we are on the front panel.                                                                             OLD  holds our old terminal personality vectors while we're        interacting with the front panel.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            This code supports the MM58167 clock/calendar chip with no atten   tion to leap years since chip won't even let us talk about 29   February.  Also, years do not increment but are remembered in   chip RAM across power failures.                                                                                              YEAR  contains full current year in binary.                     Bcd  converts 2 BCD digits to binary.  Bin does the opposite.                                                                   !day  given mmdd in BCD sets same in chip.  !DAY takes binary.  !NOW  sets clock given time in 2-second units.                                                                                  @NOW  sets system clock and year from the chip.                                                                                 @DAY  returns the BCD date from the chip without protection.                                                                                                                                    .DAY  formats a BCD date, including the year from chip RAM.                                                                     .TIME  formats time in 2-sec units as  hh:mm:ss .                                                                               HHMM  sets the time given decimal hhmm .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        E@  and  E!  support RT internal style engineering variables,      where 2 bytes of value precede one byte of flag.             V@  fetches a datum as collected over the network, differing       only in that the flag byte PRECEDES the 2-byte value.                                                                        /-/  is a positive circular difference in minutes/day space.                                                                    /hm  converts hhmm to minutes and saves at a given PAD offset.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  EROM  is a facility variable that controls the EAROM board(s).  The interrupt occurs when either short (write) or long (erase)     timers expire.  A dummy read is performed if a write was in     progress and the task owning EROM is awakened.               1ms  fires the 1.5ms oneshot, used in waiting for Vgg off.                                                                      <rom  is the inner loop of the EAROM string fetch operator.     set  initializes pointers for  >rom  and turns on Vgg supply.   -ad  backs the ROM address pointer up one for erase termination.-gg  turns off the -30v Vgg supply to "protect" ROM.                                                                            ROM  data are 1's when erased, so we INVERT these data going to    and coming from the boards; thus it looks zero when cleared.    Resident memory goes from E000 to E800.  The first expansion    board goes from 0 to 2000 and so on up to E000.                                                                              >rom  is the inner loop used to store into EAROM.  Owing to the    extremely long delays required for actual writes (1 ms) or      erase and write (10 ms), this routine is somewhat complex to    avoid delays whenever possible.  We stay in the loop at the     top, comparing stored data with ROM, until we exhaust the       string (NEXT) or encounter a difference.  We test whether the   destination is erased (all 1s).  If so we only need to write:   Set data dir & val, func, CE_, WE_, WE, CE & func, data dir.    Otherwise we note to back up the char pointer and erase word:   Set func, CE_, CE.  For either of these lengthy guys, we then   start the proper timer on the ROM board, back up I, and wait    for the timer to finish.  50uS/byte no change; 1.516mS write    atop 0; 11.816mS set to 0; 13.300mS change nonzero.                                                                                                                                                                                                          set  and  kill  are inner mechanisms.                                                                                           <ROM  fetches a string of bytes from EAROM to main memory.  The    count MUST be nonzero!  50 uS/byte in good quantity.         >ROM  stores a string from main memory to EAROM.  Time varies      depending on source and destination content.  See above.     Kill  does a block erase of the 1k-byte hunk of EAROM in which     the given address lies.  Takes 12 mS instead of 12 sec.                                                                      R@  and  R!  are cell fetch and store operators for EAROM;      RC@  and  RC!  are byte operations.  None are very fast.                                                                        -ROM  is true if the given RAM and EAROM strings differ.  Note     args same as for store.                                      RDUMP  dumps EAROM contents.                                                                                                    Each BOSS is allowed to transact one command at a time.  The 100   ms main cycle serves work on one command for each boss every    cycle.  Boss 0 is FPAN; 1 is currently controlling CT; 2 is     the MDC; and 3 is CRT.                                       C.MD  holds mode and pulse width for each command line.         C.TIM  nonzero indicates command activity exists for this boss.    Negative means do it indefinitely.  Positive means remaining    number of .1s ticks during which action needs to be taken.   C.NO  is the zero relative command line number involved.        C.ACT  and  C.RST  are the "active" state to which GPIB must set   the command line on all but the last cycle and the "rest"       state established on the last cycle.                         C.FBK  is incremented for each successful command transmission. C.INC  indicates whether the FRONT PANEL is requesting increase.CM@  returns a command output in pseudoengineering form.                                                                        +CMD  given increase flag, command line number, and boss number,   schedules a command if the boss is not presently busy.  For     latched commands, active and rest are set to increase flag,     and time is set to 2 ticks for 100 ms effective time.  For      momentary, active is ON, reset is OFF, and time is infinite.    For pulsed, active is ON, reset is OFF, and time is specified   pulse width plus 1 tick.  Returns with data base armed, feed-   back count zeroed, and C.INC set if boss is the front panel.                                                                 -MOM  is used to terminate a momentary closure for the given       boss iff such a closure is currently happening.                                                                              ?BZY  is nonzero if there is any command currently in operation    for the given boss.                                                                                                                                                                          ?CMD  is used in GPIB just before transmitting commands each .1s   cycle.  For each boss, it checks C.TIM and if nonzero pushes    args for  .!  onto the stack to establish desired state of      the command line.  Negative C.TIM is left alone, otherwise it   is decremented; if result nonzero, we establish the "active"    state otherwise the "rest" state.  We then check board status   and increment  C.FBK  if it's good.  Finally, if working on     boss zero (front panel), we select the RAISE or LOWER led and   turn same ON if board status good and decremented count still   nonzero; otherwise we turn it OFF.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           Network commands are placed in bSTIM by interrupt code and are     taken out here in the 10 Hz cycle of GPIB.                                                                                   If op has not changed, we do nothing.  Otherwise, we turn off      momentary operation and clear the A bit of bCUR.  If there      is a command still in progress for some CT, we proceed no       farther than this.  Then, if anything other than the A-bit      has changed, we zero the C.FBK byte to avoid returning any      inappropriate feedback.  Now, if A-bit is turning on, we        compose and push arguments for command initiation.                                                                           It is here that we limit residual commands that have survived      a major communications failure (loss of any normal polling of   our box by the master CT) to two seconds of effectiveness.      If this were not done it would be possible to "stick" a mom-    entary command in an active state.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           +SA  here is used by the CT.                                                                                                    )Q  indexes QUE for current logger given byte offset.                                                                           +Q  updates qPOP and qS# (i=0) or qA# (i=4) by  n .                                                                             >Q  files an internal alarm entry in all logger queues.  Merging   with events from RT's is managed by separate population count   arrays  qS#  and  qA#  with the rule that only 16 items of      each kind may exist in each queue.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           #ACK  contains count of on bits in  NACK .                      NACK  bit array of alarms needing acknowledgement.                                                                              )bit  subroutine which, given bit# and bitarray addr, returns      with W set to address in bitarray where bit is, and  ^b  and    ^-b  set to address in  BITS  of masks used to set and clear..@  given bit number in bitarray, returns clean true/false.     .!  sets or resets given bit# in bitarray.                                                                                      +ACK  sets need for acknowledgement of the given alarm.  #ACK is   maintained and the system wide alarm indicator is turned on     when 0-1 transition occurs.                                  -ACK  notes that the given alarm is acknowledged.  #ACK and the    alarm indicator are maintained.                                                                                                                                                              Event queue contains 16 6-byte items:                              +0  mmdd  BCD date                                              +2  time  In 2-sec units past midnight                          +4  mesc ccii iiii iiii   Missing, event, sts, code, ID.                                                                     qSTO  offset in buffer of next to store.  Resets at 96.         lgc  count in buffer for local logger.  Sticks at queue size+1. lmx  count since boot.  All counts stick like lgc.                                                                              +que  internal to +QUE, actually files the item.  Something-new    flag is set for all CT's.  Four logger counts per CT as well    as local logger and since-boot counts are maintained.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        +QUE  files an item given its (+4) field.                                                                                       +SA  is moved down here so that the onset of system alarms puts    an event into the queue.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     eval  evaluates the status-in state machine in parallel for the    group of eight inputs whose index is in  W .  Ground rules      that permit parallel evaluation are that all logic must be      expressed in combinatorial terms with a memory model composed   of latches clocked in a defined order by a multi phase clock.   The current logic model employs 8 input variables, 8 latches,   and 22 logic terms on each channel with a 4-phase clock.  One   of the nicer things about this model is that, in addition to    being executed in parallel, changes in functional variables     are handled neatly and with no peculiar side effects such as    stuck latches and special cases in updating.  Takes 35.5 uS     per channel, or 9 mS for all 256.                                                                                            Assumes that raw input is updated iff good data are coming at us   otherwise we use old data every cycle!                                                                                       ESTS  evaluates status in state machines for all channels.  Done   by  GPIB  during main cycle after sucking new data.                                                                          ST@  returns the given status channel in pseudo engineering fmt.   Value is clean truth state.  Value status has  ok , alm , and   perhaps later muting state info.  SINCE THIS IS ONLY USED FOR   DISPLAY, we get the channel's state from  S.SAY  and turn off   the  s.flsh  latch to indicate that the value has been seen.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 -SM  is true if the given status channel is not muted.  Notices    changes in muting state.  If changed, updates S.-MU; if new     state is muted, turns off S.ALM.  If new state unmuted, sets    S.REV to force re-evaluation next GPIB cycle.                                                                                Sid  constructs an alarm-type event queue item for the given       status channel.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              -mas  is a subroutine that resets the master 488 chip by placing   it in reset, setting its address pair to (0,1), releasing its   reset, enabling automatic command handling, and setting the     interrupt mask to set IRQ on EOI but produce no interrupt.   -talk  removes the 488 chip from TACS if we find it there.  This   MUST be done before taking control to set buffer direction.  -MAS  and  -TALK  provide high level access to these.                                                                           u100  spins for >100 uS.                                        TCA  takes control asynchronously.  Does what we hope is a com-    plete interface clear, returning with ATN_ asserted and our     controller data drivers disabled.                            TCS  takes control synchronously (normal operation).  Returns w/   ATN_ asserted and controller data drivers unchanged.  If we     are to support third party transfers this is not truly synch-   ronous takeover and will need to spin and time out.          <C  enables data bus drivers for command output.  Assumes that     we have previously taken control and untalked our own chip!     The flag  cok  is set, indicating no errors.                 C>  completes a command sequence by disabling data bus drivers     and deasserting ATN_.  Returns  true  if there were no time-    out errors.                                                  sh  performs controller source handshake for the byte in  B .      Uses D.  Does nothing if  cok  notes preceding timeout; also    clears  cok  if a timeout happened.                          SH  provides high level access for pitching one command byte.   JERK  initializes the bus, either after boot or to recover from    timeouts or other anomalies with a slave.  Takes control asy-   nchronously and sends a Device Clear command.  Slaves respond   to this command by reinitializing their interfaces.  The spin   following all DAC's covers the period during which glitches     are emitted from chips being initialized.  Errors are ignoredSPIN  is the slow readiness test loop that includes timeout.       Returns indicators zero if the slave timed out.                                                                              SND  transmits a string of bytes followed by checksum with EOI.    String must be nonzero length.  Returns nonzero if the oper-    ation completed successfully.  Failure implies need to reset    the bus before the next attempted operation of any kind!                                                                     ----------------------------------------------------------------   WARNING:  This is a carefully tuned algorithm on both ends.     Modify at your peril!  Any significant change in CPU clock      rates will require retuning.                                 ----------------------------------------------------------------                                                                                                                                                                                                RCV  receives a string of bytes given destination address and      max length +1 from the current talker.  Returns the number of   bytes received if good, or zero if bad.  Actual error status    is available in  err .                                       err  is nonzero for RCV errors:  1=overrun, 2=checksum, 3=tmot.                                                                 ----------------------------------------------------------------   WARNING:  This is a carefully tuned algorithm on both ends.     Modify at your peril!  Any significant change in CPU clock      rates will require retuning.                                 ----------------------------------------------------------------                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                SLAVE  selects a slave whose 1-relative box number is given.       The RT itself is box 0.                                                                                                      SAY  transmits a string on the given channel to the currently      selected slave exclusively.  True if successful.                                                                             HEAR  requests a string of up to but not including the given       length from the given channel (0/1) of the currently selected   slave.  Buffers must reserve the extra byte at the end for      overrun.  Returns length of message or zero if in error.        The rules permit only one inbound channel per slave after       each outbound message or broadcast.                                                                                          ENCORE  sends the undefined command 1F which causes ALL slaves     to enable a second inbound message in this case, then gets an   inbound message as does  HEAR .                              POLL  conducts a parallel poll from normal rest state.  Actual     roster of parties responding is left in  +ros .  This is then   masked by  slo  and the returned value, also left in  ros ,     is the roster of parties with which we will actually do bus-    iness.  This mechanism is required so that we can survive an    unruly box on the bus.                                                                                                       BCST  transmits a string of bytes to all existing slaves on        the given channel.                                                                                                           DOG  instructs all slave interrupt routines to stimulate their     watchdogs if such are enabled.  This is not something I want    to have here; we only need to do this so that we can keep the   expansion boxes alive while configuring.  It compromises the    robustness of the system & with longer watchdog timeouts it     would be unnecessary.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        These strings are used often enough to justify naming them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     HAS  parallels  CVEC  except that flag bytes are FF for absent     boards and otherwise the flags are box numbers.              rflg  and  rtmp  are local scratch for  RECONCILE .                                                                             RECONCILE  turns a CVEC received from the current box into AVEC    to send back.  The response is transformed by comparing the     configuration claimed by the box with that accumulated thus     far in HAS.  Boards not heard about thus far become property    of the current box and are marked as such.  In any case where   the current box has a board already owned by a lower numbered   box, the current box is instructed to ignore its existence      and the redundant board flag is set in SLAVES for the current   box.  Upon completion, AVEC is ready to be sent back to the     current box.                                                                                                                 -had  is true if current configuration differs from the saved.  SLAM  sets our active input board management tables to reflect     the configuration that was filtered for us by  RECONCILE .                                                                   ENUM  returns the actual message length + 1 expected from the      box whose reconciled configuration lies in  AVEC .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           PZZ  starts out filled w/ addresses from  LATEST  and  SLAVES      for all slaves. It is used by  >ptrs  to keep track of          those addresses while it populates pointer tables  pTLM ,       pCMD , and  pSTS .                                           Th  is given an address in  HAS  and a count. If the entry in      HAS  contains 255, it returns the address of a dummy byte       at the end of PZZ and 0; otherwise it uses the box number       in  HAS  to return the address in LATEST where data from the    subject board are found, bumps the entry in  PZZ  by the        given count, and also returns the address of this slave's       SLAVES  table entry (box status).                            >ptrs  populates pointer tables  pTLM , pCMD , and  pSTS  with     addresses in  LATEST  where data from these channels are        found, and addresses of Slave statuses.                                                                                                                                                      CFG  completely reconfigures the system by polling all unmasked    parallel poll respondents for their configurations.  Upon com   pletion, HAS reflects board assignments;  known  and  -known    list the boxes we polled; SLAVES has been set with approp.      length and LATEST addresses for all possible boxes; redundant   boards have been counted in SLAVES for all responding boxes;    SLAVES status byte has been set for all boxes; and "true" is    returned if we got good configurations from all boxes that we   polled.                                                                                                                      What is still missing here is a supervisory mechanism to scan      the SLAVES table and maintain slow-poll counts for boxes that   seem to be giving us trouble.  For now we leave this out.       Syndrome is real; just turn on a slave, climb in, say  di and   STOP.  Shazam, we start getting crapouts from configuration.                                                                 +IP  and  -IP  set and clear given bits in the instrumentation     register.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Missing things:                                                   1.  Do something about broadcast errors.                        2.  Count input errs for slow poll.                             3.  PAUSE here and there>                                       4.  Skip interrogation of device classes we lack.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             This load block constructs an RT system with Logger option.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     24-bit floating format exists in packed and unpacked forms:        eeeeeees mmmmmmmm mmmmmmmm   unpacked 0000000s eeeeeeee         Zero value indicated by number all zero, detected by zero       mantissa.  Mantissa always normalized to its MSB for full 16.   Exponent biased to look 2's complement in whatever extension.   Overflow implied by any positive exponent >63.  All floating    numbers on the stack are unpacked.  Typically we use packed     form for values saved in data bases.                                                                                         N>F  converts a signed integer to 24-bit floating unpacked.        Conversion is exact.                                         F>N  converts an unpacked floating to 16-bit integer.  Values      less than 1 are set to zero.  ANY OVERFLOW is set to 32767 or   32768 depending on sign of the value.  Note that both 32767     and 32768 are considered overflow values, although  ov  isn't   set when we compute a LEGITIMATE value of 32767.             F*  multiplies two unpacked floating numbers, returning a normal   ized result and a clean zero when indicated.  Does not moni-    tor for exponent over or underflow; assumes that we perform     too few operations to ever see this.  No rounding is done       because of the normalization difficulties that it leads to.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  F/  divides two unpacked floating numbers, returning a normal-     ized result.  Divide by zero returns a badly normalized over-   flow with correct sign if dividend was nonzero, or a zero       mantissa with overflow exponent for 0/0.  Dividend of zero      returns a clean zero.  Otherwise the divide proceeds without    rounding, producing a 17-bit quotient.  Divisor loses one bit   due to prescaling.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           )ENG  takes a 0-relative engineering channel number, returning     channel no. in  #CH  and  the address of its  TELEM  entry in   'ENG .  The  LATEST  data are returned under a flag indicat-    ing that the data are current.  Non-current data is garbage.    Derived variables are always indicated valid.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                ZONE  returns limit zone number for the given engineering value.   Zero forced if chan UNCAL, has no LMSEX, sys wide limits dis-   abled, or has no value.  MUTE forced if mute status line's      fully processed current state is OFF (0).  Table checking is    optimized for speed, searching outward from center.                # ZONE            SLOT                                          8 H Red                                                         6 Reset H Red     19 HR - HRr                                   4 H Yel           17 HRr - HY                                   2 Reset H Yel     15 HY - HYr                                   0 Normal          13 HYr - LYr                                 10 Reset Low Yel   05 LYr                                       12 Low Yel         11 LYr - LY                                  14 Reset Low Red   09 LY - LRr                                  16 Low Red         07 LRr - LR                                  18 Muted                                                   STEP  given a new limit zone number, does three things:  Using     the first nibble in the table, exercises the limit condition    state machine to come up with the new limit mark for the eng    value and sets it; determines whether the channel should be     in alarm and so marks it; and finds out whether we have had     either a transition in channel alarmed status or an instant-    aneous limit transition that passed through the event mask      just as we were watching.  If either of these things happens,   we return with a control flag.  Otherwise caller is exited.     The control flag is  x00eyraa  aa nonzero => alarm on; x nz     => alarm off; eyr event flags.                               The table entry is  0000 0sss v00e yrYR  where s is new limit      state, eyr indicate instantaneous reset/yel/red transitions,    YR indicate continuous limit exceedence.  e may be on along     with y or r, in which case the y or r takes precedence.         v indicates severity increase for ack on y->r transitions.   E@  fetches an engineering variable onto the stack with value      underneath value status byte.                                V@  fetches a datum as collected over the network, differing       only in that the flag byte PRECEDES the 2-byte value.                                                                        ?ALARMS  is given the result from  STEP  above.  Events are        filed for both events and alarms.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            Floating scale factors are kept in TELEM in packed form.  These    words are used during execution and rarely during calibration   to transform packed forms to and from stack based floating.                                                                  SCALE  returns the scale factor from the currently selected        telemetry channel.                                           !SCALE  sets the current channel's scale factor in RAM.                                                                         +of  adds the negated offset to the given raw value.  Overflow     can only happen when the value was A-D clipped, in which case   it is simply not changed.                                                                                                    sLIN  multiplies its argument by the scale factor.              sPWR  multiplies the square of its argument by the scale factor.                                                                                                                                ?LIMITS  is a temporary kludge version of the word that takes a    new engineering value, stores it, and limit checks it with      alarm detection and handling.                                                                                                ?an  returns raw value and conversion vector numbers for any       engineering variable.  Sets default decimal places into the     value status; detects A/D saturation and forces the CLIP code   of 7 in decimals in that case.                                                                                               eVEC  performs one of the 8 telemetry and 8 derived unit conver-   sions we have defined:  Uncal, mV, linear, power, ..., raw;     Uncal, product, ratio, ..., raw.                                                                                             >ENG  performs engineering unit conversion and limit checking      for the currently selected channel.                                                                                          ?new  considers whether we need to do anything about the channel   we are looking at.  Exits the caller with no action if this     channel is not configured.  Returns to caller with no action    if new data absent, OR if this channel was previously ok and    raw data are unchanged and this is not a derived measure.       In all other cases, marks the channel ok, updates previous      raw value, and BRANCHES to the engineering conversion.                                                                       ENG  returns the address of the given channel's engineering unit   value.  Any new raw data are converted and limit checked.       ~255 uS for an unchanged chan; 1 mS straight linear analog      w/o limit check.                                                                                                             &ENG  is used like  ENG  in background limit checker to force a    limit check regardless of whether the given channel is being    used currently by anyone.                                    pn  returns the current RAW value for the channel whose number     lies at the given offset in the current TELEM table.  The       channel is checked for not OK (turns off our OK), no value      (sets us to no value), and analog saturation (sets us satur-    ated IFF we are not presently "no value".)  Converts the raw    value to floating.  The commented material in  chk  should be   used if we decide to base derived measures on ENGINEERING val   at some later date.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          chn  dismantles a channel descriptor, returning channel within     class under class number (0=command, 1=status, 2=telem).                                                                     ?cm  acquires command status for 1-relative command in low byte    of top argument, shifting "ok" and "on" bits into hi byte of    the bottom argument.                                                                                                         ?cmds  takes a number and a pair of 1-relative command line nos.   in lower|raise order.  Returns 4 bits shifted into low order    of hi byte of the number underneath:                               xxxx KNkn  K,N=ok, "on" for raise; k,n=same for lower.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    !fp  sets fpan data in current MASTBL, setting currency flag iff   channel currently requested is the one we evaluated.  RETURN    STACK DEPTH SENSITIVE, constrained to local use here.                                                                        !un  factors out some overhead to acquire commands from a TELEM    table entry.  Additionally it scarfs current units field for    transmission from MASTBL+24.  Changed 1/02/87; we don't mess    with commands on TELEM, returning units instead.                                                                             fCMD, fSTS, fTLM  acquire data for each class of channel.  Vec     is a transfer vector for reaching them.                                                                                      CTFP  refreshes front panel data for each master requesting it.    Intended for use in GPIB immediately after acquiring new data                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                The required settings for the modem are:                           Switches  udddu duduu  DTR, terse results, no echo, autoans,       DCD on, RJ11, commands, 212A, DTR hangup.  S8=3 sec/comma,      S0=1 ring, S2=no +++.  S0 overrides auto ans switch.      Ctl  returns address of this task's control/status register.    typ  is a high level  TYPE  for poller tasks.                                                                                   ?K  is a flagged  ?KEY .                                        Dry  flushes incoming registers.                                KI  returns a key, with the given timeout in 2-sec units.       SNK  sends a carriage return, expecting the given character in     the given time followed by <CR>.  True if QED.               -MODEM  deasserts DTR.  +MODEM  reasserts it.                   HANGUP  gets the phone hung up and the modem initialized.  Arg     is ASCII zero to disable incoming, 1 to enable same.  In the    RT, we only allow ourselves to try this twice!               COP  is stimulated every two seconds to check for timeouts of      the various network line states.                             >NET  sets current task to use the given line for dialing.      timed  return stack sensitive, returns state*2 under true if the   channel in question has exceeded timeout for the state it's     in.  TO's table of timeouts by state, zero => irrelevant.                                                                       Check & bring forward times.                                    0 (needs init) ignore.                                       ** 1 (idle) reset interval -> 0, notify HANGER.                    2 (dial) ignore.                                             ** 3 (ID protocol) ID interval -> 0, notify HANGER.                    NOTE FAILED CALL OR FAILED ANSWER.                       ** 4 (connected) outbound interval -> 0, notify HANGER.                NOTE SEVERED CALL.                                                                                                       ?to  is true if the elapsed time given exceeds the criterion var   given, unless we are in maintenance mode.                                                                                    Ft0  time master out of comm till Ctl Failsafe Warning alarm.   Ft1  time " till Control Failsafe alarm & relay.                Ft2  time everyone out of comm before alarms trigger dialing.                                                                   NOTE:  Possible unsafety here with management of state flag.       Interaction, if any, would be with the DIALER task.             Investigate when time available.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             Telemetry timeout support using delay control board.            T.A thru D are 1-rel analog channel nos. in use for timeouts.   T.M  is 1-rel muting channel number.                                                                                            T.MO  is max unhappiness time in 2-sec units.                   T.RST  is set nz whenever ?MNT state changes.                   T.KS  is 2-sec freerunning time at which we were last happy with   each timeout slot.                                                                                                           System alarms 8 thru 13 correspond to telem failsafes 1-6.         The alarm is turned on with normal edge detection after the     first minute into the timeout period.  It is toggled to force   a second acknowledgement when the timeout period has fully      expired.                                                                                                                                                                                     TT  is executed periodically by LIMMER to check six timeouts.                                                                   TCH  checks one.  Rules are:  Timeouts effective only when maint   mode not happening.  A timeout is happy if muted or if all of   the mentioned channels are OK, have values, and raw data are    not within +/- 250mv of ground.  IMPORTANT:  When the RT re-    boots, all of these suckers MUST be considered timed out!  If   we EVER see happiness, THEN & ONLY THEN do they get 3 hrs!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   <Du , >Du , and -Du  are search, search/insert, and delete for     the currently selected RT+A/B combination.                   @No  returns dial string from the current data base item.       DIAL  entered with RT#, FR#, A/B set for the site to dial.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      Normal case:                                                      Boards 0 is cool                                                Switches match board 0 record of number                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       NUKE  zeros an entire EAROM board.                                                                                              !CML  initializes default comm line settings.  Txinit 3; txrst 2   Rxtot 83 decimal; Lead 3, trail 2.  Appropriate for 1200 baud   half duplex 2-wire fully switched.  This should be an unob-     trusive setting for a fresh box coming up on an installed net                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                'TCON  returns the EAROM address of the current telemetry chan,    atop its TOTAL table length.                                 'TK  is a semi kludge for release 3 to add handling of offset      values without requiring data base reorganization.  Entire      kludge to this effect is contained here and in block 131 that   allocates space in EAROM.  Cleanup possible in the future.                                                                   >TLM  stores everything for a telemetry channel except limits.  <TLM  initializes everything for the channel except limits.                                                                     <STS  and  >STS  fetch and store status mode configuration data    for all channels from/to EAROM.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              E.CM  indexed by line*6:  TxI|TxR RxT|Dial Spad|Epad                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            HRAY  defines a cell array at the given byte offset from HERE.  +PAD  contains engineering variable format values for 8 limits     (half of which are holes), with existence+overflow-changed      flags at offset +3.  Low thru high, no nonsense.             L.RAW  contains limit values in eng units, overflows sign clip-    ped, with nonex vals set to hi or lo infinity depending on      side.  Low thru high, no nonsense.  UNSIGNED.                L.WORK  contains limit values being massaged, same as L.RAW.    ?EXIST  is true if ANY limits are found to exist for this chan.    after unpack and scaling.  Maintains LMSEX bit.              DOWN  performs minning function on all upper limits.            UP  performs maxing function on ALL limits from low to high.       Guarantees entire table is in ascending order.               Chgd  maintains flags by incrementing those whose values did NOT   get changed.  It then derives the alarm flags attached to the   limit values' status bytes.                                  This block supports the packed form of the limit table in ROM.     A bit of a pain, but we save about 600 bytes in ROM this way.                                                                <LIMS  unpacks ROM limits for the current eng var into +PAD.       +PAD entries are  <eng val><flg>  order LR,xx,LY,xx,HY,xx,      HR,xx,%.  Flags are NOT initalized.                          >LIMS  packs limits in that format and stores them to ROM.                                                                      The data in  +PAD  survive throughout the configuration and lim    edit processes.  They are scaled independently of the channel   itself for documentation purposes.  Existence is flagged by     the decimals field just as it is for normal eng vars.  The      "alarm" bit is maintained by the limit setting mechanism to     reflect a limit that is in EXCEPTION for reasons of overflow,   or having been itself (or its hysteresis point) moved to meet   ordering requirements.                                       Still missing:  scale delta CAN be as large as 10**6.  Oops.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    LSCALE  takes the raw documentary values for limits in  +PAD       and produces the  L.RAW  table.  Flags in  +PAD  are set for    what we find:  0 if lim nonexist, FF if exists, FE if ovflo.    Later we incr the flag if the lim does not change.  Result is   pos if all cool; neg byte value indicates we should * the lim                                                                DELTAS  converts L.WORK into active limits by computing all of     the deltas and inserting the low yellow absolute bias.                                                                       !LIMS  evaluates AND IMPLEMENTS the documentary limit values in    +PAD , leaving flags and alarm bits in  +PAD  set to reflect    problems, LMSEX set to enable or disable checking for the eng   variable, and the limit tables set with proper deltas.                                                                                                                                                                                                       This version of  /DIGIT  is custom for the Moseley box;            it expects a sixteen bit unsigned number, and returns the       least significant digit over the quotient, for making a         character string. A four bit divisor is used, giving a          thirteen bit quotient and a four bit remainder.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              #dec  first byte nonzero if negative number;                       second byte is character posn + 1 of decimal point.             Second cell is save area for address of caller's  PAD .         These four scratch bytes belong in low ram.                                                                                  This subroutine is a ripoff of the fast  /DIGIT  that expects      a 16 bit number and returns the least significant digit.        While  /DIGIT  uses the caller's  BASE , this version           always uses base 10 (for speed). It expects Dividend in D;      returns Remainder in A and Quotient in W. Does not use I.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    CVT  takes address of an engineering variable, where we            pick up 16 bits of signed binary and bits 3-5 of third byte     for decimal position, and we lay down seven characters of       the number in base ten with sign and decimal point,             right justified with blank fill. It assumes  PTR  is set up,    and it leaves it updated.                                    The fragment before the code definition is branched to when        we detect one of the conditions that makes us put out seven     characters (blank, hyphens, low or high rails), followed by     a common exit point.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         M.SD  hi byte RT number or FF; low byte board number or 00.     M.DC  hi byte RT number * 4 + raise, or FF; low byte cmd chan.                                                                  M.ILOC  contains address of an  S.IS  cell for each of the 16      possible MSD rows.  M.ALOC  is the same for  S.ALM  cells.   ZRO  is a predictably zero cell in low memory.  Must be less       than 256.  Used for null pointers in  M.ILOC  and  M.ALOC .  M.IS  and  M.alm  contain most recent values we sent to each MSDM.CX  is nonzero for each MDC box that exists.                                                                                  msd , mdc , !md  are initialized message buffers.                                                                               mHIT  is latest keycode from int code;  mTO is its timeout.     mLAST  is keycode being worked on if M.BACT is positive, holding   MDC box number involved so we inhibit refreshes to it.       MDCS  is awakened to indicate new data or key activity.         mST  is current state index into mSV.                                                                                           mi  receives chars.  UART errors go to  mu  which resets state 0   Otherwise vector to appropriate state:                                                                                            0  Looking for 01 ->0; else stay here.                          2  Looking for 30 ->4; else ->0.                                4  Looking for board 3x ->6, save x in mTMP; else ->0.          6  Looking for key 3k ->8, save k in mTMP; else ->0.            8  Looking for 04; ->0 either case.  If good, mTMP -> mHIT,        AWAKEN MDCS, set mTO.                                                                                                   Upon success,  mHIT  is  0bbb kkkk .  When mTO expires, this is    set negative.  The MDC rep rate with no stream output is at     about 10 Hz.  The timeout is nominally set for 200 mS to        allow for one retransmit if needed.                          ?MSD  given the number of an MSD display row (2*bd+row), skips     the next word in the caller if that row is not configured.      Otherwise returns formatted message string to make that row     current.  Updates current display values for change detection                                                                1sd  is an internal subroutine of the above.                                                                                    !FB  returns a formatted string to set the current MDC button's    LED to the given state.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      This mechanism exercises MDC control on an RT.  A different blk    is used for CT's.                                                                                                            cma  returns arguments for  +CMD .  However, exits caller if the   button has no control assignment, and marks button inactive     on the way out if our BOSS channel is busy so that we keep      detecting leading edges.                                                                                                     fbk  returns feedback state for the MDC's BOSS channel.                                                                         +BUT  handles a leading edge;                                   ~BUT  handles a continuation of the same button, exercised at      whatever rep rate we get back to ?BUT while the button is       still being held down.                                       -BUT  handles trailing edge.                                                                                                    ?BUT  skips the next word for no button action or returns code     0 for start assert, 1 for continue assert, 2 for deassert.      The behavior table is as follows:                                           Inactive     Active/same   Active/diff              Assert:     start ass.   Continue      Deassert                 Release:    No action    Deassert      Deassert                                                                              ?MDC  given the number of an MDC box, skips the next word in the   caller if that box is not configured or is currently involved   in a button operation (box number found in M.BACT).  Otherwis   returns a string formatted to force all LEDs on the given box   to the OFF state.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            MDCS  is stimulated by arrival of new data and by receipt of any   button message.  Further, when the button state machine is      active, we stay in our local environment without AWAITing       until the button transaction is completed.  This may change     and in fact we may split this bugger into two tasks.            NEW DATA AWAKENING SHOULD BE CONDITIONAL ON EXISTENCE!       ?MSD  refreshes the given MSD row.                              ?DEL  skips the next word if no MSD outputs have changed since     the last time we displayed them.  Otherwise returns lowest      row number that has changed.                                                                                                 The main loop of MSDER refreshes each of the 8 MDC's and then      each of the 16 MSD's.  For each refresh step (less than 200     ms) we are able to insert a hi priority changed output.  For    each output of any sort we are able to perceive and act upon    button actions or perhaps timeouts.                          C.MENU  aggregate CRT menu; 1st byte neg inactive, otherwise is    RT number.  Next two are  full channel descriptor w/RT numberC.LAST  previously displayed data, used by CRT tasks.           C.ID  address in EAROM of channel ID, units, on/off text.  3rd     byte nonzero if this channel has on/off text.                th  converts a slot number relative to the current (TH) CRT to     a system wide C.xxx table index.                                                                                             -CRT  marks the given system wide CRT slot as having no value.  !MENU  sets a given menu item in the given slot for the current    CRT.                                                         ~MENU  refreshes active menus for potentially new default site.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 chn  dismantles a channel descriptor, returning channel within     class under class number (0=cmd, 1=sts, 2=tlm).              Chn  adds separate decodes 3=blank 4=text)                                                                                      Fetch  takes channel#, returns actual current value in eng form.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                .PG  displays the static portions of a page skeleton.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           .id  formats a command action given command mode/1-rel channel     and "raise" action flag.  Channel 0 means nothing is assigned   For all other channels, all commands mean "ON" state except     in the case of "lower" with a latched command.  If nomenclat-   ure exists in the data base for the channel in question, we     use the existing channel name; otherwise we synthesize one.     If named on/off states exist we use them; otherwise faked.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   [sts]  formats commands and status with user nomenclature if       such have been provided.  ON-state is the one that's edited     in the data column at user interface.                                                                                        SHOW  updates a single slot.                                                                                                    CHGD  puts up value of all channels that have actually changed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 WHAT  is current or previous selected channel ID.               *WHOM  is the RT involved; these valid iff WHERE positive.      WHERE  is the slot number (-1 if none) to be updated.  If this     differs from #CHAN, we don't update & we don't allow raise/     lower either.                                                                                                                >RL  sets raise/lower annunciators given command function code.                                                                 ?KM  is true if the CRT is currently set for command mode.      -TC  withdraws from command mode.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               ?An  returns annunciator data for current RT# in V@ format:        ---- ---- ---- Rmla  Restricted, maint, lims, alarm                                                                          ANTS  is a table of annunciator attributes working upward from     low order in the bitmask returned by  ?ann .                                                                                 .ANN  refreshes annunciators if they have changed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Rest  toggles restricted func enable.  NEEDS PASSWORDS!         !RF  exits the caller if restricted funcs are not enabled.                                                                      !sel  picks given slot number, turning previously selected         slot back to low intensity if one was selected.              sel  given delta, selects a channel: same, next, or previous.   Cr  reacts to carriage return by selecting first, next, or         previous channel on screen.                                  CTRL  produces evidence of taking control by showing selected      channel in inverse video.                                    -L'S  toggles state of limit checking.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          +-n  increments the argument if nothing entered, decrements it     if "-" entered, or returns specific number if one entered.      Decrementation may not go below one.                                                                                         Unq  toggles between generic and unique channel ID's if we are     looking at one of the vanilla channel display pages.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         ^r  rude request for control from current site (RESTRICTED)     ^p  polite request (RESTRICTED)                                 ^y  grant request for control                                   ^n  deny same                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   /ALM  decodes an alarm number, returning channel number in oper-   ator units, class as FP page no. (2=telem, 3=status, all rest   system alarms), and original alarm number.                                                                                   ?ACK  finds the LOWEST NUMBERED alarm (sys, status, telem) that    still needs acknowledgement.  The alarm is acknowledged and     the relevant front panel display page is selected.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           <key  returns false if no new key is available, or true over      keystroke from circular buffer.                               ?INKEY  for as many keys are in input buffer (max 8), vectors     through  THINGS .                                             CYCLING  is  TUBE 's runtime behavior.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          PGS  contains 5 bytes for each numbered front panel display page   indexed 1-relatively (page zero stays wherever we are).  The    first cell is addr of the page defn.  Next 3 bytes are page     nos. for previous, next, and setup toggle pages.             Flg  +0 Sign; +1 # of digits; +2 # of dec plcs; +3 Dec flg;        +4 Engineering value in work.                                'PG  holds the address of the current PGS table entry.          :PAG  defines a page definition, given all relevant page nos.      Normally, the definition will be an infinite loop.                                                                           PAG  switches to the given page; zero arg means current page.   CLR  is normal behavior at the beginning of a page definition.                                                                  The TOG definitions  -PG  +PG  SUP  invoke current page's          predecessor, follower, and setup pages.                      SAP  lists pages for system alarms.                             FLDS  is a table that keeps track of all "active" fields in the    current page.  Consists of 3-byte potential engineering val,    address of a display/edit control structure, address of a new   value storing word, and col/line position for the first char    of this field on the display.                                                                                                'FLD  returns address of the current field table entry.                                                                         FLD  incrementally defines fields within  :PAG  definitions.       Usage:   <curval> FLD fedit fsto                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             ?key  returns a raw key code and true if a new key is available.RKEY  sets repeat mode for given raw key value.                 KEYS  converts a key code to subvector and class numbers.  Codes   0 = Immediate:  test, lims, stclr, maint, asch, raise, lower    1 = Routing:  setup, site, chan, page                           2 = Data ent:  Negs  minus, punkt, a/clr, enter, help, n, y.                                                                 .00------.01------.02------.03------.04------.05------.06------.|    7   |    8   |    9   | ACK/CLR|  TEST  |  LIMS  |STAT-CLR|.07------.08------.09------.0A------.0B------.0C------.0D------||    4   |    5   |    6   | MAINT r|  HELP  |ALRM-SCH|  SETUP |.0E------.0F------.10------.11------.12------.13------.14------||    1   |    2   |    3   |  SITE c|  ENTER |   YES  |   NO   |.15------.16------.17------.18------.19------.1A------.1B------||    0   |    .   |    -   |  CHAN  |  PAGE  |RAISE-ON|LOWR-OFF|.--------.--------.--------.--------.--------.--------.--------.@n  gets value in work from  Flg+4  gives it correct sign,         and returns it on stack, while storing into  FLDS  for          the current field.                                           @e  fetches value from  Flg+4  along with # of decimals.        @b  fetches a bit value (y/n) from the sign flag byte.                                                                          AT  makes given field # current, displaying it iff procedure       exists.                                                                                                                      FSTO  turns cursor off, then if anything entered in the field,        If minus only thing entered, backs up one field, otherwise      @EXECUTE 's field storing word then redisplays field;        If nothing entered in field, advances to next field             (or next page if this is last field in the page).                                                                                                                                            "-  "e and  ".  handle minus, digit, and punkt in engineering      fields.                                                      "n-  toggles sign if no digits yet entered; used in num fields. "n  handles digits in num fields; turns sign off.               "h  handles HELP; disables sign and steps numbers cyclically.                                                                   KND                                                                                                                             ?e  accepts an engineering value field; spec cases nil, {-}.    ?n  accepts a numeric field; spec cases nil, {-}.               ?h  accepts a numeric field with HELP support.                  ?y  accepts a YES/NO field with {-} or numeric options permitted                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                .KE  is used for normal display of basic engineering values with   only the central seven characters.                           .KL  is used for limits (trailing asterisk).                                                                                    .KN  is used to display basic positive integers that may have      a minus by itself for navigational use.                      .KY  is used to display yes/no responses allowing navigational     numbers or {-} as well.                                                                                                      .SN  is used with the "phantom" fields that we get into with the   main monitoring pages.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       >OP  establishes the front panel in the OPERATE mode, and stores   a code into #CHAN 2+:  0=system pages, 1=telem, 2=status,       3=command.  This code is used to facilitate interpretation of   #CHAN itself when executing functions common to multiple data   page families.                                               >SET  establishes the front panel in SETUP mode for current fam.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                NIL  enables a nonprinting field that may be used for channel or   page entry.  When used with ENTER we can stay within the        current page or advance to the next one.                                                                                     GHOST  enables a 3-digit field that appears only when active.                                                                   NNN  is display/edit behavior for a basic garden variety 3-digit   numeric field.                                               <eng>  is display/edit behavior for garden variety engrg values.<lim>  is used only for limits with trailing trouble star.      <bit>  is used for yes/no options with numeric keyin option.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    ~lm  toggles the state of the limit enable flag  ?LIM ,  making    the LIMITS led track its state.                              ~Lms  additionally ACKNOWLEDGES ALL ALARMS.  Note that this does   not file any acknowledgement events, but it DOES make the       horn shut up.  ALL alarms are so dealt with, including at the   moment all system alarms.                                    ~Maint  toggles the state of maintenance mode flag  ?MNT , and     makes both the MAINT led and the MAINT relay track its state.                                                                /ALM  decodes an alarm number, returning channel number in oper-   ator units, class as FP page no. (2=telem, 3=status, all rest   system alarms), and original alarm number.                                                                                   ?ACK  finds the LOWEST NUMBERED alarm (sys, status, telem) that    still needs acknowledgement.  The alarm is acknowledged and     the relevant front panel display page is selected.           : FUCK   CR SPACE CR  ZZ  ABORT ;                               : Asch   PAGE ." ALARM SEARCH"  FUCK ;                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          .TLM  types value of current channel in current field.          (STS)  takes engineering value for a status or command channel     and returns addr count for typing.                           <b>  given engineering value, types latest value of sts or cmd     channel iff it has changed from last time we output it.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      DTIN  for special case of data entry key 'ack/clr', checks for     outstanding alarms acknowledgement before passing the key       along to field data input word executed by  FENT .  HOWEVER,    if the current field has had anything entered, the FIRST de-    pression of "ack/clr" is always simply a clear function!                                                                     aKBD  is a table of vectors used by front panel key entry: raw     keycodes are xlated by  KEYS  into a useful byte under          a class no., where class no. selects which of these three       vectors is executed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            ( Page definition)                                           ( fedit is a structured defn that can display and edit..)       prv nxt hlp  this :PAG   PAGE ." EAT SHIT:"                           obtain FLD fedit fsto  ." AND "                                 obtain FLD fedit fsto                                     :PAG joe   'me 'PAG !  PAGE ." EAT SHIT ETC"  format including        variables that don't change while page on screen             acquire data to edit, set in edit tables, refresh display       perform one of several control procedures                          ... need to specify next page, prev page, help page ...                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   As an experiment I have added MUTE to the alarm setup screen.      If Dan likes this, we can do away with screen 13.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            C.MD  holds all control info for each command as an 8-bit field:   pppp ppkk  p=pulse width, .1 sec units; k=kind where            0=latched, 1=momentary, 2=pulsed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            TRIM  returns loop args for the non-null leading portion of a      C-line item sitting in  PAD .                                                                                                .LOG  obtains and formats a given log line from the given RT,      both arguments 1-relative.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   .WIN  displays the set of up to 8 "c" lines called for by the      given "w" line.  Clobbers TRUE TIB, also HERE and PAD for       the individual C-line processes.  Unlike the CT which can do    4 C-lines by 4 sites, the RT can do 8 C-lines.                                                                               ^I  is the manual log operation.                                                                                                C>  types a compressed string.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ?s  returns doubled "s" line number for the current item if it     is a status thing that matches one of the first 49 "s" lines.                                                                -suk  given address of a queue entry, returns alarm ID field       under True if this is a CT internal alarm.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   sREL  is not meaningful in the RT.  sWIN  is maintained as a       single bit (byte) flag.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      OPEN  is true if the current item's window is open.             TRIGGERED  is true if the current item is triggered.                                                                            .WNS  scans window db once, updating wWIN and also making a list   (bit table) of triggered items to process at PAD 64 + which     we then display as above for ^I.                                                                                             <ej  updates the list of ejects with open windows.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              NOTE:  It is vitally important that Lflg (and sREL) be at least    initialized to their powerup default states before the first    PAUSE.  These defaults must inhibit logging so that we won't    inappropriately suck any queue items.  Note that sREL is set    to zero; THEN we get actual values for flags; THEN we scan      the data base to set sREL properly.  The effect of all this     is simply to delay logging; we won't be tricked into dropping   anything on the floor, and we won't print anything that we      should not.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ?DBOK  checks for invalid data base and asks operator for per-     mission to reinitialize it if so.  Returns if and only if we    seem to have a solid EAROM subsystem.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        VALIDATE  checks our configuration to determine whether it's ok    and safe to fire ourselves up.  Chk  sets the  ?ex  flag if     we appear to have a 488 interface for expansion boxes.  It      returns "true" if we do not seem to have a file memory con-     troller board, in which case we refuse to run.  Otherwise, we   acquire the site number from switches 2 and 1.  IF THE SITE     IS 99 AND Switch 3 is 0 (normally it's nonzero) we zero the     anchor file and halt, waiting for switches back to normal and   reset.  Otherwise, we validate the data base for self consis-   tency and insist on initializing it before we proceed.                                                                       The  099  switch boot is REQUIRED if any chips have been changed   anywhere in the file system and is a good idea but not req'd    if boards have been changed.  Deleting boards requires init.    Adding boards can be handled under normal system control.                                                                    ?hz  measures the period of line frequency in 16 uS units.         Returns zero if the PIA is not perceiving edges any faster      than 1 Hz.                                                                                                                   ?HZ  uses this measurement to do two things:  If the line freq     observed looks to be less than 30 Hz or more than 100 Hz,       wHZ is set to cause a system alarm later.  If the period of     interrupts seems to be less than 18 mS according to the CPU     crystal, we set ourselves for 60 Hz operation; otherwise, the   clock is scaled for 50 Hz.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Courteous virtual terminal for NEW disk I/O (sync after wrt).                                                                   UNITE  unites the caller with the given task so that the caller    may later PLUG into that task's port as a virtual terminal.     Parity and disk access are implicitly disabled.                                                                              +PTY  enables parity on data going to the slave port, implicitly   disabling disk requests from it (since they need 8 bit data).-PTY  disables parity to the slave.  Disk needs separate work.  +DISK  enables disk requests from the UNITEd port, and          -DISK  disables them.                                                                                                           CAMP  sets the current task to use the given EXPECT interrupt      and enables the interrupt.                                   +PAL  and  -PAL  enable and disable REMOTE's input interrupt.                                                                   +RMT  runs disk protocol for the client:                              Disk reads                   Disk writes                        80      04                   80      04                         00                           01                                 hi                           hi                                 lo      1k                   lo      04                                                      1k      04                                                                                   -RMT  stops client support task & makes it a regular terminal.                                                                  PLUG  plugs into the current client.  Escape with Ctrl-x.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       +PIA  takes the address of a 5-byte table:  2-byte port (a/b)      adr, data, control, and direction.  Control MUST have data      access bit set.  Sets desired control mode, then data (which    we now think works), then selects and sets control, and at      the same time reselects data.  Finally reads input port to      turn off IRQ bits and lines, returning advanced table address                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                PEON  does the actual work of servicing requests from the main     RT box as given us over the 488 link.                        +PEON  starts this process.  In the working system, once this      has been initiated the watchdog is active.                                                                                   uart  is the interrupt routine for operator debugging terminal     I/O.  This is optional and is not used in the working system.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                /initialize  initializes hardware.  UART's in groups 2 and 3       are forced into reset, necessary because they lack hardware     reset inputs.  The umbilical is initialized.  Restart vector    is set for running test systems from RAM.  Interrupts are       enabled fully.                                               /INITIALIZE  performs all required hardware and application data   base initialization required on power up or reset.                                                                           Interrupts are normally indirectly vectored thru hi RAM.  Some     have permanent hardware assignments:                             -2 Reset                 -4 NMI Unused                          -6 SWI Unused            -8 IRQ Unused                          -A FIRQ,INT7h 50/60 Hz   -C INT6                                -E INT5                 -10 INT4                               -12 INT3h All Modem II   -14 INT2 Debug terminal                -16 INT1                 -18 INT0 hFP Kbd; IEEE-488          These tables are used by  START  (next block) to initialize        RAM.  The important tables follow a dummy clock interrupt.   RAM  is the table containing initial values for the system         variables, following the map in block 75.  Note that initial    values are needed only for the first 38 bytes.               OPERATOR  contains the information necessary to initialize the     one task that is active following a boot.  The format of this   table is identical to those constructed by  TERMINAL (blk 24,   25).  It starts with STATUS , S0 , and the output and input     interrupt code.  The next 46 bytes map to the 1st 46 bytes      of the user variable area, as described in block 75.  Note      that the latter part of this table, the following table  ram    and the startup code will be overlaid by the dictionary after   it has been used at boot time.                                                                                                                                                               This power-up behavior supports RAM system testing and is not      suitable for high reliability installed system.                                                                              After initializing the hardware, we check for presence of the      debugging port.  The background application is started up       UNLESS the debugging port is present AND we have both CTS and   DCD, in which case we leave it alone so that changes can be     made before activating it.  If the debugging port is present,   the operator acts like a normal pF terminal; otherwise, the     operator is put to sleep.  Also, when the debugging port is     present on the box, we re-arm the restart routine so that a     RAM-tested PROM will retain its sticking power.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              This power-up behavior supports RAM system testing and is not      suitable for high reliability installed system.                                                                              After initializing the hardware, we check for presence of the      debugging port.  The background application is started up       UNLESS the debugging port is present AND we have both CTS and   DCD, in which case we leave it alone so that changes can be     made before activating it.  If the debugging port is present,   the operator acts like a normal pF terminal; otherwise, the     operator is put to sleep.  Also, when the debugging port is     present on the box, we re-arm the restart routine so that a     RAM-tested PROM will retain its sticking power.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Pagination is merely monitored in edit mode.  In logging mode,     however, things are busier.                                  /PAGE/  must do it the hard way, e.g. through CR's and strong      assumption of no wraparound.  Guarantees clean page; never      moves more than  L/P  lines.                                                                                                 'TOF  resolves a forward reference to top of form behavior.     +CR  advances the given number of lines unless we would be on a    new page, in which case we eject and produce relevant heading   and then cough up the requested number of lines anyhow.      CR'S  advances the standard number of lines for log items.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      TIB  reserves first 34 bytes of  PAD  for construction of          earom database items.                                        CRAP  and its friends are our way of giving up on making sense     of what has been typed.                                      hhmm  and  (hm)  let us type total seconds as hours, minutes.                                                                   ?SK  keeps us honest re stack effects.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          <KEYS>  is interrupt code for incoming characters from the         Printer. If the character is an xoff, we set  FLG  and          disable the output interrupt. If we are already xoff'd, then    any non-xoff character is considered an xon, and we clear       the xoff flag, and enable the output interrupt iff we were      typing. If neither of above, then we leave character in  'key   and  AWAKEN  the Logger task via  WHIP .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     This block is for parsing the input stream:                     -TIB  points  'TIB  at first byte in  TIB .                     More  is true if input buffer has not been exhausted.           @TIB  fetches the next character from the input stream.         ?ch  takes input char, mask, and desired char; returns mask and    forces exit if equal, otherwise returns char unscathed.      @Ns  converts numbers from input stream, returning mask telling    why conversion ended (terminating char left in  trm ):          1 end input; 2 colon/semicolon; 4 space/comma; 8 dash.          Incoming number is in  TMP , count of digits is in #TMP.        Aborts on crap.                                              [N]  given max digits and terminator mask, returns legal number    or aborts if size or terminator bad.                                                                                                                                                                                                                         This high level expect for loggers supports linefeed on the        first of several backspaces, echo contents of input buffer      for ctrl^D, and delete entire line for ctrl^X.                                                                               KEE  accepts a non echoed key, ignoring other stimuli.  Used in    edit mode ONLY.  Converts lower case to upper if  case  set     to 5F; to disable temporarily, set  case  to 80 around use of   KEE or KEXP.                                                                                                                 -KEE  flushes out any pending character before awaiting single     keystroke.  Keeps operator's busy fingers in phase with us.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  @ID#  parses off the line id#, present in characters 1 thru 3 of   every command line. Returns true if satisfactory, and leaves    value on stack.                                                                                                              either  parses the next character, returning 0 if it matches tos   and 1 if it matches the character underneath.                   Aborts if neither.                                           dlm  insists that next character be comma or space.                                                                             vec  and  PICK  force a jump to the first, second or third         word referenced after  PICK  in the calling definition,         along with the input argument if appropriate, or they allow     execution to continue following these words, depending on       what the user typed in at the Logger kbd. Usage is:             PICK Delete List-One List-All ...(insert new)                                                                                This code parses 'C' commands, which define contents of a Log      Line (a single line of data sent to printer). Format:        Cnnn:Tc,...,Tc                                                     nnn  Log Line number; when several defined for printing              during same window of time, lower numbered lines will           be printed first.                                          Tc   Channel descripton, where  T  may be  A (Telemetry) or          S (Status), and  c  is a one to three digit channel no.         Up to 18 allowed; Dash is NOT SUPPORTED!                .id#  lists line identification number.                         ?,  prints trailing comma iff more data will follow.            .Lc  prints a Log Line out of  PAD .                            .C  lists all existing Log Lines.                               Ckey  processes a 'C' command.                                                                                                                                                                  This code parses 'W' commands, which specify times when up to      four Log Lines are printed for up to four Sites. Format:     Wnnn:bbbb,eeee,iiii,c,c,c,c,s,s,s,s  (s's are c's for RT)          nnn  Window Line Number, for id only.                           bbbb,eeee,iiii  Beginning, Ending, and Interval Times when         following  c  (Log Line #'s) lines printed for  s  Sites. .Wn  lists given window number.                                 .W  lists all existing windows.                                 hhmm  parses a 24-hour clock time, storing it in minutes past      midnight at the given  PAD  offset.                          @HHMM  parses the [start time, end time, time incr] form and       leaves the resulting three numbers at  PAD 2+ .  The end time   is converted to end+1; the whole mess is error checked.      wx  converts next number in input stream, leaving it at  a+1 .  Wkey  processes a 'W' command.                                                                                                  This code parses 'H' commands, which specify individual lines of   headings. Format:                                            Hnnn:ll:<cr>                                                    <heading text>                                                     nnn  Heading Identification Number, for id only.                ll   Line number within Heading.                             -Lh  deletes entire Heading whose number is given.              -lh  deletes single line from a Heading.                        .H  lists all Headings found to exist in earom.                 >uq  inserts any database item in  PAD  into earom.             >Lh  inserts given hunk into earom using  >uq .                 xp  obtains a long unlimited string, returning its length.      sq  given starting address of a string just expected, squishes     it and returns address just beyond compressed string.                                                                                                                                        This code parses 'E' commands, which define Eject Lines (when to   print headings). Format:                                     Ennn:bbbb,eeee,iiii,nnn,p,f                                        nnn  Eject Line Identification Number, for id only.             bbbb,eeee,iiii  Beginning, Ending, and Interval Times when         Header number  nnn  should be printed.                       p    Y means print heading at top of page.                      f    Y means print new page at beginning of time period.     .Ej  prints an Eject Line out of  PAD .                         .E  lists all existing Eject Lines.                             Ekey  processes a 'E' command.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  This code parses 'S' commands, which define Status Msgs            (msgs to present upon alarms or events). Format:             Snnn:ss,ccc,x,y<cr>                                             <status msg text>                                                  nnn  Status Msg Identification Number, for id only.             ss   Site # (MUST BE ONE AT AN RT!!)                            ccc  Channel #                                                  x    A  means Alarms,  E  means Events.                         y    R  means Rising,  F  means Falling.                     .Ls  prints a Status Msg Line out of  PAD .                     .S  lists all existing Eject Lines.                             Skey  processes a 'S' command.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  Here are defined several miscellaneous keys, to wit:            Fkey  prints field template, for ease in laying out spacing.    Jkey  sets number of blank lines to follow each Log Line.       Pkey  prints current setting of above.                          Mkey  tells Logger that printer is currently at top of form.                                                                    4n  fetches a four digit number from the input stream.          Dkey  sets current date or displays current setting.            Tkey  sets current time or displays current setting.            Ykey  sets year.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                This vast piece of code handles the trivially simple "A" key.      Basic logger flags are displayed and maintained.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             DONE  is behavior for exiting Edit mode and returning to           Logging mode.                                                                                                                kzzz  table of vectors corresponding to legal editing keys.     ?kzzz  is given first character in input line and figures out      whether and how to parse rest of line.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       KEDIT  enters edit mode.                                        Seven unsolicited keystrokes are supported while in operational    mode:                                                        ^C     Enter Edit Mode                                          ^D     New Page with current date                               ^I     Manually Log Current Log Line.                           ^J     Blank Line.                                              ^K     New Page with Current Headers.                           ^Hnnn  New Page, Date, Header #nnn                              ^Lnnn  Print Log Line #nnn                                                                                                      kees  is the table of these keystrokes and what they do.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        NOTE:  It is vitally important that Lflg (and sREL) be at least    initialized to their powerup default states before the first    PAUSE.  These defaults must inhibit logging so that we won't    inappropriately suck any queue items.  Note that sREL is set    to zero; THEN we get actual values for flags; THEN we scan      the data base to set sREL properly.  The effect of all this     is simply to delay logging; we won't be tricked into dropping   anything on the floor, and we won't print anything that we      should not.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  OPEN  is true if the current item's window is open.             TRIGGERED  is true if the current item is triggered.                                                                            .WNS  scans window db once, updating wWIN and also making a list   (bit table) of triggered items to process at PAD 64 + which     we then display as above for ^I.                                                                                             <ej  updates the list of ejects with open windows.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              During eject processing, SCR is zero if PAGE has not been done     for the current header group; 1 if PAGE shall not be done;      and -1 if TOF has been done.  Only one PAGE per header group!wtf  is true if current eject should happen because at a PAGE.  1mt  is true if current eject is triggered or a  wtf  type.     sej  initializes SCR to the given value and displays all ejects    that satisfy the following selection.  PAGE is done if SCR      indicates that it's permissible, the eject indicates PAGE,      and the eject is itself triggered this minute.  Date is shown   at top of page on a line by itself.                          .EJ  does eject processing appropos for 1-minute boundary.                                                                      mIS  is the minute last done.  CYC  chases  mis  one minute at     a time, but will not allow itself to get ahead or more than     five minutes behind real time.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               <Le , >Le , and -Le  fetch, store, and delete logger Eject skeds   respectively.                                                <Lw , >Lw , and -Lw  do the same for Window schedules.          <Lc , >Lc , and -Lc  do the same for log lines ("C").                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           @Lf  assumes that Lflg has been initialized to default values.     If logger flags exist in data base, these are copied into       Lflg.  Otherwise defaults survive.                           !Lf  stores the values in Lflg into the data base, creating the    logger flag item if it doesn't already exist.                                                                                Each of these words maintains  Lfil  which is the prefilter        control used by SUCKER.  It must combine the effects of the     alarm and event log modes.  If either byte is nonzero, Lfil     must be nonzero.  If either byte has the value 1, Lfil must     have the value 1.                                                                                                            <sREL  finds all window schedules for the TH logger and returns    with bits in  sREL  set for all sites that we ever care about<lst  makes a list of existing items matching a 1-byte mask.                                                                    -grp  summarily nukes all records that meet its search criteria.                                                                .bts  lists the bits turned on in a bitarray(256).                                                                              ~h  given header and line numbers and mask for low cell, returns   all of this rearranged for searching for all hunks satisfying   search criteria.  To use for "s" items, add 1000 hex to tos.                                                                 <hnk  given search masks for all hunks of an "h" or "s" followed   by text bytes per hunk, returns a clean string in blanked PAD   suitable for expansion.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      TRIM  returns loop args for the non-null leading portion of a      C-line item sitting in  PAD .                                                                                                .LOG  obtains and formats a given log line from the given RT,      both arguments 1-relative.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   .WIN  displays the array of "c" lines versus sites called for by   the given "w" line.  Clobbers TRUE TIB, also HERE and PAD for   the individual C-line processes.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             ?s  returns doubled "s" line number for the current item if it     is a status thing that matches one of the first 49 "s" lines.                                                                -suk  given address of a queue entry, returns alarm ID field       under True if this is a CT internal alarm.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Network output state machine.                                   Adv  advances to the vector in  I  then...                      toss  pitches the character in  B  and accumulates CRC.         -xmt  catches carrier loss while not transmitting.              States:                                                            0 - set RxIE, RTS, TxIE, 8bits/none/2sb.  ->2.                  2 - pitch FF's until count goes neg, then pitch 00, reset           CRC, ->4.                                                   4 - pitch RT ID & Phase.  Set 1sb.  ->6.                        6 - Transmit >=1 byte of data.  End data send dle, ->10.            Extract char, send.  Not dle ->6; dle ->8.                  8 - After dle.  Send dle, ->6.                                 10 - pitch control byte with phase, ->12                        12 - pitch low CRC, ->14                                        14 - pitch high CRC; if unswitched carrier ->rcv, else ->16     16 - pitch specified trail pads, when done -RTS, ->rcv.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Rxn  accepts all network input interrupts.  Entered with table     in W, status in A.  UART problems are saved and CRC is accum-   ulated.  Finally we vector to current receive state.         States:                                                            0 - Enable receiver logic, ->2.                                 2 - Search for  xFF 00  sequence; good ->4                      4 - Take RTID/phase byte, ->6.                                  6 - Look for data.  dle => ignore, ->8;                                Other => count, stash.                                   8 - Look for dle follower.  Not ctl => store using 6.                  ctl => save flag, ->10.                                 10 - Take low CRC byte, ->12.                                   12 - Take high CRC byte, evaluate status, ...WHAT?...                                                                                                                                                                                                         Completion status byte:                                            tcof lcbi  timeout, crc, oe, fe; lng, dcd, dir/flg, rtid.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Done  receives control after a message has finished coming in      without timeout.  IFF status is good we vector to one of five   places based on high nibble of command byte with W pointing     to  Ntbl  and S pointing at  MASTBL .  Under these conditions   actual buffer start address lies in  18 W)  and actual length   in  6 W) .                                                                                                                   Nto  receives control when either transmit or receive has timed    out.  Transmission is disabled and we return to receive state   zero, looking for a new message.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             nil  returns no data.                                           xlng  takes 1's comp of response length in  D .  Sends same.       Both of these also indicate we are NOT in command mode,         but if and only if we are currently talking to the Master!                                                                   ~nil  and  ~lng  do the same things, but potentially leave us      in command mode.                                                                                                             syst  composes system status byte & blows out response.  Status:  1rve Mmla  (async busy, answer available, events to suck;                  You are Master, maint, lims enabled, alarm)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Outbound function decode:                                       * 1000 xxxx  - x=0 request system status only                                  x>0 reboot request (1 only is used)              > 1001 sfhh  Normal data poll (FP active, hi chan number)                    s set authorizes reset of comand failsafe timer.     1010 -rhh  Command (raise, hi chan number)                    * 1011 ----  Poll for async results.                            > 11xx xxxx  Async request; x=what requested.                                                                                   Main data buffers are declared with allowance for max length       FPAN reponse in front of whatever menu is current.  Poll resp   here appends 0|2|4 bytes in front depending on availability     and size of what the FPAN needs.  Length is adjusted as nec.                                                                 POLL NEEDS TO EXTRACT s-BIT AND USE IT TO RESET CMD FAILSAFE!                                                                   Command mode messages are placed in bSTIM and return last bANS     buffer with bTO reset for 2 seconds; returns dry if illegal.                                                                 Async func codes:                                                 0=Start menu sequence.  1=MSD menu data  2=Crt0 menu  3=Crt1    4=Crt2  5=Crt3  6=Limits on  7=limits off                                                                                     Async requests are funneled into GOFER via MASTBL.  The funnels    are safe because we ONLY take such a request seriously if we    (a) received it via this channel's Big Buffer and (b) MASTBL    shows no request in progress.  In that case we disable curnt    channel's big buffer and mark MASTBL+1 with the flag byte.      Actual positive data length is put in Ntbl-14.                                                                                                                                                                                                               Kode  contains security code /100 in 1st byte; mod 100 with sign   set in 2nd.  All zero if code 0 (=> none).                                                                                   Id0  handles phase 0 of ID protocol.  When a pos/neg pair has      been received, we check for match with  Kode  or just accept    it if no code effective.  (CT should send 00 00 until it has    figured out who we are; then the security code.)  Until we      are satisfied, we echo our site number and its complement.      When satisfied, we advance to ...                                                                                            Id1  which handles phase 1.  We echo 80's (not legal during the    above) to let CT know we bought code; and we keep doing this    until CT sends us an FF (not legal for phase 0 either).                                                                      Du0  waits for  1 <CR>  indicating modem has established conn,     then advances to ID protocol.                                GBUF  is the async rqst buffer; RBUF is normal response buffer. MENU  is the current master's menu list for normal polls.       aRSP  hands a given async response buffer to the current master.0rsp  returns a zero length response buffer.                                                                                    RESPONSES are put into the big buffers with actual data starting   four bytes into the buffer.  In this way nothing but an async   request (which nukes response-available) can step on response   and thus we don't need to fool with lots of other nonsense.     This does mean, however, that true polling of a slave may NOT   be done on both channels in parallel or we could be transmit-   ting one while receiving another into the same buffer (ugh!)    ZERO LENGTH RESPONSES ARE ILLEGAL!                           ?mas  is true if the current CT is Master.                                                                                                                                                      A Menu defines the data to be reported by an RT for a given CT.    The menu, being lengthy, is subdivided into five parts for      transmission.  Each CT/RT combination has an associated menu    update sequence number used to provide synchronization.                                                                      Menu synchronization procedure:                                   1.  Send msg C0 with new master event index.  Sets flag saying      synch started; zeroes response serial number.  If seven         bytes sent, +1 is 2sec time, +3 BCD date, +5 yr.            2.  Send zero or more menu hunks with last-flag zero.  Each         sends a zero length async response message.                 3.  Send one hunk with last-flag nonzero.  IFF flag saying          synch started is true, implements new response number.          Returns 1-byte message saying (nz) new response number now      in effect.                                                                                                                Resp lngths:  Menus=2; Lim chg=1; Cmd id=4; Alarms=3; Stsclr=1;    PROMid 32; Lims=33; Tlm failsafe 12;                                                                                         ?id  handles request for command id's used with the given chan.    returns 1-rel raise, lower commands in 3-byte message.       GOFE  is the transfer vector for all async requests.            Active  figures out which net link we got the request from and     stores the address of its big buffer in the async response      pointer for subsequent use by  GBUF .                                                                                        GOFING  responds to arrivals of async requests by scanning for     and dealing with any such that seem to exist.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                kct  is a downcount of menu items left to look at.              kbf  is next loc in data buffer to be used.                     kmn  is next loc in menu to be examined.                                                                                        <k  opens menu interpretation by setting pointers and saving       the number of the menu currently in place.                   >k  sets length and serial of data buffer iff menu serial hasn't   changed while we were acquiring data.  BOTH ARE RETURN STACK    SENSITIVE, for local use only.  Buffer length isn't updated     if we used bogus menu under construction.                                                                                    >m  scans for active MSD menu items.  SAY and alarms for each      are appended to the data buffer and all status lines on the     given board are un-flashed.  No attention is paid to the int    code since internal consistency is not vital here.  Returns     with  kct  set for the next step (CRT menus)                 ?c  scans for active CRT menu items.  If one found, returns its    channel and class on stack.  When done skips two words.                                                                      !c  appends a single byte datum to the data buffer, exits caller   and executes preceding word after a pause.                                                                                   >c  appends an engineering value to the data buffer SAFELY.        Main data are single buffered, so the interrupt code may be     transmitting the very engineering value we propose to change.   Therefore,  >c  spins in a PAUSE loop if either of the chan's   for this master is about to transmit the second or third byte   of the item we'd be storing.  While we may be able to stick     in resonance, following the interrupt code around, this isn't   believed likely enough to agonize over.                                                                                                                                                      cTLM  acquires an engineering value, returning it for storage      safety check.                                                                                                                cCMD  and  cSTS  acquire command and status report bytes, taking   a cheap way back through the loop after storing them.                                                                        @c  acquires the data requested, storing and looping if the data   were command or status (one byte, safety unnecessary).                                                                       +KENT  activates CLARK to acquire data for CT's.  The basic poll   cycle with nothing to do is 13.5 ms exclusive of pause's.       This comes to 3.375 mS per master.  To keep things under        control, we throw in a PAUSE after acquiring MSD data.                                                                                                                                                                                                       !MAS  establishes given CT as master.  Turns off all control       request alarms since we have resolved the issue.                                                                             ?ak  acknowledges the given alarm.  Special case:  Whoever ack's   Control Failsafe Warning immediately becomes master!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         <ev  returns up to the given number of oldest unacknowledged       event queue items for a particular logger on a particular CT.   Oldest item is sent first.  Sets the flag arming  >ak  to       accept a credit for these items later.                                                                                       >ak  acknowledges event queue items for a particular logger on a   particular CT.  Doesn't credit counts if the credit is out of   context or has already been applied.  Turns off the new-event   flag and returns concurrent new value for the per-logger mask   indicating un-sucked events.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 ?l  scans for active Log menu items.  If one found, returns its    channel and class on stack.  When done skips two words.                                                                      !l  appends a single byte datum to the data buffer, exits caller   and executes preceding word after a pause.                                                                                   >l  appends an engineering value to the data buffer.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            ?mr  message 0 normal, 1 rude request for mastership.  If rude     from CT "A", or if any request from anyone when control fail-   safe warning active, we give control immediately with no fan-   fare.  Otherwise we set alarm corresponding to this CT and      await further developments (alarm remains pending until next    decision made about mastership).                                                                                             !mr  message 0 rej, 1 grant request for mastership.  Iff this      is received from current master, we either hand it off to the   lowest lettered requestor or retain control.                                                                                 ?er  reads 32 bytes from given EAROM address and returns them      with the address in front.                                   !er  writes 1..32 bytes to EAROM and transmits 32 bytes as ?er     above for validation.                                                                                                        Rx  initializes the given channel table for connected operation.   This procedure is NOT used for dialup lines.                                                                                 Rans  given vector and state, initializes a dialup line.  Usage    is  Du0 1  to await incoming calls, and  Id0 3  to initiate     the ID protocol.  Interrupts on the line must NOT be enabled    when this is done!!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          qSTO  and  qFET  store and fetch byte pointers for each logger's   queue.  qPOP  count of waiting items per logger.             QUE  96 bytes for each logger.                                                                                                  ?room  returns number of entries left in current queue.                                                                         relevant  is true if the RT whose number is  I  is not on the      slow poll list, has events for this logger or has new events    in general, and the logger is either configured for all sites   or selects on site and  sREL  indicates the site is relevant.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                suck  absorbs event queue items for the currently selected         logger.  Takes the easy way out if queue is full.  Maintains    R.LOGS bitmask with convention that any error trying to ack-    nowledge goodies from an RT must be assumed to mean that we     have yet more to get from that source.  The other side of       this recovery procedure is that we may get zero items from      the RT when we ask it at some later time.  Note that we take    no steps to commutate between RT's on an equal time basis.      Lower numbered RT's have implicit priority; continual events    from a low numbered RT can attenuate events from higher no'd    ones just like radar in a thunderstorm.                                                                                      SUCKING  fires up SUCKER to shuffle events into CT as appropos.                                                                                                                                                                                                 CASIO  is awakened via  CLK  every two seconds.  It exists only    for the purpose of distributing infrequent stimuli to other     tasks.  Its other responsibility is to maintain  mis  which     is the current minute relative to midnight.  This is used by    various tasks that run on time schedules.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Network output state machine.                                   Adv  advances to the vector in  I  then...                      toss  pitches the character in  B  and accumulates CRC.         -xmt  catches carrier loss while not transmitting.              States:                                                            0 - set RxIE, RTS, TxIE, 8bits/none/2sb.  ->2.                  2 - pitch FF's until count goes neg, then pitch 00, reset           CRC, ->4.                                                   4 - pitch RT ID & Phase.  Set 1sb.  ->6.                        6 - Transmit >=1 byte of data.  End data send dle, ->10.            Extract char, send.  Not dle ->6; dle ->8.                  8 - After dle.  Send dle, ->6.                                 10 - pitch control byte with phase, ->12                        12 - pitch low CRC, ->14                                        14 - pitch high CRC; if unswitched carrier ->rcv, else ->16     16 - pitch specified trail pads, when done -RTS, ->rcv.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       Rxn  accepts all network input interrupts.  Entered with table     in W, status in A.  UART problems are saved and CRC is accum-   ulated.  Finally we vector to current receive state.         States:                                                            0 - Enable receiver logic, ->2.                                 2 - Search for  xFF 00  sequence; good ->4                      4 - Take RTID/phase byte, ->6.                                  6 - Look for data.  dle => ignore, ->8;                                Other => count, stash.                                   8 - Look for dle follower.  Not ctl => store using 6.                  ctl => save flag, ->10.                                 10 - Take low CRC byte, ->12.                                   12 - Take high CRC byte, evaluate status, ...WHAT?...                                                                                                                                                                                                         Completion status byte:                                            tcof lcbi  timeout, crc, oe, fe; lng, dcd, dir/flg, rtid.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Done  is entered with status in B on any kind of a termination.                                                                 Nto  is called via BSR from clock interrupt with ntbl address in   W to effect a timeout.  All registers except W volatile.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     aFUN  performs an alarm interaction with the current RT given      alarm number and function (8=find alarm needing ack; 9=do so    and acknowledge it; 0=acknowledge the given alarm; 10=find      point still in alarm or needing ack PAST the given one.)        True if exchange worked.  HERE returns an alarm number or -1.aFx  is for internal use here only!!                            ?sk  checks the given site for ack's, exiting caller if found.                                                                  ?as  performs an external alarm search from an alarm in a site. ?ak  finds an alarm needing acknowledgement, starting with the     given site in preference to internal & external alarms.         Returns RT# set for the site, -1 if internal.  Alarm number     is -1 for site as a whole, otherwise RT style alarm no.         The search is circular so we can skip troublesome sites and     still find things of interest without losing track of the       trouble.                                                        This block controls the entire target compilation of the     system, including both standard FORTH features (block 60) and   configuration characteristics:  disk & terminal drivers, amount of memory, number of disk buffers, presence/absence of heads,   etc.  Consult the Target Compiler documentation for a discussionof specific details.                                                                                                               Note especially the  ALLOT  on line 5.  This sets the  RAM   pointer to the start of the system variables area.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              'PG  holds the address of the current PGS table entry.          #CHAN  is the 1-RELATIVE channel number being looked at with the   front panel.  Zero means "system" channel.  #CHAN 2+  shows     what SORT of thing we are looking at:  0 System, 1 Telem,       2 Status, 3 Command.  #CHAN 4+  holds the encoded alarm no.     we last found via  SEARCH  or  ACK .                         TH  identifies which member of a set of replicated tasks we are.RT#  RT ID for data base & line access.                         A/B  nonzero if we are using the "B" line for this RT.          'NT  address of Ntbls for line in use.                          Lflg:  +0 alm log mode; +1 summ mode; +2 l/p; +3 lf/log         sREL  bit-table indexed by Site#: nonzero indicates interest.   Bit arrays:  sWIN , cWIN  Sites, Loglines currently Windowed;      wACT , eACT Windows, Ejects triggered this minute(s).        Loggers use  'TIB , TMP , #TMP , 'PAD , ?BS                                                                                     ac  and  ov  are accumulator and overflow flag for floating ari.SL#  current 1-rel slave #; 'SL addresses its SLAVES table.     LLNG  total length of active LATEST table.                      ireg  tracks value in 8010, instrumentation reg.                slo  mask for polls to implement slow polling.                  ros  last masked POLL result;  +ros  is unmasked version.       known  and  -known  poll masks for such boxes last config cycle.cok, tmp  local temps for 488 stuff                             err  holds error status following a receive operation.          OVAL  is nonzero if we've accepted good output data from master.tik  countdown for 10 Hz stim;  2tk for 5;  TIK  awakened at 10 ntik  is line freq Hz /10.                                      ?MNT  ?SET  ?LIM  ?RAI  ?LOW  1st byte FF if corresp led on,       2nd byte mask of bits needed to turn led on.                 ^b  and  ^-b  after call to  )bit  contain addrs in  BITS  for     masks used when turning on and off bits in a bitarray.       Stbl  is a quick lookup table for existence of status initiated    messages 1 thru 49.                                                                                                          Alarm numbering:                                                    0- 64  Comm A trouble                                          64-128  Comm B trouble                                         128-192  Dial A fail                                            192-256  Dial B fail                                            256-320  Dial A sever                                           320-384  Dial B sever                                           384-448  RT reporting alarm  (56 bytes)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       E.CM  comm line setup info (except dialup)                      E.RT  comm specifics for RT's                                   E.SD  params for MSD's                                          E.DC  params for MDC's                                          E.PW  password; zero => none.                                                                                                   E.bot  is 22 below the first file item in the top EAROM.  This     one starts abnormally (the rest go at xx20) because it's only   800 hex long.  The starting modulus of E008 arranges for us     to increment precisely to E800.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              Ntbls  exist for each CT network port possible.  Structure is:    -16  Input buffer (big)     -14  In lng 2's comp (abs=busy)     -12* Tmot | Tx reset        -10* Lead | trail pads, 100 unswit   -8* Data port               -6* Control port                    -4* Output vec              -2  Input subvec                    +0  Task(mas), Mastbl(slv)  +2  Bigbufused | fout/stsin         +4  PTR (next char)         +6  CTR (out 1's, in 2's comp)      +8  Rx RTID|Flag; Scratch  +10  Rx crud | Rx ENABLED           +12  CRC build both dir     +14  Scratch | RTID (+80 SLAVE)     +16  Tx init | Rx init | Rx total | Dialup flg                  +20  Short buffer (4 bytes) +24  Loc of current rcv buffer    RT only...                                                        +26  2fre last good rx msg  +28  Line state flag:                    0=idle/needing init, 1=idle/waiting for incoming, 2=dial,       3=ID protocol, 4=connected, 5=NONEXISTENT.                                                                               R.FAC  facility controlling RT async function subchannel.       R.ACT  nonzero if channel "B" is in active use.                 R.SLOW  nonzero if slow poll in effect                          R.A  is primary link (neg if unconnected);  R.B  is secondary.     4x => active dialup; Cx => unconnected dialup.               R.AQ  and  R.BQ  are primary & secdy quality shift registers.   R.OK  flag for last main data poll successful at LINE LEVEL.    R.COND  is last condition byte from remote (neg if ever).       R.AMEN  is active menu serial last received from remote;        R.DMEN  is desired menu serial (currently in effect) this rmt.  R.UGH  1-bit says correct menu hasn't come back filled yet;        2-bit says don't trust what the RT says no matter what; 4-bit   inciates COP action is required.                             R.LOGS  event queue to suck bits (2**logger)                    R.DU  A,B dialup link+1 w/current responsibility.               +R  and  +2R  index the above tables by  RT# .                  C.MENU  aggregate CRT menu; 1st byte neg inactive, otherwise is    RT number.  Next two are  full channel descriptor w/RT numberC.NEW  latest data for menued channel.  ok effective on all.    C.LAST  previously displayed data, used by CRT tasks.           C.ID  address in EAROM of channel ID, units, on/off text.  3rd     byte nonzero if this channel has on/off text.                                                                                M.MENU  aggregate MSD menu; 1st byte neg if INACTIVE, otherwise    is RT number and 2nd byte is status board number.            M.DATA  latest data for the board;                              M.ALM   latest alarms.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          These strings are used often enough to justify naming them.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     This  .MOVE  is highly stripped down for vetting keyins.  It is    stupid about zero length and assumes parity's been stripped.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 This clock interrupt maintains TICKS at the line frequency.  It    also divides the clock down to 10 Hz at which frequency TIK     is awakened and the alarm LED flashes, and to 5 Hz at which     the cursors flash.                                                                                                           Simulates keyboard interrupts at the line frequency whenever we    are in repeat key (trailing edge sense) mode.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                For network channels:  We check each channel's timeout slot.       If zero, do nothing.  Otherwise count down and if zero we       need to simulate a termination.  This entails loading up W      and simulating an FIRQ type interrupt to the common timeout     handler.  Implies that network interrupts MUST be loaded be-    fore this one!                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               E@  and  E!  support RT internal style engineering variables,      where 2 bytes of value precede one byte of flag.             V@  fetches a datum as collected over the network, differing       only in that the flag byte PRECEDES the 2-byte value.                                                                        /-/  is a positive circular difference in minutes/day space.                                                                    /hm  converts hhmm to minutes and saves at a given PAD offset.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  <msd  elaborates the MSD configuration vectors into the full       data acquisition table structure, updating RT menus as needed   when individual slots are changed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           mST  is current state index into mSV.                                                                                           mi  receives chars.  UART errors go to  mu  which resets state 0   Otherwise vector to appropriate state:                                                                                            0  Looking for 01 ->0; else stay here.                          2  Looking for 30 ->4; else ->0.                                4  Looking for board 3x ->6, save x in mTMP; else ->0.          6  Looking for key 3k ->8, save k in mTMP; else ->0.            8  Looking for 04; ->0 either case.  If good, mTMP -> mHIT,        AWAKEN MDCS, set mTO.                                                                                                   Upon success,  mHIT  is  0bbb kkkk .  The MDC rep rate with no     stream output is at about 10 Hz.  The timeout is nominally      set for 200 mS to allow for one retransmit if needed.                                                                        This mechanism exercises MDC control on a CT.                                                                                   cma  returns arguments for  !CMD  given increase flag.  Exits      caller if the button has no control assignment.                                                                              fbk  returns feedback state for the most recent button.                                                                         +BUT  handles a leading edge;                                   ~BUT  handles a continuation of the same button, exercised at      whatever rep rate we get back to ?BUT while the button is       still being held down.                                       -BUT  handles trailing edge.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block supports basic line level operations for the CT.  All   work is done on behalf of tasks that must acquire the line      before conducting an EXCHANGE.                                                                                               SELECT  acquires the given line for the current RT, returning      with A/B and 'NT set in the user area and with initial Rx       timeout and RTID set in Ntbls for the line.  Selects the old    line if same was disabled while we weren't looking.                                                                          EXCHANGE  starts a network exchange operation on the current and   already SELECTed channel.  Arguments are transmit buffer de-    scriptor, function byte, and input buffer descriptor.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        ~del  is pretty tricky.  It checks for LED status changes & puts   them up.  In addition, if alarm state of RT has changed, it     schedules  ~alm  to be executed on the way out of ?good .       This gives us a bit of a problem managing the exit point of     ?good himself.  Called with RT# in W, new R.COND in A, and      3 led change bits in B.                                                                                                      >OK  is used after  ?GOOD  for normal data polls to maintain       the  R.OK  flag that's used to tell the slave to reset its      control failsafe timer next time around.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     ?GOOD  is used after any EXCHANGE to check the results of the      operation.  It drags all relevant info out of the Ntbls so      that it may RELEASE the line before returning.  If status is    good, returns true atop inbound data length and condition of    THIS operation.  Otherwise returns false atop status.  Signal   quality shift register for this line/channel combination is     maintained.  Slave's latest condition is set only if result     was good AND as of time of completion, our line is ACTIVE for   the current slave.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           SLO  places current RT on the slow poll list.                   QUALS  returns line quality shift regs for inactive and active     lines on the current RT.  Nonexistent lines return zero.                                                                     Flip  alternates activity between "A" and "B" channels.                                                                         YUK  sets communications status alarm for the current RT.       ?FLIP  deals with persistent errors.  If active line has 8 con-    secutive errors we flip if backup has had any success or put    the RT on slow poll.  If active line has two consecutive errs   and backup has three consecutive successes flip.  Otherwise,    nothing is changed.                                                                                                          ?-FLIP  considers returning to normal operation.  If secondary     is currently active and primary looks solid (4 consecutive      good polls), we summarily switch back to the primary line.   ?COOL  is true if the main data buffer just received is consist-   ent with the current slave's active menu on our side.  Rules:   R.AMEN is updated on ALL polls that get this far.  We are not   cool if R.UGH has -trust or cop flags set, or if received s/n   has its 64-bit set.  We are also not cool if received s/n is    different from R.DMEN, in which case we set all bad-bits in     R.UGH and stimulate COP through FINK.  Validity bit in R.UGH    is properly maintained in all cases.                                                                                         Main data buffer is presumed to lie at  HERE .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  -ok  turns off the "ok" bits for all menu variables depending on   the current RT.  In addition turns off "ok" for front panel     if it is looking at the current RT.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          >data  disseminates data from current basic poll to all menu       slots, including front panel, receiving data from this RT.                                                                   Would actually be a good idea to SELECTIVELY stimulate the         data sinks here rather than kicking all in the tail just        because we got a dry poll from some RT!                                                                                      Main data buffer is presumed to lie at  HERE .                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  dFP  returns the desired front panel channel descriptor includ-    ing validity flag as of the current moment.  Returns zero if    front panel not looking at the current RT.  Front panel must    set FPD and then clear fDATA whenever its context changes.                                                                   &fs  attaches failsafe reset flag to the given front panel desc-   riptor, composing a normal data poll message at HERE and re-    turning arguments for EXCHANGE.  Preserves fpan descriptor so   we may check it for change before storing fp data.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           ?LIVE  conducts the basic are-you-there poll.  It may be used on   either link for an RT, normally used only by slow poller.       Poll failures consider flipping to the other line.  In cases    of success where RT is on slow poll and has at least one line   we take the RT off slow poll and request new menu.  Addition-   ally we consider restoring to "A"-line if using B and A seems   healthy.                                                                                                                     ?DATA  conducts a normal data poll for the current RT using the    indicated line.  Results are posted, quality is maintained,     control failsafe is retriggered if appropriate, and state       changes for line and slow-poll entry are noted.  Additionally   we call the cops if the RT's menu serial smells bad.  Data      are received at  HERE .                                                                                                                                                                      %MSD  composes MSD submenu for this RT, returning almost all of    the arguments required for EXCHANGE.                         %CRT  does the same for CRT n's submenu.  CRT 3 marked as last.                                                                 !RQST  transmits an async request, returning "true" if good.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    async  handles a complete asynchronous request/response cycle.     Args are output buffer, complete func code, input buffer lng    (received at HERE).  Retries the request up to four times and   retries response up to 40.  Aborts if the RT is found on slow   poll.  Also aborts if request flag handshakes fail to cycle     properly or if response length is incorrect.  Returns "true"    if the cycle completed normally.  NOTE: DOES NOT maintain the   async facility!  This is done by  <l  and  l> .                                                                              If called with negative length, the test for exact match is not    made.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        Contingencies:  1) error on request or answers.                    2) request busy on entry.  3) goes on slow poll while we        wait for answers.  4) Answers simply not available for a        long while.  5) Both busy & answer bits turn off while wait-    ing for answers.  In each of these cases we give up and ter-    minate prematurely.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          The required settings for the modem are:                           Switches  udddu duduu  DTR, terse results, no echo, autoans,       DCD on, RJ11, commands, 212A, DTR hangup.  S8=3 sec/comma,      S0=1 ring, S2=no +++.  S0 overrides auto ans switch.      Ctl  returns address of this task's control/status register.    typ  is a high level  TYPE  for poller tasks.                                                                                   ?K  is a flagged  ?KEY .                                        Dry  flushes incoming registers.                                KI  returns a key, with the given timeout in 2-sec units.       SNK  sends a carriage return, expecting the given character in     the given time followed by <CR>.  True if QED.                                                                               -MODEM  deasserts DTR.  +MODEM  reasserts it.                   HANGUP  gets the phone hung up and the modem initialized.  Arg     is ASCII zero to disable incoming, 1 to enable same.         <Du , >Du , and -Du  are search, search/insert, and delete for     the currently selected RT+A/B combination.                   @No  returns dial string from the current data base item.                                                                       +DU  addresses R.DU byte for current selection.                 +AB  addresses R.A or R.B for current selection.                'WIRE  addresses  Ntbls  for this task.                                                                                         ?mor  aborts with an immediate check for more calls to make.    -fail  if arg false, deals with failure to establish a link.                                                                    <id  obtains site ID from RT, which is alternated continuously     with its one's complement during this phase of connection.   id>  sends security code as two bytes; pos hi 2 digits, neg low.   When/if the RT buys this, it sends us continuous 80's until     we send it at least one "FF".  It then enters normal mode.   ?POLL  returns the A/B flag and executes the following word iff    the I'th RT is not on slow poll AND if its currently active     channel is the one this TH task services.  TOS flag is incr-    emented whenever this occurs.  Otherwise we skip the follow-    ing word.  Supports Dialup identifier.                                                                                       ~line  given or/and arguments, modifies R.A/B and ensures that     we cover all bases implied by any change to these.           sever  breaks connection for ABNORMAL reasons.                                                                                  poll  runs normal data polling on this channel.  We punch out if   our own quality goes to zero, our RT goes on slow poll, time    window closes, alarm time rules closed (iff ATT=3), or we get   an explicit hang-up request from some other task.  Note that    explicit hangup requests have little effect on an RT whose      time window is currently open!                               DIAL  entered with RT#, FR#, A/B set for the site to dial.                                                                      ?ASKED  if SCR is positive, someone has requested dialing that     RT using A/B in R#.  The requester does this only when our      ATT is zero; he must have set +DU, preset FR#, and validated    the thing as being dialable.  SCR=-2 => hangup request.                                                                      : ?INCOMING   if any char sits in the UART, assume that the        modem has answered the phone.  Set busy and go to ID proto      with unknown RT number. ;                                                                                                    #CHAN  gets actual starting minute followed by connection dur-     ation in minutes.  #FLD  is  2fre  plus how long to wait for    the current alarm timeout criterion.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         Each line has a task for normal data polling and dialup manage-    ment.  The task is either waiting to be called, dialing, or     running.                                                                                                                     POLL  fires up a poller task to search for RT's to poll for data   If one scan of the RT address space fails to turn up one that   we should be polling, this task is put to sleep until KICKed.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                The slow poller cycles thru all RT's repetitively.  Inactive       lines (backups) are polled if they exist.  RT's on the slow     poll list are polled for any defined links.  ANY success        whatsoever on a slow-polled RT attempts to wake it up com-      pletely.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     *LINES  must be executed immediately after ANY change to the       current RT's line assignments or parameters.  Ensures that      the active line exists, favoring "A" side, and that slow poll   is properly entered or left depending on the circumstances.                                                                  +DM  advances desired menu number for the given RT.  Used only     as part of larger change strategies.                                                                                         +ALL  obsoletes menus for all RT's.  Used to force rebroadcast     of updated date/time to all.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 TYRANT  exists to drop an RT into "command mode" where polling     is restricted to a tight interaction suitable for making adj-   ustments.  It may only be used by one boss at a time and only   on one channel.  TYRANT must be stimulated repeatedly or it     times out, releasing the slave to normal operations.         BOSS  facility, serializes task use of TYRANT.  Second cell has    address of AWAKEN cell for using task or zero.               cSTIM  stored by using task, contains  +0 arhh byte, +1 rest of    channel, +2 pulse mode flag.  Store indivisibly wrt PAUSE.   cRSP  for using task, contains 7 bytes of data from the RT.                                                                     !CTL  tries to set the given command mode on current RT; true if   we were able to get things started.  SET FOR NOMINAL 20-SEC     TIMEOUT.                                                     C!CTL  used by CRT's to allow lockout.                          !1st  sets c1st flag if we DON'T currently own line to use.     schlep  refreshes cRSP from what we just got.  If the exchange     failed, we zero the "A" in front and turn of data  ok  flag.    Otherwise the response is moved and the channel being moni-     tored is unpacked suitably for use with  V@ .                                                                                ~ok  sets question marks on all displayed data after the first     exchange following initial acquisition of a line for control    purposes.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    <CTL  "takes control" of the given channel on the current RT.      If successful, this means that the TYRANT task was available,   we got it, and TYRANT has established the high speed connect-   ion we desire with the given channel.  THIS SHOULD ALWAYS BE    DONE BEFORE ISSUING A COMMAND OR -MOM.                       -tmot  is true if control is active and has not timed out.  For    command and status channels, the timeout interval is pruned     to 4~6 seconds if it was longer.                                                                                             TYRANT  is turned off by  CTL>  or by timeout of  n  seconds       with no explicit control actions.  Momentaries merit further    attention in this regard.                                                                                                    This business is courteous about line problems.  If we get onto    the slow poll list, we release the line we are having trouble   with so that the slow poller will not get hung up.           Normal case:                                                      Boards 0 is cool                                                Switches match board 0 record of number                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       NUKE  zeros an entire EAROM board.                                                                                              !CML  initializes default comm line settings.  Txinit 3; txrst 2   Rxtot 83 decimal; Lead 3, trail 2.  Appropriate for 1200 baud   half duplex 2-wire fully switched.  This should be an unob-     trusive setting for a fresh box coming up on an installed net                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                &L  performs the integrity check on all RT's, placing those with   no hope of communicating on the slow poll list, including any   that are configured for dialup operation assuming ~DU done.                                                                  &RT  initializes the RT data base statically without any checks    on RT configuration.                                                                                                         ~DU  ensures that R.A and R.B are consistent with the current      dialup vs non- settings of our 8 ports.  For any normal ass-    ignment we generate 8x if the port is now a dialup.  For any    dialup port written to EAROM while active (4x) we generate      the Cx form (inactive dialup).  As originally designed this     procedure is NOT intended to support switching to/from dialup   on a whole channel dynamically.                              |du|  performs this sort of checking on a given R.A/R.B value.                                                                  ?DBOK  checks for invalid data base and asks operator for per-     mission to reinitialize it if so.  Returns if and only if we    seem to have a solid EAROM subsystem.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        VALIDATE  checks our configuration to determine whether it's ok    and safe to fire ourselves up.  Chk  sets the  ?ex  flag if     we appear to have a 488 interface for expansion boxes.  It      returns "true" if we do not seem to have a file memory con-     troller board, in which case we refuse to run.  Otherwise, we   acquire the site number from switches 2 and 1.  IF THE SITE     IS 99 AND Switch 3 is 0 (normally it's nonzero) we zero the     anchor file and halt, waiting for switches back to normal and   reset.  Otherwise, we validate the data base for self consis-   tency and insist on initializing it before we proceed.                                                                       The  099  switch boot is REQUIRED if any chips have been changed   anywhere in the file system and is a good idea but not req'd    if boards have been changed.  Deleting boards requires init.    Adding boards can be handled under normal system control.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This little bit of nonsense is necessary because we can't get      away with a PROM'd interrupt routine for subvectoring since     missing boards look like they are requesting.                                                                                vecs  builds a subvectoring ISR given:  PROM interrupt routine;    first UART's device address; address of a table of pointers;    number of UART's possible (spaced 4 apart); and the RAM addr    to which the interrupt initially vectors (must allocate 11      bytes per possible UART plus 1 for RTI).  For each UART we      find existing, a sequence is generated that polls the part      and branches to the PROM routine with status in A and corres-   ponding pointer table entry in W if that UART is requesting     an interrupt at the moment.                                                                                                  +net  constructs the ISR for network interfaces to RT's.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        +PIA  takes the address of a 5-byte table:  2-byte port (a/b)      adr, data, control, and direction.  Control MUST have data      access bit set.  Sets desired control mode, then data (which    we now think works), then selects and sets control, and at      the same time reselects data.  Finally reads input port to      turn off IRQ bits and lines, returning advanced table address                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                PEON  does the actual work of servicing requests from the main     RT box as given us over the 488 link.                        +PEON  starts this process.  In the working system, once this      has been initiated the watchdog is active.                                                                                   uart  is the interrupt routine for operator debugging terminal     I/O.  This is optional and is not used in the working system.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                /initialize  initializes hardware.  UART's in groups 2 and 3       are forced into reset, necessary because they lack hardware     reset inputs.  The umbilical is initialized.  Restart vector    is set for running test systems from RAM.  Interrupts are       enabled fully.                                               /INITIALIZE  performs all required hardware and application data   base initialization required on power up or reset.                                                                           Interrupts are normally indirectly vectored thru hi RAM.  Some     have permanent hardware assignments:                             -2 Reset                 -4 NMI Unused                          -6 SWI Unused            -8 IRQ Unused                          -A FIRQ,INT7h 50/60 Hz   -C INT6                                -E INT5                 -10 INT4                               -12 INT3h All Modem II   -14 INT2 Debug terminal                -16 INT1                 -18 INT0 hFP Kbd; IEEE-488          These tables are used by  START  (next block) to initialize        RAM.  The important tables follow a dummy clock interrupt.   RAM  is the table containing initial values for the system         variables, following the map in block 75.  Note that initial    values are needed only for the first 38 bytes.               OPERATOR  contains the information necessary to initialize the     one task that is active following a boot.  The format of this   table is identical to those constructed by  TERMINAL (blk 24,   25).  It starts with STATUS , S0 , and the output and input     interrupt code.  The next 46 bytes map to the 1st 46 bytes      of the user variable area, as described in block 75.  Note      that the latter part of this table, the following table  ram    and the startup code will be overlaid by the dictionary after   it has been used at boot time.                                                                                                                                                               This power-up behavior supports RAM system testing and is not      suitable for high reliability installed system.                                                                              After initializing the hardware, we check for presence of the      debugging port.  The background application is started up       UNLESS the debugging port is present AND we have both CTS and   DCD, in which case we leave it alone so that changes can be     made before activating it.  If the debugging port is present,   the operator acts like a normal pF terminal; otherwise, the     operator is put to sleep.  Also, when the debugging port is     present on the box, we re-arm the restart routine so that a     RAM-tested PROM will retain its sticking power.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 This block controls the entire target compilation of the     system, including both standard FORTH features (block 60) and   configuration characteristics:  disk & terminal drivers, amount of memory, number of disk buffers, presence/absence of heads,   etc.  Consult the Target Compiler documentation for a discussionof specific details.                                                                                                               Note especially the  ALLOT  on line 5.  This sets the  RAM   pointer to the start of the system variables area.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              gmask  holds the current interrupt mask for the 488 chip.       OVAL  is nonzero if we've accepted good output data from master.tmp  and  It  are scratch for 488 interrupt code.               ?DOG  if nonzero enables watchdog operation.                                                                                    CMDS  receives data broadcasts from master.                     ONE and TWO  alternate as data response buffers.  Absolute max     size 594; practical limit, 12 boards is 386.  Left at abs max   size so we can force a single box to generate max traffic.                                                                   CVEC  transmits configuration to master, and                    AVEC  receives commands & config confirmations.  See below.                                                                     DTBL , ATBL , and PTBL  hold flags and retained data for digital   analog and parallel inputs.  ORDER DEPENDENT!                                                                                -SLV  reinitializes the slave 68488 chip:  Places it in reset,     sets address pair base to value in switches *2, releases the    reset, TEMPORARILY enables automatic handshake completion,      computes parallel poll response of  2**(switch set -1) ,        disables automatic command handshakes, and sets interrupt       mask (for now all are prevented).                                                                                            -GPIB  places both interfaces in passive reset state, done only    at power-up.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 SNDG  is called with interrupts prevented when an unsolicited      BO interrupt is received.  The selected address is inspected    and the proper buffer is chosen.  Transmits the buffer, then    sends checksum with EOI.  Returns with  gmask  set to ignore    further BO's (we stay this way until we hear any sort of data   or command, which uncorks our ability to talk.)  While doing    this, if we get any other sort of stimulus we simply return.    In the abnormal case, we expect to be hearing a device clear    function!                                                                                                                    ----------------------------------------------------------------   WARNING:  This is a carefully tuned algorithm on both ends.     Modify at your peril!  Any significant change in CPU clock      rates will require retuning.                                 ----------------------------------------------------------------                                                                RCVNG  is called with interrupts prevented when an unsolicited     BI interrupt is received.  Chooses the proper input buffer      based on the selected address and fills it with data.  Leaves   status in the buffer descriptor and awakens a task if we got    far enough to receive the checksum.  Buffer descriptors:           +0 Buffer Address                                               +2 Max Length +1 (buffer must have this much space)             +4 Address of Task to Awaken                                    +6 Received length.  Is Max + 1 if overrun.                     +8 Received Checksum                                            +9 Computed Checksum                                            +A Nonzero if task awakened                                     +B Nonzero if overrun or checksum error                                                                                                                                                                                                                   gpib  receives control on any unmasked GPIB interrupt.  Analyzes   the situation it finds and decides what to do about it.                                                                      Commands in:  0 (may be interrupted handshake) and 14 (DCL)        causes complete reset of our chip and return to wide open.      1F, an undefined code, is the ENCORE signal sent out after      address sequence that may request second inbound from a box.    1E is used for rapid watchdog twiddle during configuration.     This is done but everything else is left as it was.             All others are simply absorbed and ignored.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ?CFG  determines actual I/O board config present in box.  Checks   for delay control at address 0, sets flag if it seems there.    For each command output board, checks for presence & if there   initializes the 6821 passively.  OVAL if zero indicates that    no valid data exist, in which case all outputs are set OPEN     with no glitches.  Otherwise, outputs are unchanged.  In all    cases we leave the output data ports selected.  Analog boards   are then checked for both addressability and for successful     completion of a dummy conversion.  Both tests must pass.  The   next block checks parallel input boards for presence and gets   switch settings for all that we find.  Strobe in is sucked &    therefore reset whether or not it existed; then we assert the   accept signal briefly.  This should get us safely in sync on    powerup, although it will pitch a sample when we read config    during operation.                                                                                                            Config vector (and message, and response):                        +0  Delay control flag.  00=absent; FF=present & working.       +1  Command out flags 0-15.                                    +17  Status in flags 0-15.                                      +33  Analog in flags 0-15.                                      +49  Parallel in triplets 0-15:  Flags & sws; sws in BCD, with       100/S2,10/S3,1/S4,config/S5.                              -----                                                            +97  Status in flags to use (from master).                     +113  Analog in flags to use.                                   +129  Parallel in table to use.                                 (177)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           SLAM  sets our active input board management tables to reflect     the configuration that we received from the master as opposed   to what we actually observed.                                                                                                ENUM  examines our active management tables, setting the total     length of the inbound data message that we will send to the     master.                                                                                                                      These two operations are performed when we receive our working,    active configuration back from the master.  Subsequently we     can begin reporting data in earnest.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         @PAR  reads any parallel input boards for which this box is con-   figured.  If no strobe has been received, the last value from   table 2+ is reported.  Otherwise new data are read and con-     verted based on S5 value in table 1+:  0-7=BCD; 8=sign/mag;     9=2's complement.  The new value is saved in table 2+ and is    reported.  Additionally, table 4+ is marked to indicate that    we trust the data (at least within the current box).  Any new   data are acknowledged with a short (21 uS) pulse.  If this is   too short we will have to go to some fairly major trouble to    lengthen it; the inherent handshake in the chip seems to be     useless as documented in the Moseley manual, damn it.  Timing   nada=475u; all 2's 1534u; all s/m 1791u; all bcd 3150u.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      aset  is called as a subroutine with aligned MUX select in high    four bits of  B .  Outer level selection CS4, via CB2 output,   must have been previously set.  Selects multiplexors on all     boards; takes 79 uS, adequate for settling, so we then strobe   start-convert on all channels.  This also takes long enough     that the first few channels have already finished conversions   by the time we can possibly read the results.  Used by both     normal conversions and ground reference sampling.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            @ANA  samples all normal analog inputs for which this box is con   figured, converts them to 2's complement 12-bit numbers, de-    biases them using ground references, and saves the results in   the message frame with major grouping by board.  Takes from     11816 to 19533 uS, incremental ~480 uS/active board.            Saturated values are returned as 7Fxx.  Sign of xx indicates    the sign of the saturated value.                                                                                             @DIG  acquires digital ins from all boards for which we are con-   figured, leaving the results in the message given.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           amain  selects normal (34) or cal (3C) major muxes on all A-D      boards possible.  During normal operations, normal inputs are   assumed to be selected.                                                                                                      @GND  reads all A-D ground references and stores them into ATBL    for debiasing of future inputs.                                                                                              @REF  reads reference voltages and saves them in ATBL where the    ground values normally lie.  Arg is an aligned MUX selection    value to be used with great caution.  We do not do this in      normal operation because they are all scaled to and therefore   track the A-D reference, and because the dividers that pro-     duce them have much less accuracy than does the A-D itself.                                                                                                                                                                                                  !CMDS  distributes the full set of command outputs and one card    worth of delay control from the master's channel zero output    message.  Assumes that the command output boards have been      set up properly.  Could be 112 uS faster with no loop.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       gdat  handles a good message from channel 0.  The watchdog is      retriggered and we capture serial from outbound into alt buf.   Commands are set, OVAL is set, and valid relay goes on.  Then   we look at the serial and if it's odd we take no data.  Other   wise, if this is a 0 mod 64 transmission we measure grounds,    and at any rate we take data based on our active config (at     boot this is NOTHING).  Finally, we copy error count into the   alt buffer and swap buffers.  Timing for out only 612u; for     normal di 13200-24100u; di+gnd 14316-25166.                                                                                  bdat  handles a bad channel 0 message (length or cksum error).     Simply counts the error and updates the error count in the      previous chan 0 response buffer.  Too many of these in succ-    ession and the watchdog times out.  Valid relay, as well as     all outputs, stay in prior states.  Also used for bad control                                                                gctl  handles a control message not obviously in error.  Code 2    causes us to reboot.  Code 1 forces our response to look like   we have rebooted and we reassess our configuration.  Code 0     acknowledges our reboot or reconfiguration.  We first set our   response to look like a reboot and check message length for     accuracy.  Then, if & only if the master's echo of our physi-   cal configuration matches what we have been sending, we adopt   whatever the master told us to act like, take a complete        input sample, swap buffers, and then set the correct response   length.  Whenever the master forces us through this sort of     nonsense it should disregard one or perhaps two more reboot     type responses from us since this will take a while and may     be asynchronous with normal data acquisition.                                                                                                                                                                                                                RSP  examines our stimulus sources for any reason to be awake.     If none is found, hangs in a STOP until one shows up.  Other-   wise, selects one of the preceding definitions to execute and   returns its address.  U3 A4 pin 6 is high whenever task code    is active.  This may be used for system wide duty cycle         monitoring.                                                                                                                  MAIN  is the outer loop for the functional task of any expansion   box.  We simply wait until something has been sent us on one    of the two 488 channels, select a reaction, and perform it.                                                                  The only other task functioning in an expansion box is the oper-   ator debugging port.  This is not actually OPERATOR and isn't   even built unless it can be used (serial port present).                                                                                                                                      di  and  ei  provide high level access to interrupt control.                                                                    RELOAD  asserts the RESET line.  All hardware is placed in the     reset state and we execute normal system powerup behaviour.                                                                  >< swaps the bytes in the top stack item.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       HIS  takes a task address and user variable and returns the        address of that user variable for the task.                     Ex.:  TYPIST CTR HIS  (returns the addr. of  TYPIST's  CTR ) BACKGROUND  sets up the task definition table for a non-terminal   task, given user area size and stack sizes.  Table is:             +0  User area address   +2  Size of same, bytes                 +4  S0                  +6  HERE                          BUILD  takes the address of a table set up by  BACKGROUND  and     builds the task's user area in RAM.                          ACTIVATE  starts a task whose address is given executing the       remainder of the colon definition in which  ACTIVATE  occurs.   Note that this code must end in  STOP ,  QUIT (for a terminal   task) or in an infinite loop.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                +PIA  takes the address of a 5-byte table:  2-byte port (a/b)      adr, data, control, and direction.  Control MUST have data      access bit set.  Sets desired control mode, then data (which    we now think works), then selects and sets control, and at      the same time reselects data.  Finally reads input port to      turn off IRQ bits and lines, returning advanced table address                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                PEON  does the actual work of servicing requests from the main     RT box as given us over the 488 link.                        GO  starts this process.  In the working system, once this         has been initiated the watchdog is active.                   DOG  actually activates the watchdog.  It's factored separately    so that with debugging terminal installed we don't have to      enable it if we don't wish to.                                                                                               uart  is the interrupt routine for operator debugging terminal     I/O.  This is optional and is not used in the working system.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                /initialize  initializes hardware.  UART's in groups 2 and 3       are forced into reset, necessary because they lack hardware     reset inputs.  The umbilical is initialized.  Restart vector    is set for running test systems from RAM.  Interrupts are       enabled fully.                                               /INITIALIZE  performs all required hardware and application data   base initialization required on power up or reset.                                                                           Interrupts are normally indirectly vectored thru hi RAM.  Some     have permanent hardware assignments:                             -2 Reset                 -4 NMI Unused                          -6 SWI Unused            -8 IRQ Unused                          -A FIRQ,INT7h 50/60 Hz   -C INT6                                -E INT5                 -10 INT4                               -12 INT3h All Modem II   -14 INT2 Debug terminal                -16 INT1                 -18 INT0 hFP Kbd; IEEE-488          These tables are used by  START  (next block) to initialize        RAM.  The important tables follow a dummy clock interrupt.   RAM  is the table containing initial values for the system         variables, following the map in block 75.  Note that initial    values are needed only for the first 38 bytes.               OPERATOR  contains the information necessary to initialize the     one task that is active following a boot.  The format of this   table is identical to those constructed by  TERMINAL (blk 24,   25).  It starts with STATUS , S0 , and the output and input     interrupt code.  The next 46 bytes map to the 1st 46 bytes      of the user variable area, as described in block 75.  Note      that the latter part of this table, the following table  ram    and the startup code will be overlaid by the dictionary after   it has been used at boot time.                                                                                                                                                               This power-up behavior supports RAM system testing and is not      suitable for high reliability installed system.                                                                              After initializing the hardware, we check for presence of the      debugging port.  The background application is started up       UNLESS the debugging port is present AND we have both CTS and   DCD, in which case we leave it alone so that changes can be     made before activating it.  If the debugging port is present,   the operator acts like a normal pF terminal; otherwise, the     operator is put to sleep.  Also, when the debugging port is     present on the box, we re-arm the restart routine so that a     RAM-tested PROM will retain its sticking power.  Watchdog is    not started up until DOG is performed, so operator MAY say      just  GO  to start normal operations without watchdog.                                                                                                                                          Here starts the notorious target compiler:                   NEW is the block where the target code will begin.              CP/M is the name of the TotalFORTH source code.                 AHEAD sizes the next word, leaving it in the input stream.      LOG is a dummy for a logging word that can be loaded from 111.  HEAD holds the target dictionary links.                         WDS stores the current and default size of a dictionary header. ?TWO flags if host definitions of target constants are made.    WO is the target address of the beginning of target code.       'H is the target  H .     VOC is the target  CONTEXT .          ORG sets  'H ,  HERE gets it and  GAP increments it.            'R is the next available location in target RAM.                THERE gets  'R  and  ALLOT increments it.                       | makes the next definition headless.                           WINDOW sets  WO  and initializes the other variables.                                                                           NAME returns the name address of the last word defined.         PREVIOUS returns its target parameter field address.            VOCAB returns its unmasked hash index in target space.          -' searches for a word in the target dictionary                                                                                 FORTH is an immediate vocabulary used with HOST                 HOST is an immediate vocabulary in which host definitions and      host versions of target definitions are placed               ASSEMBLER is the target assembler vocabulary                                                                                    TARGET moves the last definition from host's vocabulary to the     (unnamed) target vocabulary determined by  VOCAB .                                                                           TWIN constructs a host version of a target definition.          SIZE computes the header size of the next word, leaving it in      the input stream.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               This version of memory access words compiles target code intoRAM.   Block 49 compiles onto disk.                                                                                             Most words are analogous to those in block 47                                                                                   RAM is a 8192-byte array for storing the target dictionary      FLUSH is redefined to copy RAM onto disk starting at block NEW     Note that a reference to this FLUSH appears at the end of       block 61.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       This version of memory access words compiles target code ontodisk.  Block 48 compiles to RAM.                                                                                                DICTIONARY clears the target dictionary and selects the            default header size                                          >T converts a target address to a host RAM address                                                                              TC@ TC! T@ T! T+! are target versions of C@ C! @ ! and +!                                                                       C, and , are redefined to compile into the target dictionary    CMOVE is redefined to move from host to target                  DUMP is redefined to dump the target dictionary                                                                                 The old versions of C, , CMOVE and DUMP are available in the       FORTH vocabulary                                                                                                                This block is loaded for the Z80 target assembler.                                                                           EMPLACE constructs a target definition.  When executed it          will compile a reference to itself.                          (CREATE) constructs a target entry.  It includes a header if WDS   indicates and links into the vocabulary indicated by VOC                                                                     STRING constructs a string in the target dictionary             CODE creates a target CODE entry                                EQU (for equate) defines a host constant (CONSTANT will be         redefined)                                                   LABEL names the current position in the target dictionary                                                                       BEGIN and U) as well as all other assembler words are redefined    to compile into the target dictionary                        USE is also so redefined                                           The words in this block are redefined to act on the target   dictionary as their host counterparts act.                                                                                         Note that all dictionary searches are restricted to the      target (unnamed) vocabulary.                                                                                                    EXTRA IN THIS SYSTEM:  ['] definition in line 2 changes meaning    of ['] when used in a FORTH : definition.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       These words redefine the standard structure words in block   102 to use the new target run-time code.                                                                                        ( is redefined to work inside target definitions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  These words provide the compile-time code that must be       attached to the run-time code previously assembled.                Exactly the right-sized gaps were left in the dictionary and are now being filled.  Those gaps (blocks 63 and 64) must be    adjusted if changes are made here.                                                                                                 Two versions of CVARIABLE and VARIABLE are provided for      either PROM or RAM.  PROM variables point to their value from   their parameter field.  PROM variables are selected by          FORGETing the RAM version of VARIABLE (in block 60).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            This business makes it a little harder to generate vocabulary      problems in an application target.                           +UQ  enables and  -UQ  disables a check to ensure that anything    we create while targeting is unique relative to the main tgt    vocabulary into which it will be placed.  This is both better   and worse than prechecking while the stuff is being tested in   teractively:                                                 BETTER:  Headless words in PROM are not detected interactively.    In these cases, expect to find out about it when targeted.   WORSE:  No check is made for conflicts with LABELs and EQUs.       Furthermore there is no meaningful way to test for these in     EITHER environment.  On the other hand, while it's easy to      bury a LABEL it's hard to be buried by one.  Presumably the     scope of a LABEL is small so this should not be a problem.   ($  DENOTES A BLOCK LOADED ELSEWHERE!  WATCH OUT!               ($P  Denotes applic blocks whose change requires new PROM 3.    LTB  is a table of nonzero literal values;  LPT  is compilation    addresses for same.                                                                                                          >pool  files an entry in these tables for the CONSTANT whose       tic and value are given, assuming that there is room.                                                                        ?POOL  given a literal value, sees if there is a matching          CONSTANT and if so returns its compilation address.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          This utility uses the Curtis PD-5000E PROM burner at 9600 baud.    It is set up for byte organized memory!                                                                                      NEW  is the starting block of target compiler output, assumed to   be at zero in memory.                                        STS  is a buffer for reading burner status.  Its first cell is     also used to communicate 1-byte messages to the burner task.                                                                 SIZE  contains the size of the PROM in bytes.                   BIAS  contains the byte origin of the PROM in target output.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    This block implements the behavior and services that the burner    expects of its host once a burner operation has been started.   All such operations eventually end with an "E" command.                                                                      RAM  contains SIZE bytes of data representing the PROM image.   THERE  contains the current "location counter" address in  RAM .GOOD  is marked with the "good" status indications received from   the burner.                                                  BAD  is marked with any failing status received from the burner.TBL  is an execute table for command characters from the burner.:B  defines behavior for the following character in  TBL .      SERVE  listens to the burner and does whatever it asks.                                                                         The remaining definitions describe the behavior that the burner    expects of us in response to each of its defined command        characters.                                                  This block contains simple burner protocols.                    TASK  clears all status information and returns the address of     the burner task.                                             WAIT  spins until the burner task has completed an operation.   STATE  displays the status information received from the burner.SYNC  waits for the burner to complete an operation ane then       displays its status information.                                                                                             1byte  activates the burner to perform a simple 1-byte operation1BYTE  defines a simple 1-byte command protocol.  Such commands    are:  RESET (resets burner), PGM (starts burning from RAM),     VER (starts verify from RAM), and  MANUAL (manual mode).                                                                     ?sts  and  rb  activate the burner to return 3-byte status or      SIZE-byte PROM contents.                                     ?STS  and  READBACK  perform the complete protocols for these.  This block supports PROM personalities and organization.                                                                        P@  takes a PROM-relative byte address, returning the address of   the corresponding byte of target compiler output.            P!  is the corresponding store operator.                                                                                        HUNK  sets up for operations on the given PROM number, where 0     and 1 are the low and high bytes of the first chunk of the      address space, 2 and 3 of the second, and so on.  Returns       loop parameters for  P@ 'ing the entire device.                                                                              PROM  defines a PROM selection word, given device type codes       from and to the burner, and device size in bytes.  The rest     of the words in this block select the PROM types they name.                                                                                                                                  This block contains the major user-accessible function of the      utility.                                                                                                                     FETCH  fills  RAM  with what should go into the  nTH  PROM.        The values for  n  are 0 and 1 for low and high bytes of the    0th pair, 2 and 3 for the 1st pair, and so on.               STORE  does the opposite.                                                                                                       BURN  burns the  nTH  PROM.                                     ANOTHER  burns another copy of the last PROM, saving some time.                                                                 CHECK  matches the "slave" PROM against desired contents for the   nTH  PROM.                                                   SUCK  reads back the nTH PROM and stores it out into the image.                                                                                                                                 Moseley PROM burning organizations:                                                                                             CT  ID      4      0      1      2      3                           U#     U7     U8     U9    U10    U11                          ADR   6000   8000   A000   C000   E000                                                                                       RT  ID      4      0      1      2      3                           U#     U7     U8     U9    U10    U11                          ADR   6000   8000   A000   C000   E000                                                                                       EXP ID                           2      3                           U#                         U10    U11                          ADR                        C000   E000                                                                                                                                                                                                                       2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 